INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points) VALUES
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111','advanced','multiple_choice','In the English cotton count (Ne) system, a higher count number means the yarn is:','["Coarser","Finer","Heavier per unit length","Unaffected in fineness"]','Finer','Ne is an indirect (length-per-unit-weight) system, so a higher Ne means less mass per length, i.e. a finer yarn.',3),
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111','advanced','multiple_choice','Tex and Denier are both:','["Indirect count systems","Direct count systems","Twist measurement systems","Strength measurement systems"]','Direct count systems','Both express mass per unit length directly, so a higher number means a coarser yarn.',3),
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111','advanced','multiple_choice','1 Tex equals how many Denier?','["9","10","1.5","90"]','9','Denier = Tex x 9, since Denier is mass in grams per 9000 m and Tex is mass in grams per 1000 m.',3),
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111','advanced','multiple_choice','Twist multiplier (TM) is primarily used to:','["Compare twist level across different yarn counts","Measure yarn color fastness","Calculate fabric GSM","Determine loom speed"]','Compare twist level across different yarn counts','TM = TPI / sqrt(Ne), which normalizes twist so yarns of different counts can be compared fairly.',3),
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111','advanced','multiple_choice','If twist per inch increases beyond the optimum level, yarn strength will:','["Keep increasing indefinitely","Increase then decrease after a peak","Decrease immediately from zero twist","Stay constant"]','Increase then decrease after a peak','Strength rises with twist up to an optimum angle, then falls because fibers are compressed too obliquely and torque stress builds up.',3),
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111','advanced','multiple_choice','CSP (Count Strength Product) is used to express:','["Yarn evenness","Combined effect of yarn count and lea strength","Twist direction","Moisture regain"]','Combined effect of yarn count and lea strength','CSP = Ne x lea strength (lbs), giving a single figure that lets mills compare strength across different counts.',3),
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111','advanced','multiple_choice','RKM (Reissfestigkeit pro Kilometer) expresses yarn strength as:','["Breaking length in km","Twist per meter","Elongation percentage","Fiber fineness in microns"]','Breaking length in km','RKM is the length of yarn (in km) that would break under its own weight if suspended, a direct tenacity measure independent of count.',3),
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111','advanced','multiple_choice','Standard moisture regain for cotton is approximately:','["2.5%","8.5%","15%","0%"]','8.5%','Cotton''s official/standard moisture regain used for commercial weight conditioning is 8.5%.',3),
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111','advanced','multiple_choice','Yarn unevenness is most commonly quantified by:','["GSM","CV% (coefficient of variation) from Uster testing","Denier per filament","Crimp percentage"]','CV% (coefficient of variation) from Uster testing','The Uster Tester measures mass variation along yarn length and reports it as CV%, the industry-standard unevenness metric.',3),
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111','advanced','multiple_choice','A "thick place" fault in yarn is typically defined relative to mean diameter as an increase of at least:','["+15%","+35%","+100%","+5%"]','+35%','Uster classifies a thick place as a cross-section at least 35% above mean for a short length; thresholds vary slightly by standard but 35% is the common baseline.',3),
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111','advanced','multiple_choice','Yarn hairiness is measured as:','["Total protruding fiber length per unit yarn length","Number of neps per gram","Twist angle in degrees","Total moisture content"]','Total protruding fiber length per unit yarn length','Uster Hairiness Index (H) sums the length of fibers protruding beyond the yarn body per centimeter of yarn.',3),
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111','advanced','multiple_choice','Blending polyester with cotton primarily aims to improve which property versus 100% cotton?','["Moisture absorbency","Dimensional stability and strength","Biodegradability","Static generation"]','Dimensional stability and strength','Polyester adds strength, wrinkle resistance and dimensional stability that pure cotton lacks, at some cost to breathability.',3),
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111','advanced','multiple_choice','In a 65/35 polyester-cotton blend by weight, if 100 kg of yarn is produced, the polyester content is:','["35 kg","65 kg","50 kg","100 kg"]','65 kg','The first number in a blend ratio conventionally represents the dominant/first-named fiber percentage -- here polyester at 65%.',3),
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111','advanced','multiple_choice','Micronaire value primarily indicates cotton fiber:','["Length","Fineness and maturity","Color grade","Trash content"]','Fineness and maturity','Micronaire is an airflow-resistance test that reflects a combination of fiber fineness and maturity.',3),
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111','advanced','multiple_choice','An ideal Micronaire range for most spinning mills is considered to be:','["1.0-2.0","3.5-4.9","6.0-7.0","10-12"]','3.5-4.9','Values below 3.5 (immature/fine, prone to neps) and above 4.9 (coarse) are generally discounted; 3.5-4.9 is the premium mid-range.',3),
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111','advanced','multiple_choice','Combed yarn differs from carded yarn mainly because combing:','["Adds twist only","Removes short fibers and improves parallelization","Increases fiber length artificially","Adds synthetic fiber content"]','Removes short fibers and improves parallelization','The comber removes short fibers (noil) and straightens/parallelizes the remaining longer fibers, producing a finer, more even, stronger yarn.',3),
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111','advanced','multiple_choice','Noil removed during combing typically ranges from:','["1-2%","10-20%","50-60%","80-90%"]','10-20%','Typical noil extraction for combed cotton yarn is around 12-18%, depending on fiber quality and target yarn grade.',3),
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111','advanced','multiple_choice','A "double yarn" (2-ply) generally provides which advantage over an equivalent single yarn?','["Lower cost only","Higher strength and better evenness","Lower twist requirement","Reduced hairiness only"]','Higher strength and better evenness','Plying two singles together averages out weak spots and irregularities, increasing overall strength and evenness.',3),
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111','advanced','multiple_choice','Core-spun yarn is engineered by:','["Twisting two identical yarns together","Wrapping staple fiber around a filament core","Blending two fiber types before carding","Adding extra twist to a single yarn"]','Wrapping staple fiber around a filament core','A continuous filament (often spandex or polyester) forms the core while staple fiber sheaths it, combining elasticity/strength with a natural fiber hand.',3),
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111','advanced','multiple_choice','Splicing (as opposed to knotting) joins broken yarn ends by:','["Tying a mechanical knot","Using compressed air to entangle and fuse fiber ends","Gluing with adhesive","Melting synthetic fiber ends together only"]','Using compressed air to entangle and fuse fiber ends','A pneumatic splicer untwists, overlaps, and re-twists fiber ends using compressed air, producing a nearly invisible, strong join.',3),
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111','advanced','multiple_choice','Yarn package density affects downstream processing mainly by influencing:','["Color shade only","Unwinding tension consistency and dyeing liquor penetration","Fiber micronaire","Twist direction"]','Unwinding tension consistency and dyeing liquor penetration','A poorly wound (too dense or uneven) package causes unwinding tension variation and, for cheese dyeing, uneven liquor penetration.',3),
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111','advanced','multiple_choice','S-twist and Z-twist refer to:','["Yarn strength grades","The visual direction of twist in the yarn","Two different count systems","Fiber blend ratios"]','The visual direction of twist in the yarn','Held vertically, the twist direction resembles the middle of the letter S or Z; this affects fabric appearance and ply-balance.',3),
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111','advanced','multiple_choice','When plying two S-twist singles with Z-twist, the resulting ply yarn tends to be:','["Unbalanced and prone to snarling","More balanced with reduced torque/liveliness","Weaker than either single","Unaffected by ply twist direction"]','More balanced with reduced torque/liveliness','Opposing twist directions between single and ply partially cancel residual torque, producing a calmer, more balanced yarn.',3),
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111','advanced','multiple_choice','A "weak place" in yarn testing usually corresponds to a section where:','["Twist is highest","Fiber mass and/or twist is locally deficient","Moisture regain is highest","Color is lightest"]','Fiber mass and/or twist is locally deficient','Weak places typically occur where fewer fibers or lower twist exist locally in the yarn cross-section, reducing local tenacity.',3),
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111','advanced','multiple_choice','Neps in cotton yarn are best described as:','["Long straight fibers","Small entangled fiber knots","Uniform thick places","Dye contamination spots"]','Small entangled fiber knots','Neps are tiny tangled fiber clusters that show as tiny dark or light specks and disrupt yarn appearance/dyeing.',3),
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111','advanced','multiple_choice','Immature cotton fibers (low maturity ratio) tend to increase which yarn defect during spinning?','["Yarn strength","Neps","Luster","Moisture regain"]','Neps','Immature, thin-walled fibers are more prone to tangling and folding during processing, forming neps in carding/combing.',3),
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111','advanced','multiple_choice','Which count conversion is correct: Ne to Tex?','["Tex = 590.5 / Ne","Tex = Ne / 590.5","Tex = Ne x 9","Tex = 1000 / Ne"]','Tex = 590.5 / Ne','Since both are related to a fixed constant via cotton''s standard hank length, Tex = 590.5 / Ne is the standard conversion.',3),
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111','advanced','multiple_choice','A yarn labeled "40s Ne" compared to "20s Ne" is:','["Coarser","Finer","Identical fineness","Only different in color"]','Finer','Higher Ne numbers indicate finer yarn since Ne is an indirect (length-based) system.',3),
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111','advanced','multiple_choice','Sizing (slashing) applied to warp yarn before weaving primarily improves:','["Color fastness","Abrasion resistance and reduces hairiness/breakage during weaving","Moisture regain permanently","Twist multiplier"]','Abrasion resistance and reduces hairiness/breakage during weaving','Size film coats and binds surface fibers, reducing hairiness and improving abrasion resistance against heald/reed friction during weaving.',3),
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111','advanced','multiple_choice','Open-end (rotor) spun yarn compared to ring-spun yarn of similar count typically has:','["Higher strength and lower hairiness","Lower strength and higher bulk/hairiness","Identical structure and properties","No difference in production speed"]','Lower strength and higher bulk/hairiness','Rotor spinning produces a bulkier, more even but generally weaker and hairier yarn structure than ring spinning at comparable counts.',3),
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111','advanced','multiple_choice','Air-jet spinning achieves yarn cohesion mainly through:','["Mechanical twisting rollers","Wrapper fibers created by swirling compressed air","Chemical adhesive bonding","Heat fusion of fiber ends"]','Wrapper fibers created by swirling compressed air','Air-jet (vortex/MJS) spinning uses false-twist air currents to wrap outer fibers around a straight core, binding the yarn without true twist throughout.',3),
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111','advanced','multiple_choice','Yarn evenness (U%) as measured by Uster differs from CV% in that:','["U% is always exactly double CV%","U% uses a simpler averaging method historically distinct from the statistical CV% calculation","U% only applies to synthetic yarns","U% measures color, not mass"]','U% uses a simpler averaging method historically distinct from the statistical CV% calculation','U% (mean deviation) and CV% (standard-deviation based) are related but calculated differently; CV% is roughly 1.25 x U% for a normal distribution.',3),
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111','advanced','multiple_choice','Sirospun yarn is produced by:','["Twisting two separately fed rovings together at the ring spinning stage","Adding a filament core after spinning","Combing fiber twice","Using only synthetic fiber"]','Twisting two separately fed rovings together at the ring spinning stage','Sirospinning feeds two rovings into the drafting zone simultaneously, twisting them together at the spindle to mimic a two-ply yarn in one pass.',3),
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111','advanced','multiple_choice','Compact spinning primarily improves yarn quality by:','["Increasing twist multiplier only","Condensing the fiber strand before final twist insertion, reducing hairiness","Removing all short fibers","Eliminating the need for winding"]','Condensing the fiber strand before final twist insertion, reducing hairiness','A pneumatic condensing zone narrows the fiber strand just before twisting, sharply reducing hairiness and improving strength/evenness.',3),
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111','advanced','multiple_choice','Elongation at break of a yarn indicates:','["Its color fastness","How much it stretches before rupturing under tension","Its twist direction","Its fiber fineness"]','How much it stretches before rupturing under tension','Elongation% is measured on a tensile tester as the strain at the point of yarn failure, important for knitting and stretch fabrics.',3),
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111','advanced','multiple_choice','A yarn with high twist multiplier is generally preferred for:','["Soft, bulky knitwear","Sewing thread and crepe fabrics requiring strength and a firm hand","Low-strength decorative yarns only","Fabrics needing maximum softness"]','Sewing thread and crepe fabrics requiring strength and a firm hand','Higher twist increases strength and produces a firmer, sometimes crepe-like hand, suited to sewing threads and crepe fabrics.',3),
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111','advanced','multiple_choice','Staple length directly affects spinning limit because:','["Longer fibers allow finer counts to be spun with adequate strength","Shorter fibers always produce stronger yarn","Staple length has no effect on spinnability","Fiber length only affects dyeing"]','Longer fibers allow finer counts to be spun with adequate strength','Longer fibers provide more inter-fiber contact/friction per cross-section, allowing finer yarns to be spun while retaining adequate cohesive strength.',3),
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111','advanced','multiple_choice','The "spinning triangle" in ring spinning refers to:','["The triangular cross-section of the spindle","The converging zone where fibers are gathered from the drafted strand before twist is fully inserted","A defect pattern in the fabric","The shape of the traveler"]','The converging zone where fibers are gathered from the drafted strand before twist is fully inserted','At the front roller nip, fibers converge into a triangular untwisted zone before full twist locks them into yarn; edge fibers here are prone to loss, affecting hairiness/strength.',3),
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111','advanced','multiple_choice','Yarn realization (efficiency) in spinning mills is calculated as:','["Fiber input weight only","Actual yarn output weight divided by fiber input weight, expressed as a percentage","Twist per inch achieved","Number of spindles running"]','Actual yarn output weight divided by fiber input weight, expressed as a percentage','Realization% tracks how much usable yarn is produced from raw fiber input after waste extraction at each process stage.',3);
INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points) VALUES
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8','advanced','multiple_choice','The main function of the blow room in spinning is to:','["Insert final twist","Open, clean and blend raw fiber tufts before carding","Wind yarn onto cones","Comb out short fibers"]','Open, clean and blend raw fiber tufts before carding','Blow room machines progressively open compressed bale fiber into smaller tufts while removing trash and blending different bales.',3),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8','advanced','multiple_choice','The carding machine''s primary role is to:','["Add twist to sliver","Individualize fibers, remove remaining impurities, and form sliver","Wind bobbins","Insert size onto yarn"]','Individualize fibers, remove remaining impurities, and form sliver','Carding disentangles fiber tufts into individual fibers via cylinder/flat wire clothing, removing neps/trash, then condenses them into sliver.',3),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8','advanced','multiple_choice','The draw frame''s main purpose is to:','["Insert twist into roving","Blend and parallelize multiple slivers via drafting into one uniform sliver","Wind cheese packages","Perform final combing"]','Blend and parallelize multiple slivers via drafting into one uniform sliver','Drawing combines several card/comber slivers, drafts them, and doubles them to improve evenness and fiber parallelization before roving.',3),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8','advanced','multiple_choice','Doubling at the draw frame (combining multiple slivers) primarily improves:','["Fiber color","Long-term and short-term evenness by averaging out irregularities","Twist multiplier","Moisture regain"]','Long-term and short-term evenness by averaging out irregularities','Combining several slivers statistically averages out thick/thin variations present in any single sliver, improving overall uniformity.',3),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8','advanced','multiple_choice','The roving frame (simplex/speed frame) prepares sliver for ring spinning mainly by:','["Adding final fabric-ready twist","Drafting sliver further and inserting a small protective twist to form roving","Removing all trash","Dyeing the fiber"]','Drafting sliver further and inserting a small protective twist to form roving','Roving frames attenuate sliver to a manageable size and add light twist so roving holds together for handling and further drafting at ring frame.',3),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8','advanced','multiple_choice','In ring spinning, the traveler''s main function is to:','["Insert twist and wind yarn onto the bobbin as it revolves around the ring","Clean the fiber before spinning","Comb the roving","Control humidity"]','Insert twist and wind yarn onto the bobbin as it revolves around the ring','The traveler is dragged around the ring by the yarn, its lag behind the spindle speed inserting twist while simultaneously winding yarn onto the rotating bobbin.',3),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8','advanced','multiple_choice','Excessive traveler wear during ring spinning typically causes:','["Reduced yarn hairiness","Increased end breaks and yarn quality deterioration from heat/friction","Improved yarn strength","No measurable effect"]','Increased end breaks and yarn quality deterioration from heat/friction','A worn traveler generates excess friction and heat against the ring, weakening yarn locally and increasing end breakage rates.',3),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8','advanced','multiple_choice','Spindle speed in ring spinning is a major determinant of:','["Fabric color","Production rate and, combined with TPI, yarn twist level","Bale management","Card wire angle"]','Production rate and, combined with TPI, yarn twist level','Higher spindle speed increases output but must be balanced against twist requirement (front roller delivery speed) and end-breakage limits.',3),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8','advanced','multiple_choice','In rotor (open-end) spinning, fiber individualization and yarn formation occur via:','["A ring and traveler mechanism","A high-speed rotor that collects fibers in a groove and peels off twisted yarn continuously","Manual hand-drafting","A shuttle mechanism"]','A high-speed rotor that collects fibers in a groove and peels off twisted yarn continuously','Individualized fibers are fed into a spinning rotor, collected in its groove, and continuously twisted and withdrawn as yarn -- no roving stage is needed.',3),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8','advanced','multiple_choice','A key production advantage of rotor spinning over ring spinning is:','["Higher yarn strength at the same count","Much higher production speed and elimination of the roving stage","Lower hairiness always","Finer achievable counts"]','Much higher production speed and elimination of the roving stage','Rotor spinning can process card sliver directly at very high delivery speeds without a separate roving step, boosting throughput substantially.',3),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8','advanced','multiple_choice','Apron drafting systems in ring frames are used mainly to:','["Add color to fiber","Control fiber movement in the drafting zone between roller nips for improved evenness","Remove twist","Clean the roving"]','Control fiber movement in the drafting zone between roller nips for improved evenness','Aprons grip and guide fibers through the critical drafting zone, controlling floating fiber movement and reducing drafting irregularity.',3),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8','advanced','multiple_choice','A "false twist" device, as used in some spinning/texturing systems, functions by:','["Permanently locking fiber orientation with adhesive","Inserting twist that untwists again after passing a fixed point, aiding cohesion temporarily or imparting texture","Removing all twist from yarn","Adding color pigment"]','Inserting twist that untwists again after passing a fixed point, aiding cohesion temporarily or imparting texture','A false-twister imparts twist ahead of a pinning point which reverses beyond it, used both for temporary spinning cohesion and permanent texturing of filament yarn.',3),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8','advanced','multiple_choice','Total waste percentage from blow room through ring spinning for typical cotton processing is approximately:','["0.5-1%","8-14%","30-40%","60-70%"]','8-14%','Cumulative trash, short fiber, and process waste removal across blow room, carding, and combing typically totals around 8-14% for good-quality cotton.',3),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8','advanced','multiple_choice','An autoconer''s main function after ring spinning is to:','["Insert primary twist","Rewind small ring bobbins into large, faults-cleared cones for downstream use","Card the fiber","Bleach the yarn"]','Rewind small ring bobbins into large, faults-cleared cones for downstream use','Autoconers unwind multiple ring bobbins, splice ends, clear yarn faults electronically, and wind large cross-wound cones ready for weaving/knitting or dyeing.',3),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8','advanced','multiple_choice','Electronic yarn clearers on autoconers are set to detect and remove:','["Only color variations","Thick places, thin places, and long/short faults beyond set thresholds","Only moisture variation","Package winding tension only"]','Thick places, thin places, and long/short faults beyond set thresholds','Clearers continuously monitor yarn diameter/mass and cut out faults exceeding programmed limits, which are then spliced back together.',3),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8','advanced','multiple_choice','Draft in a drafting zone is calculated as:','["Delivery speed divided by feed speed (or output sliver length / input sliver length)","Feed speed divided by delivery speed","Twist per inch times count","Roller diameter divided by gauge"]','Delivery speed divided by feed speed (or output sliver length / input sliver length)','Draft = delivery roller surface speed / feed roller surface speed, representing how much the fiber strand is attenuated.',3),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8','advanced','multiple_choice','Roller gauge (distance between drafting roller nips) is set based primarily on:','["Fiber staple length","Yarn color","Twist direction","Package size"]','Fiber staple length','Gauge must be slightly greater than the fiber staple length to control fiber movement without breaking fibers or losing control.',3),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8','advanced','multiple_choice','"Lap formation" is a stage most associated with which older/combing-preparation process?','["Ring spinning","Comber lap preparation (via sliver lap or ribbon lap machines)","Winding","Sizing"]','Comber lap preparation (via sliver lap or ribbon lap machines)','Before combing, several slivers are formed into a compact, flat lap sheet on sliver lap/ribbon lap machines to feed the comber uniformly.',3),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8','advanced','multiple_choice','Combing efficiency is often evaluated by monitoring:','["Fabric GSM","Noil percentage extracted versus fiber quality improvement achieved","Loom RPM","Dyeing shade depth"]','Noil percentage extracted versus fiber quality improvement achieved','Mills balance the cost of noil (short fiber waste removed) against the resulting gains in yarn fineness, strength, and evenness.',3),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8','advanced','multiple_choice','A major limitation of rotor spinning versus ring spinning is:','["Lower production speed","Somewhat lower yarn strength and coarser minimum spinnable count","Requirement of a roving stage","Inability to run cotton fiber"]','Somewhat lower yarn strength and coarser minimum spinnable count','Rotor yarn structure (wrapper fibers, less parallel core) gives lower tenacity than ring yarn and cannot practically spin very fine counts.',3),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8','advanced','multiple_choice','Vortex (air-jet) spinning is best suited for:','["Very coarse counts only, using wool","Medium-fine counts of cotton/polyester blends at very high speed","Wet spinning of viscose filament","Hand-spinning demonstrations"]','Medium-fine counts of cotton/polyester blends at very high speed','Vortex spinning runs at very high delivery speeds and suits medium-fine synthetic/cotton blend yarns, though it needs fairly clean, well-prepared fiber.',3),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8','advanced','multiple_choice','Humidity control in a spinning mill is critical mainly because:','["It changes yarn color","Fiber moisture content affects static, fiber cohesion, and processability","It affects machine RPM directly","It changes twist direction"]','Fiber moisture content affects static, fiber cohesion, and processability','Cotton and many synthetics are hygroscopic; too-dry conditions increase static and fiber breakage, while too-humid conditions cause sticking and lapping.',3),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8','advanced','multiple_choice','End breakage rate in ring spinning is typically expressed as:','["Breaks per 1000 spindle-hours","Breaks per meter of fabric","Percentage of bales used","Twist multiplier value"]','Breaks per 1000 spindle-hours','Mills track end-down rate as breaks per 1000 spindle-hours (or per 100 spindle-hours) as a key productivity/quality KPI.',3),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8','advanced','multiple_choice','A "double roving" fault at the ring frame occurs when:','["Two rovings accidentally feed into one spinning position","Twist is doubled intentionally","The bobbin winds two layers simultaneously","Two travelers are fitted on one ring"]','Two rovings accidentally feed into one spinning position','This creping/feeding error produces an abnormally thick, faulty yarn section and must be caught by downstream clearers.',3),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8','advanced','multiple_choice','Ring frame production is inversely related to yarn count because:','["Finer yarn requires higher twist and lower delivery speed for a given spindle speed, reducing mass output","Finer yarn always uses slower spindles by design","Coarser yarn needs no twist","Count has no bearing on production rate"]','Finer yarn requires higher twist and lower delivery speed for a given spindle speed, reducing mass output','Since finer counts need proportionally more twist per unit length, front roller delivery speed (and therefore mass throughput) must be reduced at a given spindle RPM.',3),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8','advanced','multiple_choice','Balloon control rings in ring spinning are used to:','["Limit the size of the yarn balloon formed during winding, reducing tension variation and breaks","Add twist to the yarn","Clean fiber before spinning","Control humidity"]','Limit the size of the yarn balloon formed during winding, reducing tension variation and breaks','As bobbin build height changes, balloon shape varies; anti-balloon rings constrain this to stabilize tension and reduce end breaks at high speed.',3),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8','advanced','multiple_choice','Ring diameter selection in a spinning frame is chosen based on a trade-off between:','["Yarn color and moisture","Production speed potential and balloon/tension control at high spindle speeds","Twist direction only","Fiber blend ratio"]','Production speed potential and balloon/tension control at high spindle speeds','Smaller rings allow higher speeds with better balloon control but limit bobbin package size; larger rings hold more yarn but constrain top speed.',3),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8','advanced','multiple_choice','Sliver "irregularity" measured by evenness testers at the card/draw frame stage primarily predicts:','["Final fabric color","Downstream yarn evenness, since irregularities compound through subsequent processes","Loom efficiency directly","Dye uptake rate"]','Downstream yarn evenness, since irregularities compound through subsequent processes','Poor sliver evenness propagates and can even amplify through roving and spinning, so early-stage CV% monitoring is a key quality control point.',3),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8','advanced','multiple_choice','A "periodic fault" detected by spectrogram analysis in yarn testing usually points to:','["Random fiber variation","A mechanically defective roller or gear causing a repeating irregularity at a fixed wavelength","Humidity fluctuation","Dye batch variation"]','A mechanically defective roller or gear causing a repeating irregularity at a fixed wavelength','Spectrogram peaks at specific wavelengths correspond to eccentric or damaged rollers/gears in drafting systems, which repeat that fault at regular intervals.',3),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8','advanced','multiple_choice','Roller lapping (fiber wrapping around a drafting roller) is typically caused by:','["Excessive humidity, static, or roller surface damage","Too little twist in the final yarn","Overly clean fiber","High yarn strength"]','Excessive humidity, static, or roller surface damage','High humidity/static or a damaged/dirty roller surface increases fiber-to-roller adhesion, causing fiber to wrap around the roller instead of releasing.',3),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8','advanced','multiple_choice','Break draft in a ring frame drafting zone specifically refers to:','["The draft applied in the rear zone before the main drafting zone","Total draft across the whole machine","Twist inserted after drafting","The final delivery speed"]','The draft applied in the rear zone before the main drafting zone','Break draft pre-attenuates and evens the roving in the back zone, easing the load on the more critical main (front) drafting zone.',3),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8','advanced','multiple_choice','Twist contraction refers to the phenomenon where:','["Yarn diameter increases with twist","Inserted twist shortens the effective length of the fiber strand, requiring compensation in delivery speed calculations","Twist has no effect on yarn length","Fiber color darkens with twist"]','Inserted twist shortens the effective length of the fiber strand, requiring compensation in delivery speed calculations','As twist is inserted, fibers spiral rather than lie straight, slightly shortening the yarn''s linear length relative to the untwisted strand -- a factor considered in speed frame/ring frame calculations.',3),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8','advanced','multiple_choice','Which spinning preparation sequence is correct for conventional ring-spun cotton yarn?','["Blow room -> Carding -> Drawing -> Roving -> Ring spinning","Ring spinning -> Carding -> Drawing -> Blow room","Drawing -> Blow room -> Ring spinning -> Carding","Roving -> Blow room -> Carding -> Ring spinning"]','Blow room -> Carding -> Drawing -> Roving -> Ring spinning','Fiber opening/cleaning precedes individualization (carding), then evening (drawing), then further attenuation with light twist (roving), then final spinning.',3),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8','advanced','multiple_choice','Comber noil percentage is typically set higher when the target is:','["A coarse, low-cost yarn","A very fine, high-quality combed yarn requiring maximum short-fiber removal","A rotor-spun yarn","A dyed yarn"]','A very fine, high-quality combed yarn requiring maximum short-fiber removal','Higher noil extraction removes more short fiber, improving fineness/evenness potential -- justified economically only for premium fine-count yarns.',3),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8','advanced','multiple_choice','A spinning mill''s "count strength product" target is primarily used by which department to judge output quality?','["Merchandising only","Quality assurance/spinning technical department","Dyeing department exclusively","HR department"]','Quality assurance/spinning technical department','CSP is a core spinning QA benchmark used to verify that yarn strength is adequate and consistent for the spun count.',3),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8','advanced','multiple_choice','Uster Classimat fault classification groups yarn faults mainly by:','["Color and shade","Length and cross-sectional size (thick/thin, short/long faults)","Moisture content","Twist direction"]','Length and cross-sectional size (thick/thin, short/long faults)','Classimat categorizes faults into a grid based on how much thicker/thinner than normal and how long the fault segment is, guiding clearer settings.',3),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8','advanced','multiple_choice','Cot and apron hardness in drafting rollers is chosen based mainly on:','["Yarn color requirements","Fiber type and desired grip/control balance in the drafting zone","Package winding speed only","Humidity of the storage room"]','Fiber type and desired grip/control balance in the drafting zone','Softer cots grip fiber more firmly (useful for slippery/synthetic fiber) while harder cots suit natural fiber and reduce lapping tendency; selection balances control against wear.',3),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8','advanced','multiple_choice','Ring frame spindle speeds in modern cotton mills typically reach approximately:','["500-1000 rpm","2000-3000 rpm","18,000-25,000 rpm","100,000 rpm"]','18,000-25,000 rpm','Contemporary ring spindles commonly run in the 18,000-25,000 rpm range, balancing production rate against end-breakage limits.',3),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8','advanced','multiple_choice','A comber''s "feed per nip" setting primarily controls:','["Fabric color","How much fiber is presented per combing cycle, affecting noil% and combing quality","Twist multiplier of the final yarn","Loom shedding pattern"]','How much fiber is presented per combing cycle, affecting noil% and combing quality','Feed length per nip determines how much lap is advanced each cycle; shorter feeds generally improve combing quality at the cost of production rate.',3);
INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points) VALUES
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222','advanced','multiple_choice','Fabric GSM (grams per square meter) is best determined directly by:','["Weighing a precisely cut sample of known area (e.g. GSM cutter disc) and scaling to 1 m2","Counting yarns per inch only","Measuring fabric width only","Checking dye shade depth"]','Weighing a precisely cut sample of known area (e.g. GSM cutter disc) and scaling to 1 m2','A GSM round cutter takes a fixed-area sample (commonly 100 cm2); its weight is scaled up (x100 for 100 cm2) to give grams per square meter.',3),
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222','advanced','multiple_choice','Cover factor in woven fabric expresses:','["Fabric color depth","How much of the fabric area is covered by yarn versus open space","Yarn twist level","Fabric shrinkage percentage"]','How much of the fabric area is covered by yarn versus open space','Cover factor relates yarn count and thread density to the proportion of ground area actually covered by yarn, influencing openness/density.',3),
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222','advanced','multiple_choice','Warp crimp in a woven fabric is caused by:','["The warp yarn undulating over and under weft yarns during interlacing","Excess twist in weft yarn only","Finishing chemicals","Dye penetration"]','The warp yarn undulating over and under weft yarns during interlacing','As warp interlaces with weft, it bends up and down, making the yarn''s actual path longer than the fabric length -- this extra path is crimp.',3),
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222','advanced','multiple_choice','If a fabric has high thread density (EPI/PPI) with a tight weave, cover factor is:','["Low","High","Zero","Unaffected"]','High','Denser, tighter yarn packing per unit area directly increases cover factor, approaching maximum fabric coverage.',3),
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222','advanced','multiple_choice','A fabric described as "60x60, 40s x 40s" conventionally specifies:','["Fabric width in cm and GSM","EPI x PPI (thread density) and warp x weft yarn count","Loom speed and shuttle count","Fabric color code and shade"]','EPI x PPI (thread density) and warp x weft yarn count','This standard fabric specification notation states threads-per-inch in warp and weft, followed by the yarn counts used in each direction.',3),
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222','advanced','multiple_choice','Nonwoven fabric differs fundamentally from woven/knitted fabric because it is formed by:','["Interlacing yarns at 90 degrees","Bonding or interlocking fibers directly (mechanically, thermally, or chemically) without weaving/knitting yarn","Looping yarns together","Twisting fiber into yarn first, then weaving"]','Bonding or interlocking fibers directly (mechanically, thermally, or chemically) without weaving/knitting yarn','Nonwovens skip the yarn-formation step, bonding a fiber web directly via needle-punching, thermal bonding, or chemical/resin bonding.',3),
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222','advanced','multiple_choice','Fabric tear strength is best evaluated using which standard test method?','["Martindale abrasion test","Elmendorf (or wing-rip/trapezoid) tear test","Crockmeter test","AATCC colorfastness to light"]','Elmendorf (or wing-rip/trapezoid) tear test','Tear strength (resistance to a starting tear propagating) is specifically measured by the Elmendorf pendulum or trapezoid/wing-rip methods, distinct from tensile/bursting strength.',3),
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222','advanced','multiple_choice','Martindale abrasion testing primarily evaluates:','["Fabric color fastness to washing","Fabric surface durability against rubbing wear, expressed in cycles to failure/pilling","Fabric tensile strength","Fabric shrinkage after washing"]','Fabric surface durability against rubbing wear, expressed in cycles to failure/pilling','Martindale rubs a fabric sample against an abradant in a Lissajous pattern, counting cycles until visible wear, holes, or specified pilling grade appears.',3),
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222','advanced','multiple_choice','Bursting strength testing (e.g. Mullen or hydraulic burst) is most relevant for:','["Woven shirting fabric tensile strength","Knitted and nonwoven fabrics where directional tensile tests are less meaningful","Yarn twist measurement","Color matching"]','Knitted and nonwoven fabrics where directional tensile tests are less meaningful','Because knits/nonwovens can distort or run under uniaxial tension, a multi-directional bursting test better represents their real strength behavior.',3),
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222','advanced','multiple_choice','Pilling in fabric occurs when:','["Yarns shrink permanently","Loose fibers on the surface abrade, tangle into small balls, and remain attached by stronger fibers","Dye migrates to the surface","Fabric is over-starched"]','Loose fibers on the surface abrade, tangle into small balls, and remain attached by stronger fibers','Surface fibers loosen with wear/washing, entangle into small balls (pills) that stay anchored by a few strong fibers still rooted in the fabric.',3),
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222','advanced','multiple_choice','Fabric relaxation shrinkage occurs mainly because:','["Fibers physically shrink under heat","Internal stresses imparted during weaving/finishing (stretch) relax once the fabric is wetted/laundered, allowing it to return toward its natural dimensions","Dye causes fiber swelling","Sizing chemicals dissolve"]','Internal stresses imparted during weaving/finishing (stretch) relax once the fabric is wetted/laundered, allowing it to return toward its natural dimensions','Tension applied during weaving, dyeing, and finishing stretches fabric; washing lets those locked-in stresses relax, causing dimensional shrinkage.',3),
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222','advanced','multiple_choice','A fabric containing spandex/elastane primarily gains which property?','["Increased stiffness","Stretch and recovery (elasticity)","Higher GSM only","Reduced breathability only"]','Stretch and recovery (elasticity)','Even a small percentage of spandex (often 2-5%) core-spun or plied with other yarns gives fabric significant stretch and shape-recovery.',3),
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222','advanced','multiple_choice','"Grin-through" is a fabric defect where:','["The core yarn (e.g. spandex) shows through the fabric surface, especially after stretching","Fabric shrinks unevenly","Color runs during washing","Fabric develops holes"]','The core yarn (e.g. spandex) shows through the fabric surface, especially after stretching','Insufficient cover of the sheath fiber over a core yarn lets the core (often white spandex) become visible on the surface when the fabric stretches.',3),
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222','advanced','multiple_choice','Technical textiles differ from conventional apparel textiles primarily because they are selected for:','["Fashion appeal only","Specific functional/performance properties (e.g. strength, filtration, fire resistance) for a defined application","Lower cost only","Bright color range only"]','Specific functional/performance properties (e.g. strength, filtration, fire resistance) for a defined application','Technical textiles (geotextiles, medical textiles, protective wear, etc.) are engineered primarily for performance in a specific use, not fashion.',3),
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222','advanced','multiple_choice','Composite (bonded/laminated) fabrics combine two or more layers mainly to:','["Reduce total fabric weight always","Achieve a combination of properties (e.g. water resistance plus breathability) not available from a single layer","Simplify the dyeing process","Eliminate the need for finishing"]','Achieve a combination of properties (e.g. water resistance plus breathability) not available from a single layer','Laminating a membrane or bonding face and backing fabrics lets manufacturers combine complementary properties like waterproofing with a soft hand.',3),
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222','advanced','multiple_choice','Areal density variation across a fabric roll width most often indicates:','["Consistent quality","Uneven yarn tension or drafting/knitting irregularities during production","Correct dye uptake","Ideal finishing"]','Uneven yarn tension or drafting/knitting irregularities during production','GSM variation side-to-side or along a roll usually traces back to tension or process irregularities upstream in weaving/knitting.',3),
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222','advanced','multiple_choice','Fabric "skewing" or "bowing" defects are primarily corrected during:','["Dyeing only","Finishing, typically on a skew/bow straightening range prior to or after dyeing","Spinning","Ginning"]','Finishing, typically on a skew/bow straightening range prior to or after dyeing','Distortion where weft/courses run diagonally or curved across the fabric width is corrected mechanically on straightening equipment in finishing.',3),
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222','advanced','multiple_choice','Fabric stiffness is commonly quantified using which test?','["Cantilever bending (stiffness) test","Crockmeter test","Martindale abrasion test","Bursting strength test"]','Cantilever bending (stiffness) test','The cantilever test measures the length of fabric overhang needed to bend under its own weight to a set angle, giving a bending length/stiffness value.',3),
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222','advanced','multiple_choice','Air permeability testing of fabric measures:','["Color fastness to light","The rate of airflow through fabric under a set pressure differential","Fabric elongation","Yarn twist"]','The rate of airflow through fabric under a set pressure differential','This test quantifies fabric breathability/openness by measuring air volume passing through a set area under standardized pressure.',3),
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222','advanced','multiple_choice','A fabric with high cover factor and tight construction generally has:','["Higher air permeability","Lower air permeability and improved wind/water resistance","No change in permeability","Higher pilling only"]','Lower air permeability and improved wind/water resistance','Tightly packed yarns leave fewer/smaller inter-yarn gaps, restricting airflow and improving resistance to wind and light water penetration.',3),
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222','advanced','multiple_choice','Fabric width shrinkage after wet processing is generally compensated for during weaving by:','["Weaving intentionally narrower than final target width","Weaving intentionally wider (with allowance) than the required finished width","Ignoring shrinkage entirely","Adding more warp yarns only"]','Weaving intentionally wider (with allowance) than the required finished width','Since finishing/dyeing typically shrinks fabric width, mills weave with a calculated extra allowance so the finished product meets target width.',3),
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222','advanced','multiple_choice','A "seersucker" fabric effect is achieved by:','["Uniform tension across all warp yarns","Alternating high and low tension warp yarns (or differential shrinkage) to create a puckered stripe effect","Only using a plain weave with equal EPI/PPI","Special dye application"]','Alternating high and low tension warp yarns (or differential shrinkage) to create a puckered stripe effect','Seersucker''s characteristic puckered stripes come from warp sections woven at different tensions (or fibers with different shrinkage), causing localized puckering.',3),
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222','advanced','multiple_choice','Fabric "hand feel" (drape and softness) is influenced most by which combination of factors?','["Only fiber color","Fiber type, yarn twist, fabric construction, and finishing treatment together","Only loom speed","Only dye class used"]','Fiber type, yarn twist, fabric construction, and finishing treatment together','Hand is a holistic result of fiber softness, yarn structure, weave/knit tightness, and any softening/finishing chemistry applied.',3),
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222','advanced','multiple_choice','Dimensional stability testing (e.g. AATCC 135 for wash shrinkage) evaluates:','["Fabric color change only","How much a fabric shrinks or grows after specified home-laundering cycles","Yarn twist retention","Fabric tear strength"]','How much a fabric shrinks or grows after specified home-laundering cycles','This standard test launders samples under controlled conditions and measures length/width change, a key apparel quality spec.',3),
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222','advanced','multiple_choice','Which factor most directly increases a woven fabric''s tensile strength in the warp direction?','["Higher warp yarn count/strength and higher EPI, up to a practical limit","Lower EPI only","Reduced warp yarn twist to zero","Higher weft density alone"]','Higher warp yarn count/strength and higher EPI, up to a practical limit','More/stronger warp yarns per inch directly add to the total load-bearing capacity in that direction, though excessive density can cause other issues.',3),
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222','advanced','multiple_choice','A denim fabric''s characteristic diagonal line on the face is produced by:','["A plain weave structure","A twill weave structure (typically 3/1 warp-faced twill)","A satin weave structure","A leno weave structure"]','A twill weave structure (typically 3/1 warp-faced twill)','Denim is classically a warp-faced twill (often 3x1), giving its signature diagonal wale line and warp-dominant indigo face.',3),
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222','advanced','multiple_choice','Ripstop fabric construction resists tear propagation by:','["Using only very fine yarns throughout","Interweaving periodic thicker reinforcement yarns in a grid pattern within a lighter base fabric","Applying a rubber coating only","Increasing dye concentration"]','Interweaving periodic thicker reinforcement yarns in a grid pattern within a lighter base fabric','Thicker yarns spaced in a grid act as reinforcement checkpoints, stopping small tears from spreading further across the lightweight base weave.',3),
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222','advanced','multiple_choice','Fabric bow (curvature of filling yarns across width) is a defect most likely caused by:','["Uneven tentering/stenter frame tension across fabric width during finishing","Correct dye recipe","Proper sizing","Balanced twist yarn"]','Uneven tentering/stenter frame tension across fabric width during finishing','Differential pull or heat across the stenter width causes filling yarns to curve rather than run straight, producing bow distortion.',3),
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222','advanced','multiple_choice','A "leno" weave structure is primarily used to:','["Create the tightest possible fabric","Lock open, low-density fabric structures in place by twisting warp yarns around weft to prevent slippage","Add maximum GSM","Achieve maximum stretch"]','Lock open, low-density fabric structures in place by twisting warp yarns around weft to prevent slippage','Leno crosses pairs of warp yarns around each weft pick, mechanically locking an otherwise loose/open structure (e.g. mosquito net, scrim) against yarn slippage.',3),
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222','advanced','multiple_choice','Fabric weight (GSM) targets are typically communicated in a tech pack primarily to ensure:','["Correct dye shade only","Consistent hand, drape, and cost across production batches","Faster shipping","Reduced labor cost"]','Consistent hand, drape, and cost across production batches','GSM is a key spec because it directly affects fabric cost, hand/drape, and performance consistency batch to batch.',3),
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222','advanced','multiple_choice','Which is the correct general relationship between fabric cover factor and drape?','["Higher cover factor generally reduces drape/fluidity, making fabric stiffer","Higher cover factor always improves drape","Cover factor has no relationship to drape","Lower cover factor always increases stiffness"]','Higher cover factor generally reduces drape/fluidity, making fabric stiffer','Denser yarn packing restricts inter-yarn mobility, generally reducing the fabric''s ability to drape and increasing perceived stiffness.',3),
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222','advanced','multiple_choice','Crease/wrinkle recovery angle testing evaluates:','["How much a folded fabric springs back toward flat after a set time under load","Fabric color change under light","Fabric shrinkage in hot water","Yarn twist direction"]','How much a folded fabric springs back toward flat after a set time under load','A folded sample is loaded briefly then released; the recovered angle (closer to 180 degrees = better) quantifies wrinkle-recovery performance.',3),
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222','advanced','multiple_choice','Fabric defect "reediness" (fine warp-way streaks) is typically caused by:','["Bent or damaged reed dents disturbing warp yarn spacing","Incorrect dye recipe","Excess weft twist","Fiber blend ratio error"]','Bent or damaged reed dents disturbing warp yarn spacing','Damaged reed wires create localized gaps or bunching in warp spacing, showing as fine streak lines running the length of the fabric.',3),
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222','advanced','multiple_choice','A "double cloth" (double-layer woven) fabric structure is created by:','["Weaving two separate fabric layers simultaneously on one loom, interconnected by binding threads at intervals","Simply doubling the weft density","Adding a second dye bath","Using two different loom widths"]','Weaving two separate fabric layers simultaneously on one loom, interconnected by binding threads at intervals','Two warp/weft sets form two distinct cloth layers on the same loom, periodically stitched together to create pockets, reversible designs, or extra insulation.',3),
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222','advanced','multiple_choice','Fabric stretch recovery (as opposed to raw stretch %) measures:','["Maximum elongation only","How well fabric returns to its original dimension after being stretched and released","Fabric weight after washing","Dye fastness under stretch"]','How well fabric returns to its original dimension after being stretched and released','Recovery% is critical because a fabric can stretch a lot yet perform poorly if it doesn''t snap back, leading to baggy/sagging garments over wear.',3),
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222','advanced','multiple_choice','Waterproof-breathable membrane fabrics (e.g. laminated microporous films) work by having pores that are:','["Larger than a water droplet but smaller than a water vapor molecule","Smaller than a water droplet but larger than a water vapor molecule","Equal in size to both water droplets and vapor molecules","Completely sealed with no pores"]','Smaller than a water droplet but larger than a water vapor molecule','Microporous membranes are engineered so liquid water (larger droplets) cannot pass through, while gaseous water vapor molecules (much smaller) can escape.',3),
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222','advanced','multiple_choice','Flame-retardant technical fabrics achieve their property mainly through:','["Fiber inherent chemistry (e.g. aramid/modacrylic) or applied FR finishing chemicals","High GSM alone","Tight weave alone","Bright dye color"]','Fiber inherent chemistry (e.g. aramid/modacrylic) or applied FR finishing chemicals','Flame resistance comes either from inherently FR fibers (aramids, modacrylic) or from topical/back-coating FR chemical treatments on standard fibers.',3),
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222','advanced','multiple_choice','A "sateen" fabric (as opposed to satin) is distinguished by:','["Using filament yarns exclusively","Using a satin-type weave structure but constructed with staple spun yarns (often cotton)","Having no float yarns at all","Being a knitted, not woven, structure"]','Using a satin-type weave structure but constructed with staple spun yarns (often cotton)','Satin traditionally uses filament yarn (e.g. silk); sateen achieves a similar lustrous float-weave surface using spun staple yarn, typically cotton.',3),
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222','advanced','multiple_choice','Fabric inspection using the 4-point system assigns penalty points mainly based on:','["Fabric color only","Length and severity of each defect found per 100 square yards inspected","Fabric width only","Yarn count only"]','Length and severity of each defect found per 100 square yards inspected','The 4-point system scores defects (1-4 points depending on defect length) and totals them per 100 sq yd to grade a fabric roll''s acceptability.',3);
INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points) VALUES
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7','advanced','multiple_choice','The three primary motions of any weaving loom are:','["Sizing, dyeing, finishing","Shedding, picking, and beating-up","Carding, drawing, combing","Winding, warping, drawing-in"]','Shedding, picking, and beating-up','Every loom, regardless of type, performs shed formation (shedding), weft insertion (picking), and pushing the new pick into the fell (beating-up).',3),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7','advanced','multiple_choice','Shedding is the loom motion responsible for:','["Cutting the fabric","Raising and lowering warp yarns to form a gap (shed) for weft insertion","Winding the fabric onto the cloth roller","Inserting the weft yarn"]','Raising and lowering warp yarns to form a gap (shed) for weft insertion','Heald frames (or jacquard hooks) raise/lower warp ends per the design, opening the shed through which the weft carrier passes.',3),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7','advanced','multiple_choice','A dobby shedding mechanism, compared to simple tappet/cam shedding, allows:','["Only plain weave","More complex weave patterns using a greater number of independently controlled healds","Faster loom speed only","No pattern control at all"]','More complex weave patterns using a greater number of independently controlled healds','Dobbies control many more heald frames independently than cam/tappet systems, enabling more intricate small-repeat patterns beyond basic weaves.',3),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7','advanced','multiple_choice','A jacquard mechanism differs from a dobby mainly because it controls:','["Only 2-4 heald frames","Individual warp ends directly (thousands of hooks), enabling large, intricate designs","Only weft insertion speed","Only the beating-up motion"]','Individual warp ends directly (thousands of hooks), enabling large, intricate designs','Jacquard uses a hook per warp end (or small group), so each thread can be controlled independently for elaborate, large-repeat woven designs.',3),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7','advanced','multiple_choice','Air-jet weft insertion propels the weft yarn using:','["A mechanical rapier arm","Compressed air jets guiding yarn through the shed via a profile reed","A metal projectile","A shuttle containing a weft pirn"]','Compressed air jets guiding yarn through the shed via a profile reed','Main and relay nozzles blast compressed air along a grooved profile reed, carrying the weft yarn pneumatically across the shed at very high speed.',3),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7','advanced','multiple_choice','Rapier weaving inserts weft using:','["Compressed air only","Rigid or flexible rapier bands that carry/pass the weft yarn across the shed","Water jets exclusively","A traditional flying shuttle only"]','Rigid or flexible rapier bands that carry/pass the weft yarn across the shed','One or two rapiers extend across the shed from each side, gripping and passing (or transferring) the weft yarn mechanically instead of pneumatically.',3),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7','advanced','multiple_choice','Water-jet looms are best suited for weaving which type of yarn?','["Hydrophilic cotton yarn","Hydrophobic synthetic filament yarn (e.g. polyester, nylon)","Wool yarn","Any natural fiber yarn equally well"]','Hydrophobic synthetic filament yarn (e.g. polyester, nylon)','Since a water jet carries the weft, only water-resistant (hydrophobic) filament yarns are practical -- water-absorbent fibers would be damaged/soiled.',3),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7','advanced','multiple_choice','Projectile weaving machines insert weft by:','["Firing a small gripper projectile carrying the weft yarn across the shed","Using only compressed air","Using a flexible rapier band","Using water pressure"]','Firing a small gripper projectile carrying the weft yarn across the shed','A small metal gripper projectile is accelerated (historically by a torsion bar) to carry a length of weft across the full shed width.',3),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7','advanced','multiple_choice','Beating-up is the loom motion that:','["Opens the shed","Pushes the newly inserted weft pick firmly against the fell of the fabric using the reed","Winds the warp beam","Removes fabric defects"]','Pushes the newly inserted weft pick firmly against the fell of the fabric using the reed','The reed, mounted on the sley, swings forward each cycle to compact/beat the just-inserted weft against previously woven fabric at the fell line.',3),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7','advanced','multiple_choice','Let-off motion on a loom controls:','["Cloth take-up speed","Controlled, tension-regulated release of warp yarn from the warp beam as weaving proceeds","Weft insertion timing","Shed height"]','Controlled, tension-regulated release of warp yarn from the warp beam as weaving proceeds','Let-off pays out warp yarn at a rate matched to fabric consumption while maintaining consistent warp tension throughout weaving.',3),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7','advanced','multiple_choice','Take-up motion on a loom is responsible for:','["Releasing warp from the beam","Advancing/winding the newly woven fabric forward at a rate that sets pick density (PPI)","Opening the shed","Beating the weft into place"]','Advancing/winding the newly woven fabric forward at a rate that sets pick density (PPI)','Take-up pulls finished fabric forward a set distance per pick, and this distance directly determines picks-per-inch (weft density).',3),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7','advanced','multiple_choice','Warping (beam warping/sectional warping) is the process of:','["Weaving the fabric","Winding many warp ends from a creel of yarn packages onto a single warp beam under controlled tension","Applying size chemicals","Drawing warp through healds"]','Winding many warp ends from a creel of yarn packages onto a single warp beam under controlled tension','Warping combines hundreds/thousands of individual yarn ends from a creel into a parallel sheet wound uniformly onto a beam for weaving preparation.',3),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7','advanced','multiple_choice','Sizing (slashing) is applied to warp yarn primarily to:','["Add color","Coat/bind fibers to reduce hairiness and improve abrasion resistance during weaving","Add twist","Remove impurities"]','Coat/bind fibers to reduce hairiness and improve abrasion resistance during weaving','A size film (starch, PVA, or similar) binds protruding fibers and adds surface protection so warp survives heald/reed friction and shedding stress.',3),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7','advanced','multiple_choice','Drawing-in refers to the process of:','["Weaving the first few picks of fabric","Threading individual warp ends through heald eyes and reed dents in the correct sequence before weaving","Removing woven fabric from the loom","Applying finishing chemicals"]','Threading individual warp ends through heald eyes and reed dents in the correct sequence before weaving','Each warp end must be manually or automatically drawn through a specific heald eye and reed dent according to the draft plan and weave design.',3),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7','advanced','multiple_choice','Loom efficiency is calculated as:','["Total picks woven divided by picks the loom could theoretically produce running at rated speed for the same period","Fabric width divided by warp count","Total looms in the shed divided by weavers employed","Yarn strength divided by twist"]','Total picks woven divided by picks the loom could theoretically produce running at rated speed for the same period','Efficiency% accounts for stoppages (weft breaks, warp breaks, doffing, etc.), comparing actual output against maximum theoretical output.',3),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7','advanced','multiple_choice','A "float" fault in woven fabric refers to:','["A yarn that skips over more warp/weft threads than the weave design intends, without proper interlacing","A color variation","A width variation","A twist defect in yarn"]','A yarn that skips over more warp/weft threads than the weave design intends, without proper interlacing','Floats occur when a warp or weft thread fails to interlace as designed (often from a missed heald/reed threading or mechanism fault), leaving a visible unbound skip.',3),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7','advanced','multiple_choice','A temple (on a weaving loom) is used to:','["Hold the warp beam in place","Keep the woven fabric at its full width near the fell, preventing selvedge draw-in","Insert the weft","Control shed height"]','Keep the woven fabric at its full width near the fell, preventing selvedge draw-in','Temples grip the fabric edges just behind the fell line, resisting the natural narrowing (draw-in) tendency caused by weft tension during weaving.',3),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7','advanced','multiple_choice','Selvedge in woven fabric refers to:','["The center design motif","The self-finished, non-fraying edge running along both sides of the fabric length","A type of weave defect","The reverse side of the fabric"]','The self-finished, non-fraying edge running along both sides of the fabric length','Selvedges are specially constructed edges (often with tighter/leno binding) that prevent the fabric from unraveling and identify fabric orientation.',3),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7','advanced','multiple_choice','A leno selvedge specifically helps prevent:','["Fabric shrinkage","Weft yarns from slipping out at the fabric edges","Warp beam misalignment","Color bleeding"]','Weft yarns from slipping out at the fabric edges','Leno crossing warp threads at the edge locks each weft pick in place, stopping it from pulling loose at the selvedge during handling/finishing.',3),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7','advanced','multiple_choice','Weft insertion rate (WIR) on modern high-speed looms is expressed as:','["Meters of weft inserted per minute","Total warp beam weight","Number of looms per operator","Reed width in inches"]','Meters of weft inserted per minute','WIR (m/min) = picks per minute x fabric width, a key benchmark for comparing loom productivity across machine types and widths.',3),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7','advanced','multiple_choice','A "reed mark" defect appears in fabric as:','["A color variation","Fine warp-direction lines caused by damaged, bent, or incorrectly spaced reed dents","A weft-direction crease only","A pilling defect"]','Fine warp-direction lines caused by damaged, bent, or incorrectly spaced reed dents','Faulty reed dents disturb even warp spacing locally, producing thin/thick streak lines running the length (warp direction) of the fabric.',3),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7','advanced','multiple_choice','Warp stop motion on a loom automatically:','["Increases loom speed","Stops the loom when a warp end breaks, preventing a defective/missing-end fabric from being woven","Adjusts dye recipe","Changes weave pattern"]','Stops the loom when a warp end breaks, preventing a defective/missing-end fabric from being woven','Drop wires resting on each warp end fall and complete a stop circuit the instant a warp end breaks, halting the loom immediately.',3),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7','advanced','multiple_choice','Weft stop motion (weft feeler) performs a similar role to warp stop motion by:','["Stopping the loom when a weft yarn breaks or runs out","Increasing weft density","Changing the shed pattern","Speeding up take-up"]','Stopping the loom when a weft yarn breaks or runs out','Electronic/mechanical weft sensors detect a missing or broken weft pick and halt the loom before further faulty picks are inserted.',3),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7','advanced','multiple_choice','Denting order refers to:','["The color sequence of warp yarns","How many warp ends are threaded through each reed dent (e.g. 2 ends/dent)","The order fabric rolls are shipped","The sequence of finishing chemicals"]','How many warp ends are threaded through each reed dent (e.g. 2 ends/dent)','Denting order specifies ends per dent, directly affecting fabric density, appearance, and how evenly warp yarns are spaced across the width.',3),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7','advanced','multiple_choice','A "cross weave" or plain weave structure interlaces warp and weft in which pattern?','["Every warp thread over 3, under 1 weft thread","Every warp thread alternately over one, under one weft thread (1/1 interlacing)","No interlacing at all","Warp threads always float over 5 wefts"]','Every warp thread alternately over one, under one weft thread (1/1 interlacing)','Plain weave is the simplest and most interlaced structure, with maximum thread intersections (1 up, 1 down), giving strength and stability.',3),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7','advanced','multiple_choice','Twill weave, compared to plain weave at the same thread density, generally allows:','["Lower thread density due to fewer interlacements per unit area","Higher thread density due to fewer interlacements per unit area, since floats reduce friction points","Identical maximum density always","No difference in achievable density"]','Higher thread density due to fewer interlacements per unit area, since floats reduce friction points','Twill''s longer floats mean fewer intersections resisting compaction, so more threads per inch can typically be packed in than an equally fine plain weave.',3),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7','advanced','multiple_choice','Satin weave structures are characterized by:','["Maximum interlacing points for strength","Long floats with interlacing points deliberately dispersed to avoid visible diagonal lines, producing a smooth, lustrous surface","No warp yarns at all","Only being usable with wool fiber"]','Long floats with interlacing points deliberately dispersed to avoid visible diagonal lines, producing a smooth, lustrous surface','Satin''s interlacement points are scattered (not aligned diagonally like twill), leaving long uninterrupted floats that reflect light for a smooth, shiny face.',3),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7','advanced','multiple_choice','Loom speed (picks per minute) is generally inversely related to fabric width because:','["Wider looms have less reed friction","The reed and sley must travel a longer distance each beat cycle on wider looms, limiting achievable mechanical speed","Wider fabric always uses finer yarn","Fabric width does not affect loom mechanics"]','The reed and sley must travel a longer distance each beat cycle on wider looms, limiting achievable mechanical speed','Wider looms have heavier, longer-travel beating mechanisms, so the maximum safe cyclical speed (RPM/picks per minute) tends to be lower than on narrow looms.',3),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7','advanced','multiple_choice','A shuttle loom, compared to modern shuttleless looms, generally has:','["Higher production speed","Much lower production speed and higher noise, largely phased out in modern mills","Superior weft insertion consistency at high speed","No selvedge formation capability"]','Much lower production speed and higher noise, largely phased out in modern mills','Traditional shuttle looms are noisy, slower, and less efficient than air-jet/rapier/projectile shuttleless technology, which dominates modern production.',3),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7','advanced','multiple_choice','Multi-phase or multi-shed weaving concepts aim primarily to:','["Reduce fabric width","Insert multiple weft picks simultaneously across different shed sections to boost production speed beyond single-phase looms","Eliminate the need for warp yarn","Simplify pattern design only"]','Insert multiple weft picks simultaneously across different shed sections to boost production speed beyond single-phase looms','By opening several sheds simultaneously along the loom width, multiple weft insertions happen per cycle, aiming for much higher output than one-pick-at-a-time weaving.',3),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7','advanced','multiple_choice','Warp tension uniformity across a loom''s full width is critical mainly because uneven tension causes:','["Better fabric quality","Uneven fell line, fabric distortion, and increased warp breakage in the tighter sections","Faster weaving with no downsides","Lower yarn consumption"]','Uneven fell line, fabric distortion, and increased warp breakage in the tighter sections','Tension variation across the warp sheet stresses tighter ends more (raising breakage risk) and can distort the woven fabric structure and fell line.',3),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7','advanced','multiple_choice','A "pick finding" or repair process in fabric inspection addresses:','["Replacing an entire fabric roll","Manually correcting or marking isolated missing/broken weft picks found during inspection","Recalibrating the loom shedding motion","Re-dyeing the fabric"]','Manually correcting or marking isolated missing/broken weft picks found during inspection','Menders manually correct small, isolated weaving faults (like a missing pick) post-weaving rather than scrapping the whole fabric length.',3),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7','advanced','multiple_choice','Warp let-off tension control on modern looms is typically achieved via:','["Manual hand-cranking only","Electronically controlled, load-cell-monitored motorized let-off systems","A single fixed weight regardless of beam diameter","No tension control at all"]','Electronically controlled, load-cell-monitored motorized let-off systems','Modern looms use load cells and servo motors to continuously sense and adjust warp tension as beam diameter and weaving conditions change.',3),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7','advanced','multiple_choice','Positive versus negative shedding mechanisms differ in that positive shedding:','["Cannot form a shed at all","Mechanically controls both the raising and lowering of healds, while negative shedding relies partly on springs/gravity for the return motion","Only works on jacquard machines","Requires no heald frames"]','Mechanically controls both the raising and lowering of healds, while negative shedding relies partly on springs/gravity for the return motion','Positive tappet/dobby systems actively drive healds both up and down; negative systems actively raise but rely on springs or weights to return healds, generally limiting achievable speed.',3),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7','advanced','multiple_choice','A "starting mark" fabric defect appears when:','["A loom runs continuously without stopping","A loom is restarted after a stop without proper tension/timing adjustment, leaving a visible density line across the fabric","Weft yarn color changes","Warp beam is replaced"]','A loom is restarted after a stop without proper tension/timing adjustment, leaving a visible density line across the fabric','Restarting after a stoppage can momentarily alter pick spacing or tension, leaving a faint but visible band (starting mark) across the fabric width.',3),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7','advanced','multiple_choice','Heald frame (harness) capacity limits how many which type of design element a tappet/dobby loom can weave?','["Colors of dye only","Distinct warp-yarn movement groups, limiting weave repeat/pattern complexity","Weft insertion speed","Fabric width"]','Distinct warp-yarn movement groups, limiting weave repeat/pattern complexity','Each heald frame moves as one group; the number of frames available caps how many independently-controlled warp groups (and thus pattern complexity) are possible.',3),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7','advanced','multiple_choice','Pick density (PPI, picks per inch) is primarily controlled at the loom by adjusting:','["Warp tension only","The take-up motion''s fabric advance rate per pick","Reed width","Shedding timing only"]','The take-up motion''s fabric advance rate per pick','Since take-up advances the fabric a set distance for each pick inserted, that advance distance directly sets how many picks land per inch.',3),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7','advanced','multiple_choice','A "temple mark" defect in woven fabric is caused by:','["Excessive dye penetration","The fabric-gripping pins/rings of the temple damaging or marking yarns near the selvedge","Improper drying temperature","Yarn count variation"]','The fabric-gripping pins/rings of the temple damaging or marking yarns near the selvedge','Worn or misadjusted temple rings/pins can snag, pierce, or abrade yarns at the fabric edge, leaving visible marks or small holes near the selvedge.',3),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7','advanced','multiple_choice','Compared to rapier weaving, air-jet weaving is generally preferred for:','["Very heavy, coarse yarns exclusively","High-speed production of lighter to medium weight fabrics with lower air-consumption yarns","Only jacquard heavy brocades","Weaving with no weft insertion needed"]','High-speed production of lighter to medium weight fabrics with lower air-consumption yarns','Air-jet excels at very high picks-per-minute for lighter/medium fabrics; very heavy or bulky yarns are often better suited to rapier due to reliable mechanical carrying.',3),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7','advanced','multiple_choice','A "double pick" fabric fault occurs when:','["Two weft picks are inserted into the same shed position, creating a locally doubled/thick weft area","Warp tension is too low","The reed is misaligned","Sizing is applied unevenly"]','Two weft picks are inserted into the same shed position, creating a locally doubled/thick weft area','A timing/sensor malfunction can cause the loom to insert an extra pick without advancing take-up, stacking two picks in the same position.',3);
INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points) VALUES
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac','advanced','multiple_choice','Reactive dyes bond to cellulosic fibers (like cotton) mainly through:','["Physical entrapment only, with no chemical bond","A covalent chemical bond formed between the dye and the fiber''s hydroxyl groups","Weak van der Waals forces exclusively","Ionic attraction to protein fiber only"]','A covalent chemical bond formed between the dye and the fiber''s hydroxyl groups','Reactive dyes contain a reactive group that forms a true covalent bond with cellulose hydroxyl groups, giving excellent wash fastness.',3),
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac','advanced','multiple_choice','Disperse dyes are specifically formulated for dyeing:','["Cotton fiber","Hydrophobic synthetic fibers such as polyester","Wool fiber only","Nylon exclusively, never polyester"]','Hydrophobic synthetic fibers such as polyester','Disperse dyes are fine, low-water-solubility particles that diffuse into hydrophobic synthetic fiber (mainly polyester) under heat/pressure.',3),
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac','advanced','multiple_choice','Vat dyes are known for their excellent fastness because they:','["Are water-soluble at all times, requiring no chemical treatment","Are applied in a soluble reduced (leuco) form, then oxidized back to an insoluble pigment trapped inside the fiber","Only dye synthetic fiber","Bond ionically to acid-dyeable fiber"]','Are applied in a soluble reduced (leuco) form, then oxidized back to an insoluble pigment trapped inside the fiber','Vat dyeing reduces the insoluble dye to a soluble leuco form for penetration, then re-oxidizes it in situ back to its original insoluble, highly fast pigment form.',3),
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac','advanced','multiple_choice','Acid dyes are primarily used for dyeing which fiber types?','["Cotton and linen","Protein fibers (wool, silk) and nylon","Polyester exclusively","Only glass fiber"]','Protein fibers (wool, silk) and nylon','Acid dyes have an affinity for the amine/amide groups present in protein fibers and polyamide (nylon), bonding under acidic dyebath conditions.',3),
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac','advanced','multiple_choice','Salt (sodium chloride/sulfate) added during reactive dyeing serves mainly to:','["Fix the dye chemically","Promote dye exhaustion by reducing the electrostatic repulsion between the negatively charged dye and fiber","Neutralize the dyebath pH permanently","Bleach the fiber"]','Promote dye exhaustion by reducing the electrostatic repulsion between the negatively charged dye and fiber','Both reactive dye and cellulose fiber carry negative charge in water; salt reduces this repulsion, driving more dye onto the fiber (exhaustion).',3),
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac','advanced','multiple_choice','Alkali (e.g. soda ash) is added in the fixation stage of reactive dyeing to:','["Reduce dye solubility","Raise pH so the fiber''s hydroxyl groups can react covalently with the dye''s reactive group","Lower the dyebath temperature","Add color depth"]','Raise pH so the fiber''s hydroxyl groups can react covalently with the dye''s reactive group','Alkaline conditions ionize cellulose hydroxyl groups, activating them to nucleophilically attack and covalently bond with the reactive dye.',3),
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac','advanced','multiple_choice','Reactive dye "hydrolysis" is problematic because it:','["Improves color fastness","Wastes dye by reacting it irreversibly with water instead of the fiber, requiring thorough wash-off","Increases dye exhaustion rate","Has no impact on dyeing efficiency"]','Wastes dye by reacting it irreversibly with water instead of the fiber, requiring thorough wash-off','Some reactive dye always reacts with water molecules instead of fiber; this hydrolyzed dye cannot bond further and must be washed out to prevent poor rub/wash fastness.',3),
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac','advanced','multiple_choice','Liquor ratio in dyeing refers to:','["The ratio of dye to auxiliary chemicals","The ratio of total dye liquor (water + chemicals) volume to the weight of fabric/yarn being dyed","The dyeing machine speed","The dyebath pH value"]','The ratio of total dye liquor (water + chemicals) volume to the weight of fabric/yarn being dyed','Liquor ratio (e.g. 1:10) expresses liters of dye liquor per kg of material; it affects dye exhaustion economics, water/chemical use, and shade uniformity.',3),
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac','advanced','multiple_choice','A lower liquor ratio (e.g. 1:5 vs 1:20) in exhaust dyeing generally:','["Uses more water and chemicals per kg fabric","Uses less water/chemicals per kg fabric but can make shade uniformity control more difficult","Has no effect on chemical usage","Always improves fastness with no trade-off"]','Uses less water/chemicals per kg fabric but can make shade uniformity control more difficult','Lower liquor ratio machines are more resource-efficient but require tighter process control since there is less liquor volume to buffer flow/temperature variation.',3),
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac','advanced','multiple_choice','Colorfastness to crocking (rubbing) is tested using a:','["Spectrophotometer","Crockmeter, rubbing a standard white cloth against the dyed sample dry and wet","Martindale tester","Elmendorf tester"]','Crockmeter, rubbing a standard white cloth against the dyed sample dry and wet','A crockmeter rubs a standardized white test cloth against the dyed fabric under controlled pressure, then the resulting staining is graded on the grey scale.',3),
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac','advanced','multiple_choice','The grey scale used in colorfastness testing rates staining/change on a scale typically from:','["0 to 100","1 to 5, where 5 means no change/staining and 1 means severe change/staining","A to Z","-10 to +10"]','1 to 5, where 5 means no change/staining and 1 means severe change/staining','Grade 5 indicates negligible color change or staining while grade 1 indicates severe change, following standard AATCC/ISO grey scale evaluation.',3),
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac','advanced','multiple_choice','Mercerization of cotton fabric/yarn is a treatment with concentrated caustic soda that primarily:','["Bleaches the fiber white","Swells the fiber, increasing luster, dye uptake, and strength","Removes all natural wax and pectin only","Shrinks the fabric permanently by 20%"]','Swells the fiber, increasing luster, dye uptake, and strength','Caustic soda under tension causes cotton fibers to swell and become more rounded/crystalline-aligned, boosting luster, dye affinity, and tensile strength.',3),
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac','advanced','multiple_choice','Sanforizing (compressive shrinkage) treatment is applied mainly to:','["Add color to fabric","Pre-shrink woven cotton fabric mechanically so it meets a residual shrinkage specification before garment making","Increase fabric weight","Remove size from warp yarn"]','Pre-shrink woven cotton fabric mechanically so it meets a residual shrinkage specification before garment making','A rubber blanket compresses the fabric structure, physically pre-shrinking it so the finished garment shrinks minimally in subsequent home laundering.',3),
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac','advanced','multiple_choice','Heat setting of polyester (and other thermoplastic) fabric is done primarily to:','["Add permanent color","Stabilize the fabric''s dimensions and set the fiber''s crystalline structure to resist further shrinkage/distortion","Remove all dye from the fabric","Increase moisture regain permanently"]','Stabilize the fabric''s dimensions and set the fiber''s crystalline structure to resist further shrinkage/distortion','Heating thermoplastic fiber above its glass transition (and below melting) locks in the fabric''s current dimensions/structure, preventing later shrink/distortion.',3),
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac','advanced','multiple_choice','Singeing is a finishing process that:','["Applies flame or heated plates to burn off surface fiber hairiness for a cleaner fabric surface","Adds a flame-retardant chemical coating","Bleaches the fabric completely","Adds crease-resistance permanently"]','Applies flame or heated plates to burn off surface fiber hairiness for a cleaner fabric surface','Passing fabric quickly over a gas flame or heated element burns off protruding surface fibers without damaging the main fabric structure.',3),
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac','advanced','multiple_choice','Enzyme washing (biopolishing/stone-wash alternative) of denim uses:','["Only pumice stones","Cellulase enzymes that selectively break down cellulose microfibrils on the fabric surface for a softer hand and worn look","Only bleach chemicals","Concentrated sulfuric acid"]','Cellulase enzymes that selectively break down cellulose microfibrils on the fabric surface for a softer hand and worn look','Cellulase enzymes hydrolyze surface cellulose fibrils, softening the hand and abrading indigo dye for a faded look, often replacing or reducing stone use.',3),
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac','advanced','multiple_choice','Resin (crosslinking/wrinkle-free) finishing of cotton works by:','["Coating fabric with a colored pigment","Forming crosslinks between cellulose chains that help fabric recover its shape after creasing","Removing all cellulose from the fiber","Reducing fiber strength intentionally"]','Forming crosslinks between cellulose chains that help fabric recover its shape after creasing','DMDHEU or similar crosslinking agents chemically bridge cellulose molecular chains, improving wrinkle recovery at some cost to strength/abrasion resistance.',3),
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac','advanced','multiple_choice','A jigger dyeing machine processes fabric by:','["Dyeing loose fiber in a rotating drum","Passing open-width fabric back and forth between two rollers through a stationary dye bath","Dyeing yarn packages under pressure","Spray-dyeing fabric continuously"]','Passing open-width fabric back and forth between two rollers through a stationary dye bath','A jigger winds fabric rope-free (open-width) repeatedly through the dye liquor between two batching rollers, suited to woven fabrics needing minimal creasing.',3),
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac','advanced','multiple_choice','Jet dyeing machines are especially suited to:','["Dyeing loose cotton fiber only","Rope-form dyeing of knitted fabric, circulating fabric and liquor together through a tube via a jet nozzle","Continuous open-width dyeing of denim warp","Dyeing garments after sewing exclusively"]','Rope-form dyeing of knitted fabric, circulating fabric and liquor together through a tube via a jet nozzle','Jet machines circulate fabric rope through a liquor-filled tube propelled by a jet nozzle, gentle enough for knits and efficient at liquor exchange.',3),
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac','advanced','multiple_choice','Package dyeing is the process of dyeing:','["Finished garments in bulk","Yarn wound on perforated packages (cheeses/cones), with dye liquor forced through the package under pressure","Loose fiber in bales","Fabric rolls only"]','Yarn wound on perforated packages (cheeses/cones), with dye liquor forced through the package under pressure','Yarn is wound on perforated tubes/cones and loaded onto carriers; dye liquor is pumped radially in and out through the package for even penetration.',3),
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac','advanced','multiple_choice','Continuous pad-batch dyeing (common for reactive dyeing of woven cotton) works by:','["Batch immersion in a rotating drum for hours","Padding (impregnating) fabric with dye liquor via rollers, then batching it on a roll to react/fix over an extended dwell time at ambient or low heat","Spray application followed by immediate cutting","Only dyeing loose fiber before spinning"]','Padding (impregnating) fabric with dye liquor via rollers, then batching it on a roll to react/fix over an extended dwell time at ambient or low heat','Fabric is padded with dye+alkali, then rolled and covered/rotated slowly for several hours to allow slow fixation, offering water/energy savings versus exhaust dyeing.',3),
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac','advanced','multiple_choice','Levelling agents in a dyebath are added to:','["Increase dye concentration","Control the rate of dye uptake so color is applied evenly across the material, avoiding patchy/uneven shade","Remove all dye at the end of the process","Bleach residual color"]','Control the rate of dye uptake so color is applied evenly across the material, avoiding patchy/uneven shade','Levelling (retarding) agents slow or regulate initial dye strike rate, giving the dye time to migrate and distribute evenly before full exhaustion/fixation.',3),
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac','advanced','multiple_choice','Dye "substantivity" (affinity) refers to:','["The dye''s color name","The inherent attraction/bonding tendency between a specific dye class and a specific fiber type","The dyebath temperature required","The cost per kg of dye"]','The inherent attraction/bonding tendency between a specific dye class and a specific fiber type','Substantivity describes how readily a dye is taken up by and held onto a particular fiber, driven by chemical/physical compatibility between dye and substrate.',3),
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac','advanced','multiple_choice','Spectrophotometer-based color measurement in a dye house is primarily used to:','["Measure fabric GSM","Objectively quantify color values (e.g. L*a*b*) for shade matching and pass/fail tolerance against a standard","Test fabric strength","Measure dye bath pH"]','Objectively quantify color values (e.g. L*a*b*) for shade matching and pass/fail tolerance against a standard','Spectrophotometers measure reflected light to derive standardized color coordinates, letting dye houses compare a dyed lot objectively against an approved standard.',3),
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac','advanced','multiple_choice','Delta E (dE) in color measurement represents:','["The exact shade name","A single numeric value expressing the total perceptible color difference between a sample and the standard","The dye concentration used","Fabric brightness only"]','A single numeric value expressing the total perceptible color difference between a sample and the standard','Delta E combines differences in lightness, hue, and chroma into one number; smaller values mean closer color match, with tolerances set per buyer requirements.',3),
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac','advanced','multiple_choice','Metamerism in dyed textiles refers to:','["Two samples that look identical under one light source but different under another","A permanent color change from washing","A dye that never fades","Fabric shrinkage under heat"]','Two samples that look identical under one light source but different under another','Because different dye recipes can produce spectrally different but visually similar colors under one light, they may mismatch under a different light source (metameric failure).',3),
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac','advanced','multiple_choice','Desizing, as a pretreatment before dyeing, aims to:','["Add size to warp yarn","Remove the size chemicals (starch/PVA) applied before weaving so subsequent wet processing chemicals can penetrate the fabric","Bleach the fabric white","Dye the fabric a base color"]','Remove the size chemicals (starch/PVA) applied before weaving so subsequent wet processing chemicals can penetrate the fabric','Size film that protected warp yarn during weaving would otherwise block scouring/bleaching/dyeing chemical penetration, so it must be removed first (often enzymatically).',3),
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac','advanced','multiple_choice','Scouring in wet processing primarily removes:','["Dye from the fiber","Natural impurities such as wax, oil, pectin, and residual size, improving fabric absorbency","Fiber strength","Loom oil only"]','Natural impurities such as wax, oil, pectin, and residual size, improving fabric absorbency','Alkaline scouring dissolves/emulsifies natural non-cellulosic impurities in raw cotton fiber, giving the fabric the absorbency needed for even dyeing.',3),
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac','advanced','multiple_choice','Bleaching (e.g. with hydrogen peroxide) in pretreatment aims to:','["Add permanent color","Remove natural coloring matter and residual impurities so the fabric achieves the required whiteness/dye-readiness","Increase fabric weight","Add flame resistance"]','Remove natural coloring matter and residual impurities so the fabric achieves the required whiteness/dye-readiness','Oxidative bleaching destroys natural pigments and residual impurities, providing the clean, absorbent white base needed for accurate, even dyeing.',3),
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac','advanced','multiple_choice','A "tailing" shade defect in dyed fabric refers to:','["Uniform color across the entire roll","Progressive shade variation along the length of a dyed batch/roll, often lighter or darker toward the end","A specific stripe pattern applied intentionally","Color running only at the selvedge"]','Progressive shade variation along the length of a dyed batch/roll, often lighter or darker toward the end','Tailing occurs when dye exhaustion or process conditions drift over the run (e.g. depletion of dye/chemical), causing a gradual shade shift end to end.',3),
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac','advanced','multiple_choice','Batch-to-batch shade variation is a major dyeing challenge primarily because:','["Fiber and process conditions can vary slightly, requiring tight recipe/process control and regular color measurement to hold tolerance","Dye recipes never change once set","Buyers never check color consistency","Only lighting affects shade, not process"]','Fiber and process conditions can vary slightly, requiring tight recipe/process control and regular color measurement to hold tolerance','Raw material lot variation, water quality, temperature control, and machine differences can all shift shade, so dye houses use spectrophotometer checks and tight SOPs to maintain consistency.',3),
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac','advanced','multiple_choice','Sulfur dyes are notable for offering:','["The brightest, most vivid shades available","Economical, generally dark/dull shades (e.g. black, navy) with good wash fastness on cellulosic fiber","Exclusive use on synthetic fiber","No wash fastness at all"]','Economical, generally dark/dull shades (e.g. black, navy) with good wash fastness on cellulosic fiber','Sulfur dyes are cost-effective for deep, dull shades like black and navy on cotton, applied via a reduction/oxidation mechanism similar in principle to vat dyeing.',3),
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac','advanced','multiple_choice','Azoic (naphthol) dyeing is a two-stage process involving:','["A single dip in a pre-mixed dye solution","Impregnating fiber with a naphthol coupling component, then developing color by reacting it with a diazonium salt","Only physical fiber bleaching","Simultaneous application of ten dye classes"]','Impregnating fiber with a naphthol coupling component, then developing color by reacting it with a diazonium salt','Fiber is first treated with a naphtholate, then "developed" by reacting with a diazonium base solution, forming an insoluble azo pigment inside the fiber for strong fastness.',3),
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac','advanced','multiple_choice','Effluent (wastewater) treatment is a critical concern in dyeing mills primarily because dye house effluent often contains:','["Only clean water","High levels of unfixed dye, salts, and chemical oxygen demand (COD) requiring treatment before discharge","No pollutants of concern","Only heat, no chemicals"]','High levels of unfixed dye, salts, and chemical oxygen demand (COD) requiring treatment before discharge','Wash-off water carries unfixed dye, high salt content, and organic/chemical load, all of which must be treated (e.g. via ETP) to meet environmental discharge standards.',3),
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac','advanced','multiple_choice','A calendering finishing process improves fabric appearance mainly by:','["Adding chemical crosslinks","Passing fabric between heated rollers under pressure to flatten, smooth, and add surface luster/glaze","Removing all dye","Increasing fabric thickness only"]','Passing fabric between heated rollers under pressure to flatten, smooth, and add surface luster/glaze','Heated calender rollers compress and smooth the fabric surface, which can also impart a glazed or embossed effect depending on roller configuration.',3),
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac','advanced','multiple_choice','Softener finishing applied after dyeing primarily aims to improve:','["Fabric tensile strength","Fabric hand/feel (softness) and sometimes sewability, often via silicone or cationic softening agents","Color fastness to light","Fabric GSM"]','Fabric hand/feel (softness) and sometimes sewability, often via silicone or cationic softening agents','Softeners deposit a thin lubricating layer on fiber surfaces, improving hand feel, drape, and reducing friction for easier cutting/sewing.',3),
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac','advanced','multiple_choice','Water-repellent (DWR) finishing works by:','["Chemically bonding a fiber-swelling agent","Applying a hydrophobic surface coating (e.g. fluorocarbon or silicone-based) that increases the fabric surface''s contact angle with water","Removing all fabric pores completely","Adding heavy pigment coating"]','Applying a hydrophobic surface coating (e.g. fluorocarbon or silicone-based) that increases the fabric surface''s contact angle with water','DWR chemistry coats individual fibers, raising the surface energy contact angle so water beads and rolls off rather than wetting through, without necessarily blocking breathability.',3),
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac','advanced','multiple_choice','Colorfastness to washing (e.g. ISO 105-C06) evaluates dyed fabric by:','["Exposing it to UV light only","Laundering it under standardized conditions with a multi-fiber adjacent fabric, then grading color change and staining","Rubbing it against a white cloth","Measuring only dry crocking"]','Laundering it under standardized conditions with a multi-fiber adjacent fabric, then grading color change and staining','This test launders the dyed sample alongside a multi-fiber strip under set conditions, then grades both the sample''s color change and the strip''s staining on the grey scale.',3),
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac','advanced','multiple_choice','pH control throughout wet processing is critical mainly because:','["Fabric color is unaffected by pH","Many dye classes and finishing chemistries only react/perform correctly within a specific pH range, and residual alkalinity can cause fiber/skin issues","pH never changes during processing","Only synthetic fiber is pH sensitive"]','Many dye classes and finishing chemistries only react/perform correctly within a specific pH range, and residual alkalinity can cause fiber/skin issues','Reactive dye fixation needs alkaline pH, acid dyes need acidic pH, and leftover alkalinity can cause fiber damage or skin irritation -- so pH is tightly monitored throughout.',3);
INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points) VALUES
('33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333','advanced','multiple_choice','Enzymatic desizing uses which type of enzyme to remove starch-based size?','["Cellulase","Amylase","Protease","Lipase"]','Amylase','Amylase enzymes specifically hydrolyze starch molecules into water-soluble sugars, allowing starch-based size to be washed off without damaging cellulose fiber.',3),
('33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333','advanced','multiple_choice','Compared to acid or oxidative desizing, enzymatic desizing is generally preferred because it:','["Damages the fiber more","Selectively breaks down size without significantly attacking the cellulose fiber itself","Cannot be used on cotton","Is always more expensive with no quality benefit"]','Selectively breaks down size without significantly attacking the cellulose fiber itself','Amylase enzymes are highly substrate-specific to starch, leaving cellulose fiber largely unaffected, unlike harsher acid/oxidative methods that risk fiber tendering.',3),
('33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333','advanced','multiple_choice','Kier boiling, a traditional batch scouring method, removes impurities by:','["Cold water rinsing only","Boiling fabric under pressure in an alkaline solution (often with surfactants) for an extended time to dissolve waxes/pectin","Applying dye directly","Mechanical brushing only"]','Boiling fabric under pressure in an alkaline solution (often with surfactants) for an extended time to dissolve waxes/pectin','Kier boiling exposes fabric to prolonged high-temperature alkaline treatment under pressure, thoroughly saponifying/emulsifying natural impurities.',3),
('33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333','advanced','multiple_choice','Hydrogen peroxide bleaching requires a stabilizer mainly to:','["Speed up the reaction uncontrollably","Prevent uncontrolled/premature peroxide decomposition that could damage fiber (over-oxidation) or waste chemical","Add color to the fabric","Neutralize the fabric completely"]','Prevent uncontrolled/premature peroxide decomposition that could damage fiber (over-oxidation) or waste chemical','Stabilizers (e.g. sodium silicate or organic stabilizers) control the peroxide decomposition rate, ensuring steady, safe bleaching without fiber tendering or wasted chemical.',3),
('33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333','advanced','multiple_choice','Catalase enzyme treatment after peroxide bleaching is used to:','["Add more peroxide","Rapidly and safely decompose residual hydrogen peroxide before subsequent dyeing, saving rinse water/time","Bleach the fabric further","Remove natural wax"]','Rapidly and safely decompose residual hydrogen peroxide before subsequent dyeing, saving rinse water/time','Catalase breaks down leftover peroxide into water and oxygen quickly at lower temperatures, avoiding the risk of peroxide interfering with dye (especially reactive dyes) and reducing rinsing needs.',3),
('33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333','advanced','multiple_choice','Water hardness (dissolved calcium/magnesium) in process water is a concern in wet processing mainly because it can:','["Improve dye fixation","Cause scale/deposit formation, interfere with surfactants, and lead to uneven dyeing or dull shades","Have no measurable effect","Bleach the fabric automatically"]','Cause scale/deposit formation, interfere with surfactants, and lead to uneven dyeing or dull shades','Hard water ions can precipitate with dyes/soaps, deposit scale on machinery, and cause dulling or unevenness, so demineralized/softened water is often required.',3),
('33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333','advanced','multiple_choice','A combined scouring-bleaching process (single-bath) is typically chosen to:','["Increase total processing time and cost","Save time, water, and energy by combining two pretreatment steps into one bath under compatible chemistry","Avoid removing any natural impurities","Replace dyeing entirely"]','Save time, water, and energy by combining two pretreatment steps into one bath under compatible chemistry','Combining compatible scouring and bleaching chemistry (e.g. alkaline peroxide bath) in a single stage reduces water, energy, and time versus two separate baths.',3),
('33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333','advanced','multiple_choice','Biopreparation refers to using enzymes to replace which conventional pretreatment step(s)?','["Dyeing","Conventional alkaline scouring, using pectinase/other enzymes to remove non-cellulosic impurities under milder conditions","Weaving","Sizing"]','Conventional alkaline scouring, using pectinase/other enzymes to remove non-cellulosic impurities under milder conditions','Pectinase-based biopreparation removes pectin and associated impurities under milder, more energy-efficient and eco-friendly conditions than traditional caustic scouring.',3),
('33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333','advanced','multiple_choice','Effluent Treatment Plant (ETP) primary treatment mainly involves:','["Biological digestion of organic matter","Physical/chemical processes (screening, equalization, coagulation-flocculation) to remove suspended solids and neutralize pH","Reverse osmosis only","UV disinfection only"]','Physical/chemical processes (screening, equalization, coagulation-flocculation) to remove suspended solids and neutralize pH','Primary treatment removes coarse solids and uses coagulation/flocculation plus pH neutralization to prepare wastewater for the biological (secondary) stage.',3),
('33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333','advanced','multiple_choice','Secondary ETP treatment typically uses which process to reduce organic pollutant load (BOD/COD)?','["Simple sedimentation only","Biological treatment (e.g. activated sludge process) that uses microorganisms to break down organic matter","Only UV filtration","Reverse osmosis exclusively"]','Biological treatment (e.g. activated sludge process) that uses microorganisms to break down organic matter','Secondary treatment relies on aerobic microorganisms in an activated sludge or similar biological process to consume and reduce dissolved organic pollutants (BOD/COD).',3),
('33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333','advanced','multiple_choice','Tertiary (advanced) ETP treatment is used when:','["No further purification is ever needed","Water needs to be purified further, e.g. for reuse or to meet stricter discharge standards, using methods like filtration, RO, or advanced oxidation","Only for removing solid waste","Only applicable to drinking water plants"]','Water needs to be purified further, e.g. for reuse or to meet stricter discharge standards, using methods like filtration, RO, or advanced oxidation','Tertiary treatment polishes secondary-treated effluent further (color removal, dissolved solids reduction) enabling safe discharge or water reuse in the mill.',3),
('33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333','advanced','multiple_choice','Zero Liquid Discharge (ZLD) systems in textile wet processing aim to:','["Discharge all wastewater directly to rivers","Treat and recycle essentially all process water, leaving no liquid effluent discharged to the environment","Increase total water consumption","Eliminate the need for an ETP"]','Treat and recycle essentially all process water, leaving no liquid effluent discharged to the environment','ZLD combines advanced treatment (e.g. RO, evaporation) to recover and reuse nearly all water on-site, minimizing/eliminating liquid discharge.',3),
('33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333','advanced','multiple_choice','BOD (Biochemical Oxygen Demand) in effluent measures:','["Total dissolved solids","The amount of oxygen microorganisms need to break down organic matter in the water over a set period (commonly 5 days)","Water color intensity","Water temperature"]','The amount of oxygen microorganisms need to break down organic matter in the water over a set period (commonly 5 days)','BOD5 quantifies biodegradable organic pollution load by measuring oxygen consumed by microbes decomposing it over 5 days at a standard temperature.',3),
('33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333','advanced','multiple_choice','COD (Chemical Oxygen Demand) differs from BOD in that COD measures:','["Only biodegradable matter","The total oxygen equivalent needed to chemically oxidize both biodegradable and non-biodegradable organic matter","Water pH only","Only inorganic salts"]','The total oxygen equivalent needed to chemically oxidize both biodegradable and non-biodegradable organic matter','COD uses a strong chemical oxidant to measure total oxidizable material (bio and non-biodegradable), generally giving a higher and faster-obtained value than BOD.',3),
('33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333','advanced','multiple_choice','ZDHC (Zero Discharge of Hazardous Chemicals) MRSL is a program that primarily:','["Sets fabric color standards","Restricts hazardous chemical inputs used in textile manufacturing to protect workers, consumers, and the environment","Regulates loom speed","Sets minimum wage standards"]','Restricts hazardous chemical inputs used in textile manufacturing to protect workers, consumers, and the environment','ZDHC''s Manufacturing Restricted Substances List (MRSL) restricts/bans specific hazardous chemicals from being intentionally used in production formulations.',3),
('33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333','advanced','multiple_choice','OEKO-TEX Standard 100 certification for a finished textile product primarily verifies:','["Factory labor conditions","That the product is tested and free from harmful substances above defined limits, safe for human use","Fabric GSM specification","Loom production capacity"]','That the product is tested and free from harmful substances above defined limits, safe for human use','OEKO-TEX Standard 100 tests the finished textile article itself against limits for a wide range of potentially harmful substances, certifying human-ecological safety.',3),
('33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333','advanced','multiple_choice','GOTS (Global Organic Textile Standard) certification, unlike OEKO-TEX 100, specifically requires:','["No requirement regarding fiber source","Both organic fiber content verification and environmental/social criteria across the full supply chain","Only chemical safety testing of the final product","Only water usage limits"]','Both organic fiber content verification and environmental/social criteria across the full supply chain','GOTS certifies organic fiber origin plus environmental processing criteria and social compliance across spinning, knitting/weaving, and wet processing stages.',3),
('33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333','advanced','multiple_choice','Ozone bleaching, an emerging alternative pretreatment/finishing technology, is notable for:','["Requiring large volumes of water and chemicals","Using ozone gas to achieve bleaching/fading effects with significantly reduced water and chemical consumption","Only working on synthetic fiber","Adding permanent dye color"]','Using ozone gas to achieve bleaching/fading effects with significantly reduced water and chemical consumption','Ozone technology (notably for denim fading) can replace or reduce conventional bleach/wash chemistry, cutting water and chemical usage substantially.',3),
('33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333','advanced','multiple_choice','Causticizing (mercerizing with recovery) systems recover and reuse:','["Dye liquor","Caustic soda from the wash water after mercerization, reducing chemical cost and effluent load","Water only, no chemicals","Steam only"]','Caustic soda from the wash water after mercerization, reducing chemical cost and effluent load','Caustic recovery plants concentrate and reuse dilute caustic soda washed off after mercerization, cutting chemical consumption and reducing effluent alkalinity.',3),
('33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333','advanced','multiple_choice','A continuous pretreatment range (as opposed to batch processing) processes fabric by:','["Loading small batches into a rotating drum","Passing open-width fabric continuously through sequential process zones (desize, scour, bleach, wash) at speed","Only dyeing loose fiber","Hand-dipping fabric repeatedly"]','Passing open-width fabric continuously through sequential process zones (desize, scour, bleach, wash) at speed','Continuous ranges move fabric steadily through connected chemical/steaming/washing zones, offering high throughput suited to large-volume woven fabric processing.',3),
('33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333','advanced','multiple_choice','Steam saturation/J-box units in a continuous pretreatment range are used to:','["Cool the fabric rapidly","Hold fabric under steam heat for a controlled dwell time to allow chemical reactions (e.g. bleaching) to complete","Dry the fabric fully","Apply final dye color"]','Hold fabric under steam heat for a controlled dwell time to allow chemical reactions (e.g. bleaching) to complete','A J-box (or steamer) provides the residence time and heat needed for chemical reactions like bleaching or desizing to reach completion before fabric moves to the next stage.',3),
('33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333','advanced','multiple_choice','Chemical padding (impregnation) in pretreatment uses a padder mangle mainly to:','["Dry the fabric completely","Squeeze fabric between rollers to achieve a controlled, uniform wet pick-up of chemical liquor","Add mechanical abrasion","Cut the fabric to width"]','Squeeze fabric between rollers to achieve a controlled, uniform wet pick-up of chemical liquor','Padder nip pressure controls how much liquor (and thus chemical) is retained per unit fabric weight, critical for consistent, even chemical application.',3),
('33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333','advanced','multiple_choice','Wet pick-up percentage in padding is defined as:','["Dry fabric weight only","The weight of liquor absorbed by the fabric expressed as a percentage of the fabric''s dry weight","Total machine speed","Fabric width after padding"]','The weight of liquor absorbed by the fabric expressed as a percentage of the fabric''s dry weight','Wet pick-up% = (wet weight - dry weight)/dry weight x 100, directly determining the actual chemical dosage delivered onto the fabric.',3),
('33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333','advanced','multiple_choice','Chemical recipe dosages in wet processing are typically expressed as:','["Percentage on weight of fabric/material (%owf)","Absolute liters regardless of batch size","Fixed kilograms regardless of fabric quantity","Percentage of machine capacity"]','Percentage on weight of fabric/material (%owf)','%owf standardizes dosage relative to the material weight being processed, so recipes scale consistently regardless of batch size.',3),
('33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333','advanced','multiple_choice','If a recipe calls for 2% owf of a chemical for a 500 kg batch, the actual chemical quantity required is:','["2 kg","10 kg","100 kg","500 kg"]','10 kg','2% of 500 kg = 0.02 x 500 = 10 kg, following the standard %owf dosage calculation used throughout wet processing.',3),
('33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333','advanced','multiple_choice','Fabric tendering (unwanted strength loss) after bleaching is most often caused by:','["Insufficient rinsing","Residual/excess oxidizing agent (e.g. uncontrolled peroxide) left reacting with cellulose fiber over time","Too little dye applied","Excess softener application"]','Residual/excess oxidizing agent (e.g. uncontrolled peroxide) left reacting with cellulose fiber over time','Uncontrolled or residual peroxide continues oxidizing cellulose chains, progressively weakening the fiber -- a key reason stabilizers and proper neutralization matter.',3),
('33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333','advanced','multiple_choice','A "pin hole" fabric defect from wet processing is most commonly caused by:','["Uneven weft density","Localized chemical over-concentration (e.g. acid/bleach spot) damaging and eventually perforating the fabric","Excess softener","Insufficient dye"]','Localized chemical over-concentration (e.g. acid/bleach spot) damaging and eventually perforating the fabric','A concentrated chemical droplet or uneven application can locally over-oxidize/degrade fiber to the point of forming a small hole.',3),
('33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333','advanced','multiple_choice','Steam consumption is a major utility cost in wet processing mainly because it is required for:','["Cutting fabric","Heating dye baths, drying, and curing/fixation stages throughout pretreatment, dyeing, and finishing","Only loom operation","Only spinning"]','Heating dye baths, drying, and curing/fixation stages throughout pretreatment, dyeing, and finishing','Nearly every wet processing stage (scouring, bleaching, dyeing, drying, curing) needs significant thermal energy, making steam one of the largest utility costs.',3),
('33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333','advanced','multiple_choice','Water reuse/recycling in a wet processing mill most commonly targets which water streams first?','["Only drinking water","Relatively cleaner streams like final rinse or cooling water, which need less treatment before reuse","Only the most heavily contaminated dye bath","No streams are ever suitable for reuse"]','Relatively cleaner streams like final rinse or cooling water, which need less treatment before reuse','Lower-contamination streams (final rinses, cooling water) are the easiest and most cost-effective to treat minimally and recirculate back into the process.',3),
('33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333','advanced','multiple_choice','Sludge generated at an ETP (from coagulation and biological treatment) must be:','["Discharged directly into rivers","Dewatered and disposed of or treated according to environmental regulations, since it concentrates pollutants removed from the wastewater","Reused as drinking water without treatment","Burned in the open air with no controls"]','Dewatered and disposed of or treated according to environmental regulations, since it concentrates pollutants removed from the wastewater','ETP sludge concentrates the solids and contaminants removed from wastewater and must be properly dewatered and disposed of per environmental regulations, not simply discharged.',3),
('33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333','advanced','multiple_choice','Color removal from dye house effluent (decolorization) before discharge often uses:','["Simple sedimentation alone","Advanced oxidation, adsorption (activated carbon), or membrane processes targeting dissolved/colloidal dye","Only pH neutralization","Only aeration"]','Advanced oxidation, adsorption (activated carbon), or membrane processes targeting dissolved/colloidal dye','Residual dye color, especially from reactive dyes, resists simple biological treatment, so advanced methods like oxidation, carbon adsorption, or membrane filtration are used for decolorization.',3),
('33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333','advanced','multiple_choice','Chemical Management System (CMS) audits in a wet processing mill primarily assess:','["Only fabric color accuracy","Proper storage, handling, inventory, and restricted substance compliance of process chemicals","Loom production speed","Yarn twist consistency"]','Proper storage, handling, inventory, and restricted substance compliance of process chemicals','CMS audits (often tied to ZDHC/brand requirements) verify safe chemical storage/handling and that no banned/restricted substances are used in the process.',3),
('33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333','advanced','multiple_choice','Formaldehyde limits in finished textiles (regulated in many markets) are a concern mainly because formaldehyde:','["Improves dye fastness with no risk","Can be released from certain resin/crosslinking finishes and cause skin/respiratory irritation at elevated levels","Is only present in synthetic fiber","Has no health-related regulation anywhere"]','Can be released from certain resin/crosslinking finishes and cause skin/respiratory irritation at elevated levels','Some crosslinking (easy-care/wrinkle-free) finishes can release free formaldehyde, so regulations and certifications like OEKO-TEX set strict residual limits.',3),
('33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333','advanced','multiple_choice','Azo dye restrictions in textile regulations specifically target azo dyes that can:','["Improve wash fastness","Cleave under reductive conditions to release specific carcinogenic aromatic amines","Only affect synthetic fiber dyeing","Increase fabric strength"]','Cleave under reductive conditions to release specific carcinogenic aromatic amines','Certain azo dyes can reductively cleave (e.g. under skin contact/sweat conditions) to release banned aromatic amines classified as carcinogenic, hence strict regulatory limits.',3),
('33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333','advanced','multiple_choice','A dip/rinse counter-current washing system in a continuous range is designed to:','["Waste the maximum amount of water","Reuse progressively cleaner rinse water in the opposite direction of fabric flow, minimizing total fresh water consumption","Apply dye at each stage","Add sizing chemicals repeatedly"]','Reuse progressively cleaner rinse water in the opposite direction of fabric flow, minimizing total fresh water consumption','Counter-current washing feeds the cleanest water at the final rinse stage and reuses it backward through dirtier stages, substantially cutting fresh water demand.',3),
('33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333','advanced','multiple_choice','Total dissolved solids (TDS) becoming elevated in mill effluent is primarily driven by:','["Only dye pigment","Salts (e.g. from reactive dyeing) and other dissolved chemicals used across pretreatment/dyeing processes","Fiber lint only","Fabric color depth alone"]','Salts (e.g. from reactive dyeing) and other dissolved chemicals used across pretreatment/dyeing processes','Large quantities of salt used to promote reactive dye exhaustion, plus various process chemicals, significantly raise the dissolved solids load in effluent.',3),
('33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333','advanced','multiple_choice','A "shade band" issue across a fabric roll width in continuous dyeing/pretreatment is typically caused by:','["Correct even nip pressure across the padder","Uneven padder nip pressure or chemical application across the fabric width, causing side-to-side variation","Correct steam temperature","Uniform fabric tension"]','Uneven padder nip pressure or chemical application across the fabric width, causing side-to-side variation','If padder rollers apply uneven pressure (often more at the center or edges), chemical/dye pick-up varies across width, producing visible shade bands.',3),
('33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333','advanced','multiple_choice','Ecolabel water-usage benchmarks (e.g. liters per kg of fabric processed) are used by mills mainly to:','["Increase water consumption for higher output","Track and progressively reduce specific water consumption as part of sustainability compliance and cost control","Replace all ETP requirements","Set fabric color standards"]','Track and progressively reduce specific water consumption as part of sustainability compliance and cost control','Liters-per-kg benchmarking lets mills monitor efficiency over time, supporting both cost reduction and compliance with brand/certification sustainability targets.',3);
INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points) VALUES
('44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444','advanced','multiple_choice','SMV (Standard Minute Value) in garment production represents:','["The selling price of a garment","The standard time, in minutes, a skilled operator should take to complete one operation under defined conditions","The total factory shift length","The fabric consumption per garment"]','The standard time, in minutes, a skilled operator should take to complete one operation under defined conditions','SMV is derived from time/method study and used as the base unit for costing labor, planning capacity, and measuring efficiency.',3),
('44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444','advanced','multiple_choice','Line efficiency in a sewing line is calculated as:','["Total garments produced divided by number of workers","(Total minutes produced / Total minutes available) x 100, where minutes produced = output x SMV","Fabric consumed divided by fabric wasted","Total operators divided by machines"]','(Total minutes produced / Total minutes available) x 100, where minutes produced = output x SMV','Efficiency compares earned minutes (output achieved x SMV per piece) against the total available working minutes of all operators on the line.',3),
('44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444','advanced','multiple_choice','Line balancing in a sewing line aims to:','["Maximize the number of operations on a single machine","Distribute work content evenly across operators/workstations to minimize bottlenecks and idle time","Reduce fabric consumption","Increase SMV values"]','Distribute work content evenly across operators/workstations to minimize bottlenecks and idle time','Balancing assigns operations so each station''s cycle time is close to the line''s target cycle time, avoiding one slow station bottlenecking the whole line.',3),
('44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444','advanced','multiple_choice','A "bottleneck" operation in a production line is the one that:','["Has the fastest cycle time","Has the slowest cycle time, limiting the overall output rate of the entire line","Uses the least fabric","Has no operator assigned"]','Has the slowest cycle time, limiting the overall output rate of the entire line','The bottleneck sets the maximum throughput of the whole line since output cannot exceed what the slowest station can process per unit time.',3),
('44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444','advanced','multiple_choice','CM (Cut-Make) pricing in garment costing refers to a pricing model where the buyer:','["Pays for fabric, trims, cutting, and sewing together","Supplies or separately pays for fabric/trims, while the factory is paid only for cutting and making (labor/overhead)","Only pays for finished, packed garments with no breakdown","Pays exclusively for raw materials"]','Supplies or separately pays for fabric/trims, while the factory is paid only for cutting and making (labor/overhead)','Under CM, fabric/trim costs are handled separately (often buyer-nominated), and the factory''s quoted price covers only cutting and sewing labor plus overhead/profit.',3),
('44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444','advanced','multiple_choice','CMT (Cut-Make-Trim) pricing differs from CM mainly because CMT:','["Excludes cutting cost","Includes trims cost in the factory''s quoted price along with cutting and making, while fabric is usually still buyer-supplied","Includes fabric cost fully","Only covers packing"]','Includes trims cost in the factory''s quoted price along with cutting and making, while fabric is usually still buyer-supplied','CMT bundles trims (buttons, zippers, labels, etc.) cost into the factory''s price alongside cutting and sewing, typically leaving only fabric supplied separately.',3),
('44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444','advanced','multiple_choice','FOB (Free on Board) pricing for a garment order means the quoted price includes:','["Only sewing labor","All costs (fabric, trims, CM, overhead, profit) up to loading the goods onto the shipping vessel at the port of origin","Only shipping to the buyer''s warehouse","Only raw material cost"]','All costs (fabric, trims, CM, overhead, profit) up to loading the goods onto the shipping vessel at the port of origin','FOB is a full landed-at-port price covering the entire manufacturing cost chain plus margin, with the buyer responsible for freight/insurance from that point onward.',3),
('44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444','advanced','multiple_choice','5S workplace organization methodology stands for (in English translation):','["Sort, Set in order, Shine, Standardize, Sustain","Speed, Scale, Sew, Ship, Sell","Style, Size, Shape, Shade, Style","Sample, Sew, Sort, Ship, Score"]','Sort, Set in order, Shine, Standardize, Sustain','5S is a lean workplace organization system that systematically improves cleanliness, order, and discipline to boost efficiency and safety.',3),
('44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444','advanced','multiple_choice','Kaizen, as applied in apparel manufacturing, refers to:','["A one-time major factory renovation","A philosophy of continuous, incremental improvement involving all employees","A single quality-control checkpoint","A specific sewing machine brand"]','A philosophy of continuous, incremental improvement involving all employees','Kaizen emphasizes small, ongoing improvements driven by workers and management together, rather than occasional large-scale overhauls.',3),
('44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444','advanced','multiple_choice','Kanban, a lean production tool, is primarily used to:','["Design new garment styles","Visually signal and control the flow/replenishment of materials or work-in-process between stages, limiting overproduction","Measure fabric shrinkage","Test garment color fastness"]','Visually signal and control the flow/replenishment of materials or work-in-process between stages, limiting overproduction','Kanban cards/signals trigger the next process step only when needed, pulling production based on actual demand rather than pushing based on forecast.',3),
('44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444','advanced','multiple_choice','Value Stream Mapping (VSM) is used in lean manufacturing to:','["Design fabric prints","Visualize the entire flow of materials and information through a process, identifying waste and improvement opportunities","Calculate garment SMV only","Set factory holiday schedules"]','Visualize the entire flow of materials and information through a process, identifying waste and improvement opportunities','VSM maps every step from raw material to finished product, exposing non-value-added time/waste so teams can target specific improvement areas.',3),
('44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444','advanced','multiple_choice','A Unit Production System (UPS) moves garment components between operators using:','["Manual bundle carrying only","An overhead conveyor/hanger system that automatically transports individual pieces to each workstation","Fabric rolls only","Only manual trolleys"]','An overhead conveyor/hanger system that automatically transports individual pieces to each workstation','UPS hangs individual garment parts on carriers that travel automatically along an overhead track, reducing handling time and work-in-process versus bundle systems.',3),
('44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444','advanced','multiple_choice','The traditional bundle system in sewing lines organizes work by:','["Producing one garment fully at a single workstation","Grouping cut pieces into bundles of a fixed quantity that move together from operation to operation","Randomly distributing pieces with no grouping","Assigning one operator per garment style permanently"]','Grouping cut pieces into bundles of a fixed quantity that move together from operation to operation','Bundles (e.g. 20-30 pieces) travel as a batch through sequential operations, a traditional and still widely used method despite higher WIP than UPS.',3),
('44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444','advanced','multiple_choice','A Time and Action Calendar (TNA) in merchandising primarily tracks:','["Only shipping cost","The critical milestones and deadlines (sampling, fabric booking, cutting, sewing, shipment) required to deliver an order on time","Operator attendance only","Fabric dye recipes"]','The critical milestones and deadlines (sampling, fabric booking, cutting, sewing, shipment) required to deliver an order on time','TNA plans backward from the shipment date, mapping every critical process step with target dates so delays are caught and managed proactively.',3),
('44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444','advanced','multiple_choice','GSD (General Sewing Data) is a predetermined time system used mainly to:','["Design new fashion trends","Estimate SMV for sewing operations by breaking them into standardized micro-motions with known time values, without full stopwatch study","Test fabric strength","Calculate dye recipes"]','Estimate SMV for sewing operations by breaking them into standardized micro-motions with known time values, without full stopwatch study','GSD (like MTM) assigns predetermined time values to basic motions (reach, grasp, sew) so SMV can be synthesized quickly and consistently before production starts.',3),
('44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444','advanced','multiple_choice','A proto sample in the garment development process is primarily made to:','["Confirm final bulk fabric color only","Give the buyer a first physical interpretation of the design/tech pack for initial style approval","Ship to the retail store","Test washing machine settings"]','Give the buyer a first physical interpretation of the design/tech pack for initial style approval','The proto (or first) sample translates the design/tech pack into a real garment for the buyer''s initial review, before fit or pre-production refinements.',3),
('44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444','advanced','multiple_choice','A fit sample in the sampling sequence is specifically made to:','["Test only fabric colorfastness","Confirm garment fit and proportion on a body form or fit model before further development","Confirm packaging design","Replace the need for a proto sample"]','Confirm garment fit and proportion on a body form or fit model before further development','Fit samples check measurements, silhouette, and comfort on a form/model, often going through multiple rounds until fit is approved.',3),
('44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444','advanced','multiple_choice','A PP (Pre-Production) sample is made using:','["Any available substitute fabric and trims","The actual bulk fabric, trims, and approved construction, serving as the final reference before mass production starts","Only paper patterns","Digital renderings only"]','The actual bulk fabric, trims, and approved construction, serving as the final reference before mass production starts','PP samples use the real bulk materials and finalized construction/wash, acting as the master reference approved by the buyer before cutting bulk fabric.',3),
('44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444','advanced','multiple_choice','A size set sample is produced to verify:','["Only the sample (base) size fit","That grading across the full size range maintains correct proportion and fit at each size","Fabric shrinkage only","Packing carton dimensions"]','That grading across the full size range maintains correct proportion and fit at each size','Size sets (one garment per size in the range) confirm the pattern grading rules produce consistent, well-proportioned fit across all sizes, not just the sample size.',3),
('44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444','advanced','multiple_choice','A shipment (top-of-production/TOP) sample is pulled to:','["Replace the need for final inspection","Represent actual bulk production quality just before or at the start of shipment, for buyer final approval","Test only zipper function","Confirm fabric dye lot before weaving"]','Represent actual bulk production quality just before or at the start of shipment, for buyer final approval','TOP samples, drawn from actual running production, give the buyer a final quality/consistency check against approved standards before goods ship.',3),
('44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444','advanced','multiple_choice','Work study (method study + time study) in apparel production aims to:','["Increase fabric wastage","Systematically analyze and improve how work is done, then set standard times for the improved method","Only measure worker attendance","Replace all sewing machines with automation"]','Systematically analyze and improve how work is done, then set standard times for the improved method','Method study finds the most efficient way to perform a task; time study then measures and sets the standard time (SMV) for that improved method.',3),
('44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444','advanced','multiple_choice','Factory capacity planning primarily uses which core inputs?','["Only fabric color availability","Machine/line count, working hours, target efficiency, and SMV of the styles to be produced","Only buyer preferences","Only worker attendance records"]','Machine/line count, working hours, target efficiency, and SMV of the styles to be produced','Capacity (pieces/day or minutes available) is calculated from available machine-minutes and expected efficiency, then divided by style SMV to estimate output.',3),
('44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444','advanced','multiple_choice','Buyer social compliance audits (e.g. BSCI, WRAP) primarily assess:','["Fabric quality only","Labor rights, working conditions, health & safety, and ethical business practices in the factory","Machine brand preferences","Only export documentation"]','Labor rights, working conditions, health & safety, and ethical business practices in the factory','These audits verify factories meet standards on wages, working hours, worker safety, and ethical treatment as a condition of doing business with the buyer.',3),
('44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444','advanced','multiple_choice','A "critical path" in apparel production planning refers to:','["Any random task in the process","The sequence of dependent tasks that determines the minimum possible time to complete the order; delay on this path delays the whole shipment","The shortest task in the process","The cheapest process step"]','The sequence of dependent tasks that determines the minimum possible time to complete the order; delay on this path delays the whole shipment','Since critical path tasks have zero slack, any delay directly pushes back the final shipment date, making them the top priority to monitor.',3),
('44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444','advanced','multiple_choice','Work-in-process (WIP) inventory reduction is a key lean manufacturing goal mainly because excess WIP:','["Improves cash flow","Ties up capital, hides production problems, and increases lead time without adding value","Has no impact on lead time","Always improves quality"]','Ties up capital, hides production problems, and increases lead time without adding value','Excess WIP sits idle between processes, tying up working capital and masking upstream bottlenecks, while extending overall lead time.',3),
('44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444','advanced','multiple_choice','A modular/team-based sewing system (e.g. Toyota Sewing System) differs from a straight-line system mainly by:','["Having only one operator handle the entire garment","Small, flexible, cross-trained teams working standing/moving through single-piece flow rather than a fixed conveyor line","Removing all quality checks","Using only automated machines"]','Small, flexible, cross-trained teams working standing/moving through single-piece flow rather than a fixed conveyor line','Modular systems use small multi-skilled teams practicing single-piece flow, offering more flexibility, faster response to changes, and reduced WIP than rigid conveyor lines.',3),
('44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444','advanced','multiple_choice','Fabric consumption per garment (used in costing) is primarily calculated from:','["Only fabric price per meter","The marker efficiency (fabric utilization from pattern layout) applied to the garment''s pattern area requirement","Only garment SMV","Only order quantity"]','The marker efficiency (fabric utilization from pattern layout) applied to the garment''s pattern area requirement','Fabric consumption depends on how efficiently pattern pieces are laid out (marker efficiency) relative to the total area those pieces require, directly affecting cost.',3),
('44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444','advanced','multiple_choice','Marker efficiency (marker utilization) is calculated as:','["Total fabric roll length divided by garment count","(Total area of pattern pieces / Total marker area) x 100","Number of size combinations on the marker","Total cutting time"]','(Total area of pattern pieces / Total marker area) x 100','Higher marker efficiency means less fabric wasted as gaps between pattern pieces, directly reducing fabric cost per garment.',3),
('44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444','advanced','multiple_choice','A production line''s "target" (daily output goal) is typically calculated using:','["Random estimation","Working minutes available x target efficiency%, divided by the style''s SMV","Only fabric availability","Worker headcount alone"]','Working minutes available x target efficiency%, divided by the style''s SMV','Target output = (total available line minutes x target efficiency) / SMV, converting available time capacity into an expected piece output goal.',3),
('44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444','advanced','multiple_choice','Andon systems on a production floor are used to:','["Automatically cut fabric","Visually/audibly signal a production problem immediately so it can be addressed quickly, minimizing downtime impact","Replace all supervisors","Track buyer orders only"]','Visually/audibly signal a production problem immediately so it can be addressed quickly, minimizing downtime impact','An Andon light/signal alerts supervisors the instant a problem (machine down, quality issue, material shortage) occurs, enabling fast response before it escalates.',3),
('44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444','advanced','multiple_choice','Overhead cost allocation in garment costing typically includes:','["Only fabric cost","Factory rent, utilities, administrative salaries, and depreciation spread across production volume","Only direct labor wages","Only trims cost"]','Factory rent, utilities, administrative salaries, and depreciation spread across production volume','Overhead covers indirect costs not tied to a specific garment (rent, utilities, admin, depreciation), allocated per unit based on production volume/capacity.',3),
('44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444','advanced','multiple_choice','A "critical operation" in method study analysis is one identified as:','["Least important to product quality","Having a high impact on quality, SMV, or bottleneck risk, warranting closer engineering/training focus","Always the first operation in sequence","Only performed by supervisors"]','Having a high impact on quality, SMV, or bottleneck risk, warranting closer engineering/training focus','Industrial engineers flag operations that most affect line balance, quality risk, or cycle time for targeted method improvement and operator training.',3),
('44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444','advanced','multiple_choice','Sourcing decisions (choosing fabric/trim suppliers) in apparel merchandising balance primarily:','["Only lowest price regardless of quality/lead time","Cost, quality, lead time, and reliability/compliance of the supplier","Only supplier location","Only supplier size"]','Cost, quality, lead time, and reliability/compliance of the supplier','Effective sourcing weighs multiple factors together since the cheapest option may compromise quality, delivery timing, or compliance requirements.',3),
('44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444','advanced','multiple_choice','Absenteeism and labor turnover are tracked by apparel factory management mainly because they directly impact:','["Fabric dye shade","Line efficiency, training cost, and delivery reliability","Only marketing budget","Fiber content of garments"]','Line efficiency, training cost, and delivery reliability','Unplanned absence or turnover disrupts trained line balance, forces retraining, and risks missing shipment deadlines, so both are closely monitored KPIs.',3),
('44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444','advanced','multiple_choice','A "pull system" in lean production, as opposed to a "push system," triggers production based on:','["Forecasted demand pushed from planning regardless of downstream need","Actual downstream demand/consumption signals, producing only what is needed when needed","Random scheduling","Only supervisor discretion"]','Actual downstream demand/consumption signals, producing only what is needed when needed','Pull systems (like Kanban) only replenish/produce in response to real consumption downstream, reducing overproduction and excess inventory versus forecast-driven push systems.',3),
('44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444','advanced','multiple_choice','Root cause analysis (e.g. the "5 Whys" technique) is applied in apparel quality/production management to:','["Immediately blame an individual worker","Systematically trace a problem back to its underlying cause rather than just addressing the visible symptom","Skip corrective action entirely","Only apply to fabric defects"]','Systematically trace a problem back to its underlying cause rather than just addressing the visible symptom','Repeatedly asking "why" traces a defect or delay back through contributing causes to the true root cause, enabling a permanent fix rather than a temporary patch.',3),
('44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444','advanced','multiple_choice','A "learning curve" in a new production line is used by planners to:','["Assume 100% efficiency from day one","Predict how efficiency will gradually rise from a lower starting point as operators gain proficiency on a new style","Reduce SMV artificially","Eliminate the need for training"]','Predict how efficiency will gradually rise from a lower starting point as operators gain proficiency on a new style','New styles/lines typically start below target efficiency; learning curve models forecast the realistic ramp-up so delivery planning isn''t based on unrealistic day-one output.',3);
INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points) VALUES
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2','advanced','multiple_choice','The fundamental structural unit of any knitted fabric is the:','["Yarn twist","Interlooped stitch (loop)","Warp thread","Weft float"]','Interlooped stitch (loop)','Knitting builds fabric by forming interconnected loops of yarn, unlike weaving''s interlacing of two perpendicular yarn sets.',3),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2','advanced','multiple_choice','A "course" in knitted fabric refers to:','["A vertical column of loops","A horizontal row of loops running across the fabric width","The yarn feed direction only","A type of knitting defect"]','A horizontal row of loops running across the fabric width','Courses run crosswise (analogous to weft/picks in weaving), each representing one knitting cycle''s row of loops.',3),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2','advanced','multiple_choice','A "wale" in knitted fabric refers to:','["A horizontal row of loops","A vertical column of loops running along the fabric length","A finishing defect","A dye pattern"]','A vertical column of loops running along the fabric length','Wales run lengthwise down the fabric (analogous to warp in weaving), formed by successive loops interlooping vertically.',3),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2','advanced','multiple_choice','Weft knitting differs fundamentally from warp knitting in that weft knitting:','["Uses one continuous yarn fed crosswise, forming loops across a course sequentially","Requires a separate yarn for every single wale simultaneously","Cannot produce circular fabric","Only works with filament yarn"]','Uses one continuous yarn fed crosswise, forming loops across a course sequentially','A single (or few) yarn feeds horizontally, forming one loop after another across a course -- this is why a weft-knit fabric can unravel/run if a yarn breaks.',3),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2','advanced','multiple_choice','Warp knitting differs from weft knitting in that warp knitting:','["Uses one yarn for the whole fabric","Feeds a separate yarn (from a warp beam) to each needle, with all loops formed simultaneously along the fabric length","Cannot be run on a machine, only by hand","Produces only single jersey fabric"]','Feeds a separate yarn (from a warp beam) to each needle, with all loops formed simultaneously along the fabric length','Each needle in warp knitting is fed its own warp yarn from a beam, forming all loops across the width simultaneously each cycle, giving greater run-resistance.',3),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2','advanced','multiple_choice','Machine gauge in knitting refers to:','["Fabric GSM","The number of needles per inch (or per 1.5 inches) across the needle bed/cylinder","Yarn twist multiplier","Loom picks per inch"]','The number of needles per inch (or per 1.5 inches) across the needle bed/cylinder','Higher gauge means more, finer needles packed per inch, allowing finer yarn and denser, finer-gauge fabric to be knitted.',3),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2','advanced','multiple_choice','Single jersey (plain knit) fabric structure is characterized by:','["Identical face and back appearance","A distinctly different technical face (smooth V-shape loops) and technical back (semi-circular loop appearance)","Being produced only on flat knitting machines","No possible curling tendency"]','A distinctly different technical face (smooth V-shape loops) and technical back (semi-circular loop appearance)','Single jersey has all loops drawn to one side, giving a smooth face and a more textured back, and is prone to edge curling due to unbalanced loop forces.',3),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2','advanced','multiple_choice','Rib knit structure is created by:','["Using only one set of needles","Alternating face and back loops (using two opposing needle beds/cylinders and dials) in vertical columns","Warp knitting exclusively","Removing every alternate course"]','Alternating face and back loops (using two opposing needle beds/cylinders and dials) in vertical columns','Rib knitting alternates knit and purl wales using two needle sets, producing identical, elastic, and curl-resistant faces on both sides.',3),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2','advanced','multiple_choice','Interlock knit fabric is essentially formed by:','["A single set of needles only","Two rib structures knitted and combined so the loops interlock, hiding the technical back and giving a smooth, thick fabric","Warp beams feeding one needle each","No needle beds at all"]','Two rib structures knitted and combined so the loops interlock, hiding the technical back and giving a smooth, thick fabric','Interlock combines two 1x1 rib fabrics locked together, giving a fabric that looks identical (smooth, jersey-like) on both faces and has excellent dimensional stability.',3),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2','advanced','multiple_choice','Purl knit structure is notable for:','["Having identical loop faces to single jersey","Alternating face and back loops within the same wale, giving stretch in both directions and a textured, reversible appearance","Being impossible to produce on circular machines","Requiring no needle latching mechanism"]','Alternating face and back loops within the same wale, giving stretch in both directions and a textured, reversible appearance','Purl fabric alternates knit and purl stitches along the same wale (needing double-headed needles), giving high stretch and a distinctive ribbed-across texture.',3),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2','advanced','multiple_choice','Stitch length in knitting directly determines:','["Fabric color depth","Loop size, and therefore fabric GSM, width, and dimensional properties","Machine gauge permanently","Dye fixation rate"]','Loop size, and therefore fabric GSM, width, and dimensional properties','Longer stitch length means bigger, looser loops (lighter, wider fabric); shorter stitch length gives tighter, denser, heavier fabric -- a primary knitting control variable.',3),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2','advanced','multiple_choice','Tightness factor (K-value) in knitting expresses the relationship between:','["Yarn color and fabric shade","Yarn linear density (tex) and stitch length, indicating how tightly/loosely a fabric is constructed","Machine RPM and gauge only","Dye bath temperature and time"]','Yarn linear density (tex) and stitch length, indicating how tightly/loosely a fabric is constructed','Tightness factor = sqrt(tex) / stitch length, allowing comparison of relative fabric tightness across different yarn counts and stitch lengths.',3),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2','advanced','multiple_choice','A latch needle, used in most modern weft knitting machines, forms loops using:','["A fixed hook with no moving parts","A hinged latch that opens/closes to control old loop clearing versus new loop formation","Only a sliding tongue with no latch","Manual hand manipulation only"]','A hinged latch that opens/closes to control old loop clearing versus new loop formation','As the needle rises and falls, the latch swings open to let the old loop pass over, then closes to protect the hook while a new loop is drawn -- self-acting, unlike bearded needles.',3),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2','advanced','multiple_choice','A bearded needle, used in warp knitting and some traditional machines, requires:','["No auxiliary part","A separate presser to close the flexible beard hook after loop formation","Two latches instead of one","No yarn feed system"]','A separate presser to close the flexible beard hook after loop formation','Unlike self-closing latch needles, a bearded needle''s flexible hook (beard) must be mechanically pressed closed by a presser bar to clear the old loop safely.',3),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2','advanced','multiple_choice','A sinker in a knitting machine functions to:','["Insert twist into the yarn","Hold down the old loop and help form/control the new loop as the needle rises and falls","Dye the fabric during knitting","Cut the yarn between courses"]','Hold down the old loop and help form/control the new loop as the needle rises and falls','Sinkers sit between needles, holding fabric down (preventing it from rising with the needle) so the new loop can form and knock over the old loop cleanly.',3),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2','advanced','multiple_choice','Spirality (fabric twisting diagonally) is a common single jersey defect caused mainly by:','["Correct, balanced yarn twist","Residual twist liveliness in the yarn causing loops to skew, especially in high-twist singles yarn","Excessive fabric GSM only","Correct machine gauge"]','Residual twist liveliness in the yarn causing loops to skew, especially in high-twist singles yarn','Unbalanced residual torque in singles yarn causes loops to twist slightly in one consistent direction each course, accumulating into visible fabric spirality (wale skew).',3),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2','advanced','multiple_choice','Courses per inch (CPI) and wales per inch (WPI) together determine:','["Fabric color fastness","Fabric stitch density, which relates directly to GSM and dimensional stability","Only machine brand identification","Yarn twist direction"]','Fabric stitch density, which relates directly to GSM and dimensional stability','CPI x WPI gives stitch density (loops per unit area), a core determinant of fabric weight, cover, and how the fabric will behave dimensionally after relaxation/washing.',3),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2','advanced','multiple_choice','Knit fabric GSM is generally calculated in relation to:','["Stitch length, yarn count, and stitch density together","Only machine gauge","Only fabric color","Only dye recipe"]','Stitch length, yarn count, and stitch density together','GSM in knits results from how much yarn mass is packed into loops per unit area, governed jointly by yarn linear density, loop size (stitch length), and density.',3),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2','advanced','multiple_choice','A "dropped stitch" defect appears in knitted fabric as:','["A color variation","A run or hole where a loop failed to form/hold and unravelled along the wale","A width variation only","A pilling defect"]','A run or hole where a loop failed to form/hold and unravelled along the wale','If a needle fails to properly clear or catch yarn, that loop drops and often "runs" (ladders) down the wale, creating a visible vertical hole line.',3),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2','advanced','multiple_choice','A "needle line" defect (a fine vertical line repeating at machine gauge intervals) usually indicates:','["Correct machine timing","A damaged, bent, or worn needle consistently producing an irregular loop at that needle position","Excess dye penetration","Fiber blend variation"]','A damaged, bent, or worn needle consistently producing an irregular loop at that needle position','A single faulty needle repeats its defect every revolution, producing a continuous fine line the length of the fabric roll at that specific wale position.',3),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2','advanced','multiple_choice','Knit fabric shrinkage after wet processing is typically larger than woven fabric shrinkage mainly because:','["Knit loops are more mobile/flexible and relax more freely once tension applied during knitting/finishing is released","Knitted fiber is chemically different","Knit fabric contains no fiber shrinkage potential","Woven fabric never shrinks"]','Knit loops are more mobile/flexible and relax more freely once tension applied during knitting/finishing is released','The looped structure allows greater dimensional movement than the tightly interlaced woven structure, so knits typically show higher relaxation shrinkage.',3),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2','advanced','multiple_choice','Compacting (compressive shrinkage finishing) for knit fabric aims to:','["Increase final shrinkage potential","Pre-shrink/stabilize the fabric mechanically so garment shrinkage after washing stays within specification","Add color to the fabric","Remove all elasticity"]','Pre-shrink/stabilize the fabric mechanically so garment shrinkage after washing stays within specification','Similar in principle to sanforizing for wovens, compacting mechanically pre-shrinks knit fabric length-wise to minimize further shrinkage in the finished garment.',3),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2','advanced','multiple_choice','A circular knitting machine, compared to a flat (V-bed) machine, is generally better suited for:','["Producing shaped panels with fashioning (increases/decreases)","High-speed production of tubular fabric in continuous rolls for garment cutting","Sweater/intarsia patterned knitting exclusively","Zero fabric waste garment production"]','High-speed production of tubular fabric in continuous rolls for garment cutting','Circular machines rotate continuously at high speed to produce long tubular fabric efficiently, ideal for T-shirt/jersey fabric later cut-and-sewn.',3),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2','advanced','multiple_choice','A flat knitting machine''s key advantage over circular machines is its ability to:','["Produce fabric faster in bulk","Fashion (shape) panels directly by increasing/decreasing needles in action, enabling seamless or fully-fashioned garment parts","Knit only single jersey","Eliminate the need for linking/finishing"]','Fashion (shape) panels directly by increasing/decreasing needles in action, enabling seamless or fully-fashioned garment parts','Flat machines can bring needles in/out of action to shape panel edges (fully-fashioning), producing pre-shaped sweater panels or seamless garments without cutting.',3),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2','advanced','multiple_choice','Terry (loop-pile) knit fabric, used for towels/sweatshirt backing, is created by:','["A special sinker mechanism that forms extra-long loops on the technical back using a plated extra yarn","Adding chemical foam backing","Weaving instead of knitting","Only using rib structure"]','A special sinker mechanism that forms extra-long loops on the technical back using a plated extra yarn','A terry sinker holds an additional (pile) yarn to form longer loops than the ground yarn''s loops, creating the characteristic pile surface once these are optionally brushed for fleece.',3),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2','advanced','multiple_choice','Fleece fabric is typically produced by:','["Knitting a terry-back structure and then brushing/napping the pile loops into a raised fiber surface","Weaving only","Chemical bonding of two fabrics","Applying only a printed pattern"]','Knitting a terry-back structure and then brushing/napping the pile loops into a raised fiber surface','A terry-loop back is mechanically brushed (napped), breaking the loops into a soft, raised fiber surface that gives fleece its characteristic warmth and hand.',3),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2','advanced','multiple_choice','Jacquard knitting mechanisms enable:','["Only solid-color single jersey","Individual needle selection to create multi-color or structural patterns within the knitted fabric","Fabric printing after knitting only","Elimination of needle selection entirely"]','Individual needle selection to create multi-color or structural patterns within the knitted fabric','Jacquard selection (mechanical or electronic) controls each needle independently per course, enabling complex multi-color/patterned knit designs.',3),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2','advanced','multiple_choice','Plating in knitting refers to:','["Adding a metallic finish","Feeding two yarns simultaneously to the same needle so one consistently appears on the face and the other on the back","Removing yarn from the needle","A defect where two loops overlap accidentally"]','Feeding two yarns simultaneously to the same needle so one consistently appears on the face and the other on the back','Plating deliberately feeds two yarns together with controlled relative tension/angle so one stays on the technical face and the other on the back, useful for moisture management or color effects.',3),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2','advanced','multiple_choice','Seamless knitting technology produces a garment by:','["Cutting and sewing flat knit panels together as usual","Knitting the entire 3D garment shape (or very close to it) directly on the machine, minimizing cutting and sewing operations","Only knitting flat 2D rectangles","Requiring extensive embroidery afterward"]','Knitting the entire 3D garment shape (or very close to it) directly on the machine, minimizing cutting and sewing operations','Seamless circular machines shape the garment body/sleeves directly during knitting, drastically reducing cut-and-sew labor and side seams.',3),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2','advanced','multiple_choice','A "barre" defect (faint crosswise streaks) in knitted fabric most commonly results from:','["Correct, uniform yarn count across all feeders","Slight yarn count, twist, or dye-uptake variation between different yarn packages/feeders on a multi-feeder circular machine","Only fabric width variation","Machine gauge being too coarse"]','Slight yarn count, twist, or dye-uptake variation between different yarn packages/feeders on a multi-feeder circular machine','With many feeders knitting simultaneously, even small differences between yarn lots/packages create repeating faint streaks (barre) across the fabric.',3),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2','advanced','multiple_choice','Yarn tension consistency across all feeders on a multi-feeder circular knitting machine is critical because tension variation directly causes:','["Improved fabric evenness","Uneven stitch length between feeders, leading to visible barre or dimensional inconsistency","No measurable fabric effect","Only color variation"]','Uneven stitch length between feeders, leading to visible barre or dimensional inconsistency','Since stitch length depends on how much yarn is drawn into each loop, tension differences between feeders directly cause loop-size (and therefore appearance/GSM) variation.',3),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2','advanced','multiple_choice','Course length monitoring/control systems on modern knitting machines are used to:','["Track fabric color only","Continuously measure yarn feed length per course and adjust to maintain consistent stitch length/GSM in real time","Control dyeing temperature","Replace needle selection systems"]','Continuously measure yarn feed length per course and adjust to maintain consistent stitch length/GSM in real time','Electronic course-length feeders precisely regulate yarn delivery per revolution, holding stitch length (and thus GSM) tightly consistent despite tension fluctuations.',3),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2','advanced','multiple_choice','Pique knit (a variant used for polo shirts) is structurally a:','["Plain single jersey with no variation","Derivative of single jersey incorporating tuck stitches to create a textured surface pattern","Warp knit structure exclusively","Purl-only structure"]','Derivative of single jersey incorporating tuck stitches to create a textured surface pattern','Pique uses periodic tuck stitches within a single-jersey base to create the characteristic small honeycomb-like surface texture.',3),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2','advanced','multiple_choice','A "tuck stitch" in knitting occurs when a needle:','["Forms a new loop and clears the old loop normally","Holds the old loop and receives an additional new loop without clearing, creating a slightly raised, textured effect","Skips forming any loop","Breaks the yarn intentionally"]','Holds the old loop and receives an additional new loop without clearing, creating a slightly raised, textured effect','In a tuck stitch, the needle doesn''t fully clear the prior loop before taking new yarn, accumulating loops that create surface texture and slightly wider fabric.',3),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2','advanced','multiple_choice','A "float" (miss) stitch in knitting occurs when a needle:','["Forms a normal loop","Stays out of action entirely for that course, so the yarn floats behind/in front without forming a loop at that position","Ties a permanent knot","Doubles the loop size"]','Stays out of action entirely for that course, so the yarn floats behind/in front without forming a loop at that position','Missed/float needles don''t engage the feeder yarn at all that course, leaving a float thread that spans across, useful for reducing fabric width/weight in patterned areas.',3),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2','advanced','multiple_choice','Compared to woven fabric, knitted fabric generally offers superior:','["Dimensional stability with zero stretch","Stretch/elasticity and drape, due to the mobile, interlooped loop structure","Tear resistance always","Print sharpness always"]','Stretch/elasticity and drape, due to the mobile, interlooped loop structure','Knit loops can deform and recover more freely than the fixed interlacing of woven yarns, giving knits their characteristic stretch and soft drape.',3),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2','advanced','multiple_choice','Cylinder and dial timing (synchronization) on a rib/interlock circular machine is critical because incorrect timing causes:','["Improved fabric quality","Needle collisions or malformed loops where cylinder and dial needles interact","Faster machine speed with no downside","Only a color shift"]','Needle collisions or malformed loops where cylinder and dial needles interact','Precise timing ensures cylinder and dial needles move in/out at the correct relative moments; mismatched timing can cause needle breakage or badly formed interlocking loops.',3);
INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points) VALUES
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc','advanced','multiple_choice','ISO 4915 stitch class 300 (lockstitch) is formed by:','["A single thread looping through itself","Two threads (needle and bobbin) interlocking within the fabric layers","Overedge thread wrapping the fabric edge only","No thread at all, using adhesive"]','Two threads (needle and bobbin) interlocking within the fabric layers','Lockstitch forms when the needle thread loop is caught by a rotating hook and interlocked with the bobbin thread, locking within the fabric thickness.',3),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc','advanced','multiple_choice','A key disadvantage of lockstitch (class 300) compared to chainstitch is that lockstitch:','["Uses more thread","Is more prone to unraveling if a seam breaks, and has less inherent stretch, since it does not chain-release easily but also cannot be pulled out neatly","Cannot be used on wovens","Requires no bobbin"]','Is more prone to unraveling if a seam breaks, and has less inherent stretch, since it does not chain-release easily but also cannot be pulled out neatly','Lockstitch has minimal elasticity (poor for stretch fabrics) and, unlike chainstitch, a broken lockstitch seam does not run out cleanly for easy unpicking, though it also resists unraveling less predictably in fabric with movement.',3),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc','advanced','multiple_choice','ISO 4915 stitch class 400 (chainstitch) is characterized by:','["Using only one thread with no looper","Two or more threads interlooping (needle thread looped by a looper thread) to form a chain-like structure on the underside","Using no bobbin and no looper","Only being used for buttonholes"]','Two or more threads interlooping (needle thread looped by a looper thread) to form a chain-like structure on the underside','Chainstitch uses a looper (not a rotating hook) to interloop threads, forming a visible chain pattern underneath, offering more elasticity and easy unpicking.',3),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc','advanced','multiple_choice','ISO 4915 stitch class 500 (overedge/overlock) stitches are primarily used to:','["Form the main seam of jeans","Wrap and finish raw fabric edges to prevent fraying, often while simultaneously joining two fabric plies","Attach buttons only","Create buttonholes"]','Wrap and finish raw fabric edges to prevent fraying, often while simultaneously joining two fabric plies','Overlock/overedge stitching wraps thread around the raw fabric edge, both securing the seam and finishing the edge against unraveling in one pass.',3),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc','advanced','multiple_choice','ISO 4915 stitch class 600 (coverstitch) is most commonly used for:','["Buttonholes","Hemming knit garments with a flat, stretchable, professional finish (e.g. T-shirt hems)","Attaching zippers","Overedging raw fabric only"]','Hemming knit garments with a flat, stretchable, professional finish (e.g. T-shirt hems)','Coverstitch machines lay parallel rows of stitching on top with a covering thread underneath, giving knit hems both stretch and a clean, durable finish.',3),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc','advanced','multiple_choice','SPI (Stitches Per Inch) in garment sewing is a specification that controls:','["Fabric GSM","Seam strength, elasticity, and appearance by setting how many stitches are formed per inch of seam","Thread color only","Needle size only"]','Seam strength, elasticity, and appearance by setting how many stitches are formed per inch of seam','Higher SPI generally increases seam strength/durability but reduces stretch and can weaken fabric via more needle perforations; SPI is specified per style/fabric.',3),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc','advanced','multiple_choice','Seam puckering, a common sewing defect, is most often caused by:','["Correct thread tension only","Thread tension imbalance, feed dog/presser foot mismatch, or fabric-thread shrinkage differential","Perfect stitch formation","Using the correct needle size"]','Thread tension imbalance, feed dog/presser foot mismatch, or fabric-thread shrinkage differential','Puckering arises when top/bottom tension is unbalanced, differential feeding pushes plies unevenly, or thread and fabric shrink at different rates after sewing.',3),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc','advanced','multiple_choice','Needle size selection for sewing is primarily based on:','["Machine brand only","Fabric weight/type and thread size, to avoid needle holes being too large or thread breakage from too-small a needle","Thread color","Season of production"]','Fabric weight/type and thread size, to avoid needle holes being too large or thread breakage from too-small a needle','A needle too coarse for light fabric leaves visible holes/damage; one too fine for heavy fabric or thick thread risks breakage/skipped stitches -- matching is essential.',3),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc','advanced','multiple_choice','A ballpoint needle is specifically designed for sewing:','["Woven fabrics only","Knitted fabrics, since its rounded tip pushes yarn fibers aside rather than piercing/cutting them","Leather exclusively","Denim exclusively"]','Knitted fabrics, since its rounded tip pushes yarn fibers aside rather than piercing/cutting them','A ballpoint tip slides between knit loops instead of piercing them, reducing the risk of damaged yarns, runs, or holes in knitted fabric.',3),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc','advanced','multiple_choice','Fusible interlining is bonded to garment components (e.g. collars, cuffs) using:','["Only mechanical stitching","Heat and pressure that activate an adhesive resin coating on the interlining, bonding it to the face fabric","Only chemical soaking","Ultrasonic welding exclusively"]','Heat and pressure that activate an adhesive resin coating on the interlining, bonding it to the face fabric','A fusing press applies controlled heat, pressure, and dwell time to melt the interlining''s resin coating, bonding it permanently to the outer fabric layer.',3),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc','advanced','multiple_choice','Delamination (fusing failure) between interlining and face fabric is often caused by:','["Correct fusing temperature, pressure, and time","Incorrect fusing parameters (too low temperature/pressure/time) or incompatible fabric-interlining combination","Only fiber content of the interlining","Machine color settings"]','Incorrect fusing parameters (too low temperature/pressure/time) or incompatible fabric-interlining combination','Fusing bond strength depends on the correct combination of heat, pressure, and dwell time matched to both materials; getting this wrong causes bubbling or separation after washing.',3),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc','advanced','multiple_choice','Pattern grading is the process of:','["Creating the base sample size pattern","Proportionally increasing/decreasing a base pattern to produce all other sizes in the size range","Marking fabric flaws","Cutting fabric plies"]','Proportionally increasing/decreasing a base pattern to produce all other sizes in the size range','Using defined grade rules, each pattern piece is systematically resized from the base (sample) size to generate a complete, consistent size range.',3),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc','advanced','multiple_choice','CAD (Computer-Aided Design) systems in pattern making primarily allow:','["Only digital fabric printing","Digital pattern creation, grading, and marker making with far greater speed/accuracy than manual methods","Replacing all sewing machines","Eliminating fabric consumption entirely"]','Digital pattern creation, grading, and marker making with far greater speed/accuracy than manual methods','CAD software drafts, grades, and arranges (nests) pattern pieces digitally, significantly speeding up development and improving marker efficiency versus manual drafting.',3),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc','advanced','multiple_choice','Fabric spreading in the cutting room refers to:','["Cutting individual garment pieces","Layering multiple plies of fabric on the cutting table to the required length/height before cutting","Ironing fabric flat","Dyeing fabric before cutting"]','Layering multiple plies of fabric on the cutting table to the required length/height before cutting','Spreading stacks many fabric plies (a "lay") on the table so a single marker cut produces many identical garment pieces at once, boosting cutting efficiency.',3),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc','advanced','multiple_choice','Straight knife cutting machines, compared to band knife machines, are primarily used for:','["Only pre-cut small components","Cutting through the whole fabric lay directly on the spreading table, following marker lines freehand or CNC-guided","Only cutting single-ply fabric","Only cutting leather"]','Cutting through the whole fabric lay directly on the spreading table, following marker lines freehand or CNC-guided','Straight knife cutters move through the entire stacked fabric lay on the table itself, whereas band knife cutting typically re-cuts smaller stacks or components at a stationary fixed blade.',3),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc','advanced','multiple_choice','Die cutting (using steel-rule dies/clicker press) is best suited for:','["Long fabric runs of simple large panels","High-precision cutting of small, repeated components (e.g. collars, cuffs, pocket flaps) in bulk","Cutting a single garment prototype only","Only cutting knitted fabric"]','High-precision cutting of small, repeated components (e.g. collars, cuffs, pocket flaps) in bulk','Steel-rule dies stamp out identical small components with high precision and speed, ideal for high-volume small parts rather than large full-body panels.',3),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc','advanced','multiple_choice','Laser cutting in garment manufacturing offers the advantage of:','["Only cutting one ply at a time with rough edges","Precise, often sealed/fused edges (useful for synthetics) with no mechanical blade wear","Being unusable for intricate designs","Requiring no digital pattern file"]','Precise, often sealed/fused edges (useful for synthetics) with no mechanical blade wear','A laser beam vaporizes fabric along the digital cut path, giving highly precise cuts and, for synthetic fiber, a clean fused edge that resists fraying.',3),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc','advanced','multiple_choice','A superimposed seam (SS-class per ISO 4916) is formed by:','["Placing two fabric edges together, edge to edge, and joining with a single stitch line","Overlapping two fabric plies (one on top of the other) and sewing through both layers, the most common basic seam type","Folding fabric edges under before joining","Using no stitching at all"]','Overlapping two fabric plies (one on top of the other) and sewing through both layers, the most common basic seam type','Superimposed seams simply lay one ply over another and stitch through both -- the fundamental, most widely used seam construction in garment sewing.',3),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc','advanced','multiple_choice','A lapped seam (LS-class) construction is typically used for:','["Only the most delicate, sheer fabrics","Flat, non-bulky joins such as those found in some jeans/workwear where one edge overlaps another and is stitched flat","Only stretch swimwear","Only invisible zippers"]','Flat, non-bulky joins such as those found in some jeans/workwear where one edge overlaps another and is stitched flat','Lapped seams overlap one fabric edge onto another (rather than aligning edges together) and stitch through both, producing a flat, durable seam common in heavier garments.',3),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc','advanced','multiple_choice','A bound seam (BS-class) finishes a raw fabric edge by:','["Leaving it completely unfinished","Enclosing the raw edge within a folded strip of binding fabric stitched around it","Cutting it with pinking shears only","Fusing interlining over it"]','Enclosing the raw edge within a folded strip of binding fabric stitched around it','Binding wraps a folded fabric strip around the raw edge and stitches through all layers, both finishing the edge decoratively and preventing fraying.',3),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc','advanced','multiple_choice','A flat-felled seam, common in jeans and quality shirting, is characterized by:','["A raw, unfinished edge visible on the inside","Both raw edges folded and enclosed within the seam itself, with two visible rows of topstitching on the outside","Only one row of stitching with no folding","Requiring an overlock machine exclusively"]','Both raw edges folded and enclosed within the seam itself, with two visible rows of topstitching on the outside','Flat-felled construction folds and encloses both raw edges flat against the fabric, secured by two parallel visible stitch lines -- very durable and clean on both sides.',3),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc','advanced','multiple_choice','A french seam is favored for garments made from lightweight/sheer fabric because it:','["Adds significant bulk","Fully encloses raw edges within the seam itself using two sewing passes, leaving no visible raw edge on either side","Requires no sewing machine at all","Is only usable on heavy denim"]','Fully encloses raw edges within the seam itself using two sewing passes, leaving no visible raw edge on either side','A french seam is first sewn wrong-sides-together, then trimmed and re-sewn right-sides-together to enclose the raw edges completely -- ideal for sheer fabric where seam finish shows through.',3),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc','advanced','multiple_choice','Seam allowance refers to:','["The total garment size","The distance between the cut edge of fabric and the intended stitch line","The thread color used","The needle size specification"]','The distance between the cut edge of fabric and the intended stitch line','Seam allowance is the extra fabric margin beyond the seam line, needed to physically join fabric pieces together with a stitch and avoid the seam pulling apart.',3),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc','advanced','multiple_choice','Buttonhole stitching (typically a specialized lockstitch/zigzag class) must primarily balance:','["Only thread color matching","Durability against repeated button stress and a clean, tight edge that resists fraying","Fabric GSM only","Needle brand only"]','Durability against repeated button stress and a clean, tight edge that resists fraying','Buttonholes see repeated stress from buttoning/unbuttoning, so dense, tight stitching is needed for durability while keeping edges clean and fray-resistant.',3),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc','advanced','multiple_choice','Button attaching machines typically use which stitch approach to secure a button?','["A single straight stitch only","A repeated figure-eight or parallel lockstitch pattern through the button holes and fabric, sometimes with a thread shank","No stitching, only adhesive","Overlock stitching around the button perimeter"]','A repeated figure-eight or parallel lockstitch pattern through the button holes and fabric, sometimes with a thread shank','Multiple passes through the button holes anchor it firmly; a thread shank (extra thread wraps beneath the button) allows room for the fabric layer thickness when buttoned.',3),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc','advanced','multiple_choice','Zipper insertion methods (e.g. centered, lapped, invisible) are chosen based primarily on:','["Random operator preference only","The garment style''s design requirement for visible topstitching versus a hidden, seamless appearance","Zipper color only","Fabric GSM exclusively"]','The garment style''s design requirement for visible topstitching versus a hidden, seamless appearance','Centered/lapped zippers show visible topstitching as a design feature, while invisible zippers are inserted to disappear completely within the seam line.',3),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc','advanced','multiple_choice','Ultrasonic welding/bonding, used as a seaming alternative to stitching, works by:','["Melting fabric with a laser","Using high-frequency vibration to generate localized heat that fuses thermoplastic fabric layers together without thread","Applying only glue by hand","Using steam pressure alone"]','Using high-frequency vibration to generate localized heat that fuses thermoplastic fabric layers together without thread','Ultrasonic energy vibrates a horn against the fabric, generating frictional heat that melts and fuses thermoplastic (often synthetic) layers together, common in activewear/outerwear for seamless, waterproof seams.',3),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc','advanced','multiple_choice','Seam taping (heat-sealed tape over a stitched seam) is applied mainly to:','["Add decorative color","Make a stitched seam waterproof by sealing the needle-hole perforations","Increase seam bulk intentionally","Replace the need for stitching entirely"]','Make a stitched seam waterproof by sealing the needle-hole perforations','Even a well-sewn seam has tiny needle holes that let water through; heat-applied seam tape seals these, critical for rainwear/outerwear waterproofing.',3),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc','advanced','multiple_choice','Thread breakage during high-speed sewing is commonly caused by:','["Correct thread tension and needle size","Excessive tension, a damaged needle eye/hook, or poor thread quality/lubrication for the sewing speed used","Using compatible thread and needle","Low sewing speed"]','Excessive tension, a damaged needle eye/hook, or poor thread quality/lubrication for the sewing speed used','High sewing speeds generate heat and friction; mismatched tension, machine wear, or inadequate thread lubrication/quality increases the risk of thread snapping.',3),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc','advanced','multiple_choice','Skipped stitch defects are most often caused by:','["Correct needle-hook timing","Needle-to-hook timing misalignment, a blunt/bent needle, or incorrect needle-thread combination","Proper thread tension only","Using the correct presser foot"]','Needle-to-hook timing misalignment, a blunt/bent needle, or incorrect needle-thread combination','A skipped stitch happens when the hook fails to catch the needle thread loop at the right moment -- usually from timing drift, needle damage, or deflection.',3),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc','advanced','multiple_choice','Core-spun sewing thread (polyester core wrapped in cotton) is popular because it combines:','["Only decorative appearance","The strength/heat resistance of the polyester core with the sewability and natural look of a cotton wrap","Only low cost with no performance benefit","Complete biodegradability"]','The strength/heat resistance of the polyester core with the sewability and natural look of a cotton wrap','A polyester filament core provides strength and resistance to needle heat, while the cotton wrap improves sewing machine friction behavior and gives a natural thread appearance.',3),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc','advanced','multiple_choice','Waistband construction on trousers commonly incorporates which reinforcement element?','["No reinforcement is typically used","A stiff interlining or waistband tape/canvas to maintain shape and support belt-loop/closure stress","Extra dye application only","An extra layer of lining fabric with no interlining"]','A stiff interlining or waistband tape/canvas to maintain shape and support belt-loop/closure stress','Waistbands endure significant stress from fastening and belt use, so interlining/tape reinforcement maintains shape retention and durability over repeated wear.',3),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc','advanced','multiple_choice','Steam pressing/finishing of garments after sewing primarily aims to:','["Add permanent color","Set creases/shape, remove wrinkles, and give the garment its final, sale-ready appearance","Increase fabric strength","Replace the need for quality inspection"]','Set creases/shape, remove wrinkles, and give the garment its final, sale-ready appearance','Steam and heat relax fiber structure temporarily, allowing pressing to remove sewing wrinkles and set intended creases/shape for a polished final look.',3),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc','advanced','multiple_choice','A form/dummy finisher (steam-air finishing mannequin) is primarily used for:','["Pressing flat panels only","Simultaneously steaming and shaping a whole finished garment (e.g. shirts) on an inflated body-shaped form for a smooth, wrinkle-free result","Cutting fabric","Sewing buttonholes"]','Simultaneously steaming and shaping a whole finished garment (e.g. shirts) on an inflated body-shaped form for a smooth, wrinkle-free result','The garment is placed over an inflatable dummy that steams and shapes it in 3D, achieving a smoother, more uniform finish than flat pressing alone for shirts/jackets.',3),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc','advanced','multiple_choice','A care label attached to a finished garment is legally/commercially required mainly to communicate:','["The factory''s production capacity","Proper washing, drying, ironing, and care instructions to preserve the garment and avoid consumer damage claims","The garment''s selling price","The pattern maker''s name"]','Proper washing, drying, ironing, and care instructions to preserve the garment and avoid consumer damage claims','Care labels use standardized symbols/text to guide consumers on safe laundering/care, reducing damage from improper handling and meeting many countries'' labeling regulations.',3),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc','advanced','multiple_choice','Sewbots (fully automated robotic sewing systems) attempt to solve the traditional challenge of:','["Cutting fabric accurately","Automatically manipulating limp, flexible fabric through a full sewing sequence without manual handling, a historically difficult automation problem","Only attaching buttons","Only measuring fabric GSM"]','Automatically manipulating limp, flexible fabric through a full sewing sequence without manual handling, a historically difficult automation problem','Unlike rigid materials, limp fabric is notoriously hard to handle/position automatically; sewbots use vision systems and specialized grippers to attempt full automation of sewing operations.',3),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc','advanced','multiple_choice','3D body scanning technology in made-to-measure/mass customization garment production is used to:','["Replace fabric cutting entirely","Capture precise individual body measurements digitally, which feed directly into pattern grading/customization software","Only measure fabric shrinkage","Test garment colorfastness"]','Capture precise individual body measurements digitally, which feed directly into pattern grading/customization software','3D scanners quickly capture accurate individual measurements, enabling automated custom pattern generation for made-to-measure or mass-customized garments.',3),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc','advanced','multiple_choice','An "open seam" defect found during garment inspection refers to:','["A seam that is correctly and fully sewn","A seam where stitching has broken or was incomplete, leaving fabric plies unjoined at that point","A style with no side seams by design","A pressed crease line"]','A seam where stitching has broken or was incomplete, leaving fabric plies unjoined at that point','Open seams are a critical quality defect since the garment structure fails at that point -- either from broken thread, skipped stitches, or incomplete sewing during construction.',3);
INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points) VALUES
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0','advanced','multiple_choice','AQL (Acceptable Quality Level) in garment inspection defines:','["The exact number of defects allowed with zero tolerance","The maximum percentage/number of defective units in a sample that is still considered acceptable for the whole lot","The total order quantity","The factory''s production capacity"]','The maximum percentage/number of defective units in a sample that is still considered acceptable for the whole lot','AQL statistically balances inspection cost against risk, defining a defect threshold (e.g. AQL 2.5) within which a sampled lot is still accepted.',3),
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0','advanced','multiple_choice','ANSI/ASQ Z1.4 (formerly MIL-STD-105) sampling standard is used in garment QC to determine:','["Fabric dye recipe","The sample size to inspect and the accept/reject numbers based on lot size and chosen inspection level/AQL","Sewing thread color","Machine maintenance schedule"]','The sample size to inspect and the accept/reject numbers based on lot size and chosen inspection level/AQL','This standard provides sampling tables that translate total lot size, inspection level, and target AQL into a defined sample size and accept/reject criteria.',3),
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0','advanced','multiple_choice','A common AQL level used for garment major defects in final inspection is:','["AQL 10.0","AQL 2.5","AQL 100","There is no standard level used"]','AQL 2.5','AQL 2.5 is a widely used standard for major defects in apparel final random inspection, being stricter than the commonly paired AQL 4.0 for minor defects.',3),
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0','advanced','multiple_choice','Defects in garment QC are typically classified into which severity categories?','["Only pass or fail","Critical, major, and minor","Only visual and functional","Only fabric and sewing"]','Critical, major, and minor','This standard classification (critical = safety/legal issue, major = affects use/appearance significantly, minor = slight, cosmetic) prioritizes inspection focus and reporting.',3),
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0','advanced','multiple_choice','A "critical defect" in garment QC would typically include:','["A slightly crooked stitch line on an inner seam","A sharp needle/pin left in the garment posing a safety hazard","A minor thread end untrimmed","A slightly loose button by one thread"]','A sharp needle/pin left in the garment posing a safety hazard','Critical defects pose safety, legal, or regulatory risk (e.g. broken needle fragments, hazardous drawstrings) and typically trigger automatic lot rejection regardless of AQL math.',3),
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0','advanced','multiple_choice','The four-point fabric inspection system assigns penalty points based primarily on:','["Fabric color depth only","The length of each defect found (longer defects score more points), summed and normalized per 100 square yards","Total roll weight","Yarn count only"]','The length of each defect found (longer defects score more points), summed and normalized per 100 square yards','Defects are scored 1-4 points based on length brackets, then totaled and compared against a points-per-100-sq-yard acceptance threshold to grade the fabric roll.',3),
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0','advanced','multiple_choice','DHU (Defects per Hundred Units) is a QC metric used to:','["Measure fabric shrinkage","Track the average number of defects found per 100 garments inspected, as a running quality benchmark","Measure dye concentration","Count total machines in a factory"]','Track the average number of defects found per 100 garments inspected, as a running quality benchmark','DHU = (total defects found / total garments inspected) x 100, giving a standardized quality-trend metric comparable across lines, styles, and time periods.',3),
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0','advanced','multiple_choice','Inline (in-process) quality inspection, performed during sewing rather than only at the end, primarily aims to:','["Replace final inspection entirely","Catch and correct defects early in the process, reducing rework cost and preventing defective units from accumulating downstream","Slow down production intentionally","Only check fabric before cutting"]','Catch and correct defects early in the process, reducing rework cost and preventing defective units from accumulating downstream','Finding a defect immediately at its source (a specific operation) is far cheaper to fix than discovering the same issue after the garment is fully assembled.',3),
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0','advanced','multiple_choice','A "traffic light" quality reporting system in a sewing line typically uses color codes to indicate:','["Machine electrical status only","Real-time quality performance status (e.g. green = acceptable, red = defect rate above threshold requiring action)","Fabric dye lot number","Worker attendance status"]','Real-time quality performance status (e.g. green = acceptable, red = defect rate above threshold requiring action)','A visual traffic-light indicator lets supervisors instantly see which stations/lines are within acceptable quality limits versus needing immediate intervention.',3),
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0','advanced','multiple_choice','Statistical Process Control (SPC) charts are used in textile/garment QC primarily to:','["Track only final shipment dates","Monitor a process measurement over time, distinguishing normal variation from a significant, correctable shift out of control","Replace all physical testing","Record worker names only"]','Monitor a process measurement over time, distinguishing normal variation from a significant, correctable shift out of control','SPC charts plot measurements against statistically derived control limits, flagging when a process drifts out of its normal variation range so corrective action can be taken before defects accumulate.',3),
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0','advanced','multiple_choice','Process capability index (Cpk) in a manufacturing quality context measures:','["Total factory output","How well a process''s actual performance fits within specified tolerance limits, accounting for both variation and centering","Worker skill level only","Fabric dye recipe accuracy"]','How well a process''s actual performance fits within specified tolerance limits, accounting for both variation and centering','A higher Cpk indicates the process consistently produces output well within specification limits, considering both process spread and how centered it is on target.',3),
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0','advanced','multiple_choice','CAPA (Corrective and Preventive Action) in quality management refers to:','["Only documenting a defect with no follow-up","A structured process to fix the immediate problem (corrective) and prevent its recurrence (preventive)","A type of fabric test","A sampling plan"]','A structured process to fix the immediate problem (corrective) and prevent its recurrence (preventive)','CAPA formally separates fixing the current defect from addressing its root cause so the same issue does not recur in future production.',3),
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0','advanced','multiple_choice','Six Sigma''s DMAIC framework stands for:','["Design, Manufacture, Inspect, Approve, Control","Define, Measure, Analyze, Improve, Control","Develop, Model, Analyze, Implement, Close","Deliver, Monitor, Audit, Inspect, Confirm"]','Define, Measure, Analyze, Improve, Control','DMAIC is the structured Six Sigma methodology for systematically improving an existing process: defining the problem, measuring current performance, analyzing root causes, improving, then controlling to sustain gains.',3),
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0','advanced','multiple_choice','Calibration of testing equipment (e.g. tensile testers, GSM scales) in a QC lab is essential to ensure:','["Faster test completion only","Measurement accuracy and traceability, since uncalibrated equipment can produce misleading pass/fail results","Lower equipment cost","Reduced need for trained technicians"]','Measurement accuracy and traceability, since uncalibrated equipment can produce misleading pass/fail results','Regular calibration against certified reference standards ensures test results are accurate and trustworthy, preventing false pass/fail decisions based on drifted instruments.',3),
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0','advanced','multiple_choice','Colorfastness to perspiration testing (e.g. AATCC 15 / ISO 105-E04) evaluates dyed fabric by:','["Exposure to direct sunlight only","Exposing the sample to simulated acidic and alkaline perspiration solutions under pressure/heat, then grading color change and staining","Rubbing with a dry cloth only","Freezing the sample"]','Exposing the sample to simulated acidic and alkaline perspiration solutions under pressure/heat, then grading color change and staining','This test simulates sweat exposure (both acid and alkaline conditions) to check whether dyed garments will stain or fade from normal wear-related perspiration contact.',3),
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0','advanced','multiple_choice','Seam slippage testing evaluates:','["Fabric color fastness","How much fabric yarns pull apart/slip at a stitched seam under a specified tensile load, important for loosely woven fabrics","Fabric flame resistance","Yarn twist direction"]','How much fabric yarns pull apart/slip at a stitched seam under a specified tensile load, important for loosely woven fabrics','This test checks whether yarns at a seam will shift/gap open under normal wear stress, a particular risk for lower-density or slippery woven fabrics.',3),
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0','advanced','multiple_choice','Needle detection (metal detector scanning) of finished garments is a QC step primarily required to:','["Check garment color","Find and remove broken sewing needle fragments that may have embedded in the fabric during construction, a safety/critical defect","Test fabric shrinkage","Measure garment weight"]','Find and remove broken sewing needle fragments that may have embedded in the fabric during construction, a safety/critical defect','Broken needle tips can lodge invisibly in fabric during sewing; metal detection scanning is a mandatory safety check (especially for children''s wear) before shipment.',3),
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0','advanced','multiple_choice','Pilling grade (per ICI/Martindale visual standards) is typically reported on a scale of:','["0 to 10, higher is worse","1 to 5, where 5 means no pilling and 1 means severe pilling","A to F letter grades","Pass or fail only, no scale"]','1 to 5, where 5 means no pilling and 1 means severe pilling','After a set number of abrasion cycles, samples are visually compared to standard photographic references and graded 1 (severe) to 5 (none), similar in structure to the grey scale.',3),
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0','advanced','multiple_choice','A single sampling plan (as opposed to a double sampling plan) in AQL inspection means:','["Two separate samples are always drawn before a decision","One sample is drawn from the lot, and the accept/reject decision is made based on that single sample alone","No sample is drawn at all -- 100% inspection is required","Sample size changes randomly each time"]','One sample is drawn from the lot, and the accept/reject decision is made based on that single sample alone','A single sampling plan makes an accept/reject decision from just one sample draw, simpler than double/multiple sampling plans which may draw a second sample if the first is inconclusive.',3),
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0','advanced','multiple_choice','General Inspection Level II (as opposed to Level I or Level III) in ANSI/ASQ Z1.4 represents:','["The loosest, smallest sample size option","The standard/default inspection stringency most commonly used unless a specific reason calls for tighter or looser sampling","The strictest possible sampling, always used for critical defects","A level reserved only for raw fiber testing"]','The standard/default inspection stringency most commonly used unless a specific reason calls for tighter or looser sampling','Level II is the default general inspection level; Level I reduces sample size (less discrimination) and Level III increases it (more discrimination) as situations warrant.',3),
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0','advanced','multiple_choice','A "reduced" versus "tightened" inspection scheme in ongoing quality sampling is applied based on:','["Random daily rotation with no logic","Recent supplier/lot quality history -- consistently good performance can allow reduced sampling, while poor performance triggers tightened sampling","Only the buyer''s mood","Fabric color changes"]','Recent supplier/lot quality history -- consistently good performance can allow reduced sampling, while poor performance triggers tightened sampling','Switching rules reward consistently good quality history with lighter sampling burden, while recent failures trigger stricter (tightened) inspection until performance recovers.',3),
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0','advanced','multiple_choice','Lot rejection in an AQL-based inspection means:','["The entire order is automatically destroyed","The inspected sample failed to meet the acceptance criteria, so the buyer/QC typically requires 100% re-inspection/sorting or rework before acceptance","The factory is immediately closed","Only the sample garments are discarded with no further action"]','The inspected sample failed to meet the acceptance criteria, so the buyer/QC typically requires 100% re-inspection/sorting or rework before acceptance','A failed AQL inspection doesn''t destroy the goods -- it signals the lot doesn''t meet the statistical confidence threshold, requiring full sorting/rework before the goods can proceed.',3),
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0','advanced','multiple_choice','Random sampling in quality inspection is essential to ensure:','["Inspectors only check the best-looking garments","The sample statistically represents the true quality of the entire lot, avoiding selection bias","Faster inspection with no accuracy concern","Only front-of-carton garments are checked"]','The sample statistically represents the true quality of the entire lot, avoiding selection bias','If inspectors could choose which units to check, results could be biased (intentionally or not); true random selection is what makes the statistical AQL math valid.',3),
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0','advanced','multiple_choice','ISO 9001 certification for a textile/garment factory primarily demonstrates:','["Fabric organic content","A documented, consistently applied quality management system across the organization''s processes","Only social compliance standards","Only environmental standards"]','A documented, consistently applied quality management system across the organization''s processes','ISO 9001 certifies that a company has implemented and consistently follows a structured quality management system, not any specific product-level guarantee.',3),
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0','advanced','multiple_choice','A control chart showing points consistently trending upward (even while staying within control limits) should be interpreted as:','["Definitely acceptable with no concern","A potential early warning of a systematic process shift worth investigating, even though no single point breached the limit","Proof the process is perfectly stable","Irrelevant unless a point exceeds the limit"]','A potential early warning of a systematic process shift worth investigating, even though no single point breached the limit','SPC interpretation includes trend/run rules (not just single-point limit breaches) since a sustained directional trend often signals a developing assignable cause before it becomes a full defect.',3),
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0','advanced','multiple_choice','Endline inspection in a sewing line refers to checking garments:','["Before cutting begins","At the very end of the sewing line, immediately after assembly is complete, before finishing/packing","Only after washing","Only at the fabric mill"]','At the very end of the sewing line, immediately after assembly is complete, before finishing/packing','Endline QC catches assembly defects right as garments leave the sewing line, before they proceed to finishing/pressing/packing stages.',3),
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0','advanced','multiple_choice','A pre-shipment (final random) inspection, often conducted by a third-party auditor, is performed:','["Before any fabric is purchased","After the goods are fully finished, packed, and ready to ship, as a final independent quality checkpoint","Only during raw fiber testing","Only for domestic sales, never exports"]','After the goods are fully finished, packed, and ready to ship, as a final independent quality checkpoint','Pre-shipment inspection gives the buyer independent confidence that the finished, packed goods meet quality/quantity requirements right before they leave the factory.',3),
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0','advanced','multiple_choice','Dimensional stability specification limits (e.g. +/-3% shrinkage) in a tech pack are set primarily to:','["Allow unlimited shrinkage","Define an acceptable shrinkage range so the finished garment continues to fit properly after consumer washing","Increase fabric cost","Eliminate the need for a wash test"]','Define an acceptable shrinkage range so the finished garment continues to fit properly after consumer washing','Buyers specify tight shrinkage tolerances because excessive shrinkage after washing would make a correctly-fitted garment become too small, a major consumer complaint risk.',3),
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0','advanced','multiple_choice','A "pass/fail" test result versus a "measured value" test result in a lab report differ in that:','["They mean exactly the same thing","Pass/fail gives a simple binary outcome against a spec, while a measured value gives the actual numeric result that can be compared against varying tolerance limits","Measured value tests are never used in textiles","Pass/fail always requires more equipment"]','Pass/fail gives a simple binary outcome against a spec, while a measured value gives the actual numeric result that can be compared against varying tolerance limits','Reporting the actual measured value (not just pass/fail) lets different buyers apply their own tolerance limits to the same underlying test data.',3),
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0','advanced','multiple_choice','A conditioning atmosphere (standard temperature/humidity, e.g. 21C/65% RH) before physical testing is required because:','["It has no effect on test results","Many textile properties (strength, dimensions, weight) are affected by moisture content, so standardized conditioning ensures comparable, repeatable results","It only affects color testing","It is only relevant for wool fiber"]','Many textile properties (strength, dimensions, weight) are affected by moisture content, so standardized conditioning ensures comparable, repeatable results','Because fibers are hygroscopic, un-conditioned samples tested at different ambient humidity could give inconsistent results; standard conditioning removes this variable.',3),
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0','advanced','multiple_choice','Root cause corrective action for a recurring seam-strength failure would most appropriately start with:','["Immediately blaming the sewing operator without investigation","Investigating potential causes systematically (thread type, SPI, seam type, fabric strength) rather than assuming a single cause","Increasing the price charged to the buyer","Ignoring the issue since AQL sampling already passed once"]','Investigating potential causes systematically (thread type, SPI, seam type, fabric strength) rather than assuming a single cause','Effective root cause analysis examines all plausible contributing factors methodically rather than jumping to an unverified single explanation.',3),
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0','advanced','multiple_choice','A quality audit checklist for a factory typically evaluates which combination of areas?','["Only fabric color","Incoming material QC, in-process QC, final inspection, and quality documentation/records together","Only worker attendance","Only machine brand"]','Incoming material QC, in-process QC, final inspection, and quality documentation/records together','A comprehensive quality system audit checks controls at every stage of production plus whether proper records/traceability documentation is maintained.',3),
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0','advanced','multiple_choice','Testing a fabric sample''s tensile strength in both warp and weft (or wale and course) directions is necessary because:','["Fabric strength is always identical in both directions","Woven/knitted fabric properties are typically anisotropic (different) in each direction due to yarn/construction differences","Only one direction ever matters for garments","Direction has no bearing on fabric performance"]','Woven/knitted fabric properties are typically anisotropic (different) in each direction due to yarn/construction differences','Because thread count, yarn type, and construction can differ between the two fabric directions, strength (and many other properties) is rarely identical in both, so both must be tested.',3),
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0','advanced','multiple_choice','A "golden sample" retained by QC/merchandising serves as:','["A decorative display item only","The definitive approved physical reference standard that ongoing bulk production must match for quality/appearance","A backup fabric roll","A discarded rejected sample"]','The definitive approved physical reference standard that ongoing bulk production must match for quality/appearance','The golden sample is the buyer-approved benchmark kept on hand throughout production so QC can physically compare bulk output against the agreed standard at any time.',3),
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0','advanced','multiple_choice','Traceability documentation (batch/lot records linking fabric, trims, and production dates) is maintained mainly to:','["Increase paperwork with no real benefit","Allow any quality issue to be traced back to its source (specific fabric lot, machine, or shift) for investigation and recall management if needed","Replace the need for inspection entirely","Only satisfy tax authorities"]','Allow any quality issue to be traced back to its source (specific fabric lot, machine, or shift) for investigation and recall management if needed','If a defect or safety issue surfaces after shipment, traceability records let a factory pinpoint exactly which batch/lot/line was affected, containing the impact.',3),
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0','advanced','multiple_choice','A "walk-through" inspection performed periodically by a supervisor differs from formal AQL sampling in that it:','["Follows the exact same statistical sampling table","Is a less formal, more frequent visual check across the line to catch obvious issues quickly between formal inspection points","Replaces the need for any formal inspection","Only applies to raw fabric, never garments"]','Is a less formal, more frequent visual check across the line to catch obvious issues quickly between formal inspection points','Walk-through checks are quick, informal, high-frequency spot checks meant to catch obvious problems early, complementing (not replacing) formal statistical inspection.',3),
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0','advanced','multiple_choice','When a fabric roll fails four-point inspection, the typical next step is to:','["Ship it anyway without further action","Segregate/downgrade the roll, and either use it for lower-grade products, request a replacement, or negotiate a price adjustment with the supplier","Immediately destroy all fabric in the warehouse","Ignore the failure and proceed to cutting"]','Segregate/downgrade the roll, and either use it for lower-grade products, request a replacement, or negotiate a price adjustment with the supplier','A failed roll is typically segregated from good stock, with disposition (downgrade, return, discount, or rework) decided based on the severity and nature of defects found.',3);
INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points) VALUES
('ddedfe3c-fe07-4167-b56f-656c9fb89f5e','advanced','multiple_choice','Vertical integration in a textile business means a single company:','["Only sells to one customer","Controls multiple stages of production (e.g. spinning through garment making) rather than relying entirely on external suppliers","Only operates in one country","Only produces one fiber type"]','Controls multiple stages of production (e.g. spinning through garment making) rather than relying entirely on external suppliers','Vertically integrated mills own several production stages in-house, improving supply chain control, lead time, and cost, versus fragmented sourcing across separate companies.',3),
('ddedfe3c-fe07-4167-b56f-656c9fb89f5e','advanced','multiple_choice','An HS (Harmonized System) code in international textile trade is used to:','["Identify the dye recipe used","Classify products for customs duty and trade statistics purposes worldwide using a standardized numeric code","Identify the sewing machine brand","Set the retail price"]','Classify products for customs duty and trade statistics purposes worldwide using a standardized numeric code','HS codes provide an internationally standardized classification system so customs authorities globally can apply consistent duties and track trade statistics.',3),
('ddedfe3c-fe07-4167-b56f-656c9fb89f5e','advanced','multiple_choice','Rules of origin in trade agreements determine:','["Only the fiber content of a garment","Which country a product is considered to originate from for preferential duty/tariff treatment eligibility","The dye color used","The garment''s care label instructions"]','Which country a product is considered to originate from for preferential duty/tariff treatment eligibility','Origin rules (e.g. requiring a certain percentage of local value-add or specific processing stages) determine whether a shipment qualifies for reduced/duty-free tariff treatment under a given trade agreement.',3),
('ddedfe3c-fe07-4167-b56f-656c9fb89f5e','advanced','multiple_choice','GSP (Generalized System of Preferences) trade status generally allows eligible developing countries to:','["Pay higher tariffs than other exporters","Export certain goods to participating markets at reduced or zero import duties","Be banned from exporting entirely","Only trade with neighboring countries"]','Export certain goods to participating markets at reduced or zero import duties','GSP programs grant preferential (lower/zero) tariff access to eligible developing-country exports as an economic development tool for the granting country''s market.',3),
('ddedfe3c-fe07-4167-b56f-656c9fb89f5e','advanced','multiple_choice','Incoterms (e.g. FOB, CIF, EXW) in international trade contracts primarily define:','["The garment''s fiber content","The division of shipping cost, risk, and responsibility between buyer and seller at each stage of transport","The fabric dye recipe","The factory''s labor standards"]','The division of shipping cost, risk, and responsibility between buyer and seller at each stage of transport','Incoterms standardize exactly where cost and risk transfer from seller to buyer during shipment, preventing disputes over who pays/is liable for what at each transport stage.',3),
('ddedfe3c-fe07-4167-b56f-656c9fb89f5e','advanced','multiple_choice','CIF (Cost, Insurance, and Freight) pricing, compared to FOB, means the seller additionally covers:','["Nothing extra beyond FOB","Shipping freight cost and marine insurance up to the destination port","Import duty at the destination country","Retail distribution costs"]','Shipping freight cost and marine insurance up to the destination port','Under CIF, unlike FOB, the seller arranges and pays for both ocean freight and insurance to the named destination port, though risk may still transfer earlier per Incoterm rules.',3),
('ddedfe3c-fe07-4167-b56f-656c9fb89f5e','advanced','multiple_choice','The Higg Index, developed by the Sustainable Apparel Coalition, is used primarily to:','["Rate garment fashion trends","Assess and measure the environmental and social sustainability performance of a facility, product, or brand","Set factory wages","Test fabric strength"]','Assess and measure the environmental and social sustainability performance of a facility, product, or brand','The Higg Index provides standardized self-assessment tools (facility, product, brand modules) for measuring and improving sustainability performance across the value chain.',3),
('ddedfe3c-fe07-4167-b56f-656c9fb89f5e','advanced','multiple_choice','Life Cycle Assessment (LCA) of a textile product evaluates environmental impact across:','["Only the retail sale stage","The entire product life -- raw material extraction, processing, use, and end-of-life disposal/recycling","Only the dyeing stage","Only the shipping stage"]','The entire product life -- raw material extraction, processing, use, and end-of-life disposal/recycling','LCA takes a cradle-to-grave (or cradle-to-cradle) view, quantifying environmental impacts at every stage rather than just one isolated process step.',3),
('ddedfe3c-fe07-4167-b56f-656c9fb89f5e','advanced','multiple_choice','Mechanical recycling of textile waste (as opposed to chemical recycling) works by:','["Dissolving fiber chemically back into raw polymer","Physically shredding/tearing fabric back into fiber form for respinning, without breaking down the polymer chemically","Burning the waste for energy only","Composting the waste"]','Physically shredding/tearing fabric back into fiber form for respinning, without breaking down the polymer chemically','Mechanical recycling tears fabric back into shorter, often lower-quality fiber for reuse, whereas chemical recycling breaks the polymer down to reconstitute higher-quality fiber.',3),
('ddedfe3c-fe07-4167-b56f-656c9fb89f5e','advanced','multiple_choice','Chemical recycling of polyester (e.g. depolymerization) offers an advantage over mechanical recycling because it can:','["Only process cotton","Break the polymer down to monomer level and rebuild virgin-quality fiber, overcoming the quality degradation typical of mechanical recycling","Never be used commercially","Increase fabric GSM directly"]','Break the polymer down to monomer level and rebuild virgin-quality fiber, overcoming the quality degradation typical of mechanical recycling','Depolymerizing polyester back to its base monomers allows repolymerization into fiber with properties comparable to virgin material, avoiding the fiber-shortening/quality loss of mechanical recycling.',3),
('ddedfe3c-fe07-4167-b56f-656c9fb89f5e','advanced','multiple_choice','A circular economy approach in textiles emphasizes:','["Maximizing single-use disposal","Designing products and systems to keep materials in use longer through reuse, repair, and recycling, minimizing waste","Only reducing production volume to zero","Ignoring end-of-life product impact"]','Designing products and systems to keep materials in use longer through reuse, repair, and recycling, minimizing waste','Circularity aims to close the loop -- designing for durability/recyclability and building systems that recover and reuse materials rather than a linear make-use-dispose model.',3),
('ddedfe3c-fe07-4167-b56f-656c9fb89f5e','advanced','multiple_choice','Carbon footprint reporting for a textile product typically quantifies:','["Only the fabric color used","Total greenhouse gas emissions generated across the product''s production and/or life cycle, usually in CO2-equivalent units","The garment''s selling price","Fiber content percentage"]','Total greenhouse gas emissions generated across the product''s production and/or life cycle, usually in CO2-equivalent units','Carbon footprint aggregates emissions from energy, transport, and process chemistry into a standardized CO2-equivalent figure for sustainability benchmarking/reporting.',3),
('ddedfe3c-fe07-4167-b56f-656c9fb89f5e','advanced','multiple_choice','JIT (Just-In-Time) inventory management in a textile supply chain aims to:','["Maximize warehouse stock at all times","Minimize inventory holding by receiving materials/producing goods only as needed, closely synchronized with demand","Eliminate the need for supplier relationships","Increase lead time intentionally"]','Minimize inventory holding by receiving materials/producing goods only as needed, closely synchronized with demand','JIT reduces carrying cost and waste by timing material arrival and production closely to actual demand rather than holding large buffer stock.',3),
('ddedfe3c-fe07-4167-b56f-656c9fb89f5e','advanced','multiple_choice','EOQ (Economic Order Quantity) is a formula used to determine:','["The exact garment style to produce","The optimal order quantity that minimizes total inventory cost (ordering cost plus holding cost)","Fabric dye recipe quantity","Worker headcount needed"]','The optimal order quantity that minimizes total inventory cost (ordering cost plus holding cost)','EOQ balances the trade-off between cost per order (favoring larger, less frequent orders) and holding cost (favoring smaller, more frequent orders) to find the cost-minimizing order size.',3),
('ddedfe3c-fe07-4167-b56f-656c9fb89f5e','advanced','multiple_choice','ABC inventory analysis classifies materials based on:','["Alphabetical order of item name only","Their relative value/consumption significance, with \"A\" items being highest-value/priority requiring tighter control","Only their physical size","Random selection"]','Their relative value/consumption significance, with "A" items being highest-value/priority requiring tighter control','ABC analysis (based on the Pareto principle) prioritizes management attention on the small number of high-value "A" items that represent most of total inventory value/impact.',3),
('ddedfe3c-fe07-4167-b56f-656c9fb89f5e','advanced','multiple_choice','ERP (Enterprise Resource Planning) systems in a textile business primarily provide:','["Only fabric dye recipes","An integrated software platform connecting planning, inventory, production, and finance data across the organization","A replacement for all human decision-making","Only a communication chat tool"]','An integrated software platform connecting planning, inventory, production, and finance data across the organization','ERP integrates previously siloed functions (sales, planning, inventory, production, finance) into one connected system for better visibility and coordinated decision-making.',3),
('ddedfe3c-fe07-4167-b56f-656c9fb89f5e','advanced','multiple_choice','Industry 4.0 concepts applied to textile manufacturing (e.g. IoT-connected machines) primarily enable:','["Elimination of all human workers immediately","Real-time data collection and connectivity across machines/processes for improved monitoring, predictive maintenance, and decision-making","Only marketing benefits with no operational change","Reduced need for quality control"]','Real-time data collection and connectivity across machines/processes for improved monitoring, predictive maintenance, and decision-making','Connected sensors and machines feed real-time production/performance data into analytics systems, enabling proactive maintenance and data-driven process improvement.',3),
('ddedfe3c-fe07-4167-b56f-656c9fb89f5e','advanced','multiple_choice','A digital twin in a smart textile factory context refers to:','["A backup factory building","A virtual, data-driven digital replica of a physical process/machine used to simulate, monitor, or optimize its real-world counterpart","A duplicate fabric roll","A second shift of workers"]','A virtual, data-driven digital replica of a physical process/machine used to simulate, monitor, or optimize its real-world counterpart','Digital twins mirror real equipment/processes virtually using live data, letting engineers simulate changes or predict issues before applying them to the physical system.',3),
('ddedfe3c-fe07-4167-b56f-656c9fb89f5e','advanced','multiple_choice','Break-even analysis in textile factory financial planning determines:','["The exact fabric dye recipe","The production volume at which total revenue equals total cost, beyond which the operation becomes profitable","The fiber content of a garment","The buyer''s preferred shipping method"]','The production volume at which total revenue equals total cost, beyond which the operation becomes profitable','Break-even point (fixed costs / contribution margin per unit) tells management the minimum output needed before a product/line starts generating profit.',3),
('ddedfe3c-fe07-4167-b56f-656c9fb89f5e','advanced','multiple_choice','Standard costing (as opposed to actual costing) in a textile mill involves:','["Recording only real costs after the fact with no predetermined benchmark","Setting predetermined cost benchmarks in advance, then analyzing variances between standard and actual costs incurred","Ignoring cost tracking entirely","Only tracking fabric costs, never labor"]','Setting predetermined cost benchmarks in advance, then analyzing variances between standard and actual costs incurred','Standard costing sets expected costs upfront (material, labor, overhead) so management can measure and investigate variances against real performance, aiding cost control.',3),
('ddedfe3c-fe07-4167-b56f-656c9fb89f5e','advanced','multiple_choice','SA8000 certification for a textile/garment factory primarily addresses:','["Fabric quality standards","Social accountability -- labor rights, working conditions, and ethical workplace practices","Machine calibration standards","Dye chemical restrictions"]','Social accountability -- labor rights, working conditions, and ethical workplace practices','SA8000 is a voluntary social certification standard covering labor practices such as child labor prevention, fair wages, working hours, and freedom of association.',3),
('ddedfe3c-fe07-4167-b56f-656c9fb89f5e','advanced','multiple_choice','ISO 14001 certification for a manufacturing facility relates specifically to:','["Product quality management","Environmental management systems, demonstrating structured control and improvement of environmental impact","Financial accounting standards","Worker social welfare only"]','Environmental management systems, demonstrating structured control and improvement of environmental impact','ISO 14001 certifies that a facility has implemented a systematic approach to managing and continually improving its environmental performance and compliance.',3),
('ddedfe3c-fe07-4167-b56f-656c9fb89f5e','advanced','multiple_choice','Nearshoring in apparel sourcing strategy refers to:','["Moving production to the furthest possible country","Sourcing production from countries geographically closer to the end market to reduce lead time and shipping cost/risk","Only producing domestically with no imports","Eliminating international trade entirely"]','Sourcing production from countries geographically closer to the end market to reduce lead time and shipping cost/risk','Nearshoring trades potentially higher labor cost for shorter, faster, and lower-risk supply chains, valuable for fast-fashion or replenishment-sensitive categories.',3),
('ddedfe3c-fe07-4167-b56f-656c9fb89f5e','advanced','multiple_choice','Demand forecasting in textile/apparel retail planning primarily helps a business:','["Ignore seasonal trends","Anticipate future sales volume so production, inventory, and sourcing can be planned proactively rather than reactively","Only set factory holiday schedules","Determine dye recipes"]','Anticipate future sales volume so production, inventory, and sourcing can be planned proactively rather than reactively','Accurate forecasting reduces the risk of stockouts (lost sales) or overproduction (excess inventory/markdowns) by aligning supply planning with expected demand.',3),
('ddedfe3c-fe07-4167-b56f-656c9fb89f5e','advanced','multiple_choice','A "chargeback" in retail/buyer relationships refers to:','["A bonus payment to the factory","A financial penalty deducted by the retailer/buyer from payment due to a factory for compliance failures such as late shipment or quality issues","A type of fabric defect","A shipping method"]','A financial penalty deducted by the retailer/buyer from payment due to a factory for compliance failures such as late shipment or quality issues','Chargebacks are contractual penalties buyers apply against factory invoices for violations like late delivery, mislabeling, or failing routing/compliance guidelines.',3),
('ddedfe3c-fe07-4167-b56f-656c9fb89f5e','advanced','multiple_choice','Lead time in apparel supply chain planning refers to:','["Only the sewing time for one garment","The total elapsed time from order placement to finished goods delivery","The dye bath temperature ramp time","The fabric shrinkage percentage"]','The total elapsed time from order placement to finished goods delivery','Lead time encompasses the entire order fulfillment cycle -- sourcing, production, and shipping -- and is a critical planning and competitiveness metric.',3),
('ddedfe3c-fe07-4167-b56f-656c9fb89f5e','advanced','multiple_choice','Bonded warehouse facilities in textile trade allow:','["Goods to be sold tax-free domestically with no restriction","Imported raw materials to be stored/processed without immediate payment of import duty, until the finished goods are exported or released domestically","Only finished garment storage with no processing","Elimination of all customs procedures"]','Imported raw materials to be stored/processed without immediate payment of import duty, until the finished goods are exported or released domestically','Bonded facilities defer duty payment on imported inputs, particularly valuable for export-oriented manufacturing where inputs will leave the country again as finished exports.',3),
('ddedfe3c-fe07-4167-b56f-656c9fb89f5e','advanced','multiple_choice','A back-to-back letter of credit (LC) arrangement in textile export financing allows a supplier to:','["Avoid ever receiving payment","Use the buyer''s incoming LC as security to open a second LC to pay their own raw material/sub-supplier","Bypass all banking requirements","Ship goods without any financial documentation"]','Use the buyer''s incoming LC as security to open a second LC to pay their own raw material/sub-supplier','A back-to-back LC lets an exporter leverage the credit-worthiness of the buyer''s LC to finance and guarantee payment to their own upstream suppliers.',3),
('ddedfe3c-fe07-4167-b56f-656c9fb89f5e','advanced','multiple_choice','Quota systems (historically under agreements like the Multi-Fiber Arrangement) restricted textile trade by:','["Setting unlimited export volume","Limiting the quantity of specific textile/apparel categories that could be exported from one country to another within a set period","Only regulating fiber content labeling","Setting global dye standards"]','Limiting the quantity of specific textile/apparel categories that could be exported from one country to another within a set period','Quota systems capped how much of a product category a country could export to a given market, historically shaping global sourcing patterns until such agreements phased out.',3),
('ddedfe3c-fe07-4167-b56f-656c9fb89f5e','advanced','multiple_choice','LDC (Least Developed Country) status in trade policy often grants exporting apparel factories:','["Higher tariffs than other countries","Preferential duty-free/quota-free market access in many developed country markets as a development incentive","No trade benefits at all","Mandatory export bans"]','Preferential duty-free/quota-free market access in many developed country markets as a development incentive','Many developed markets grant LDC-status countries preferential (often duty-free, quota-free) access as part of international development support policy.',3),
('ddedfe3c-fe07-4167-b56f-656c9fb89f5e','advanced','multiple_choice','A textile industry value chain typically flows in which general sequence?','["Retail -> Garment making -> Weaving -> Fiber","Fiber/Yarn -> Fabric (weaving/knitting) -> Wet processing -> Garment making -> Retail/Distribution","Garment making -> Fiber -> Fabric -> Retail","Retail -> Fiber -> Garment making -> Fabric"]','Fiber/Yarn -> Fabric (weaving/knitting) -> Wet processing -> Garment making -> Retail/Distribution','The value chain progresses from raw fiber/yarn through fabric formation, wet processing/finishing, garment assembly, and finally to distribution/retail.',3),
('ddedfe3c-fe07-4167-b56f-656c9fb89f5e','advanced','multiple_choice','Diversification of buyer/customer base is a risk management strategy for a textile exporter mainly because:','["Relying on one buyer increases risk if that buyer''s orders or business changes suddenly","Concentrating on one buyer always reduces risk","Diversification always increases cost with no benefit","Buyers never change their sourcing strategy"]','Relying on one buyer increases risk if that buyer''s orders or business changes suddenly','Heavy dependence on a single customer exposes a factory to significant risk if that buyer reduces orders, changes suppliers, or faces its own business trouble.',3),
('ddedfe3c-fe07-4167-b56f-656c9fb89f5e','advanced','multiple_choice','Compliance with REACH regulation (EU) for textile exports mainly concerns:','["Fabric color trends","Restriction/registration of certain chemical substances used or present in products sold into the EU market","Worker overtime hours","Garment sizing standards"]','Restriction/registration of certain chemical substances used or present in products sold into the EU market','REACH regulates chemical substances in products sold in the EU, restricting or requiring registration of certain hazardous chemicals that could be present in textiles.',3),
('ddedfe3c-fe07-4167-b56f-656c9fb89f5e','advanced','multiple_choice','A "sourcing map" or supplier matrix used by brand merchandising teams primarily helps:','["Design new fabric prints","Compare and select suppliers/factories based on capability, cost, capacity, compliance, and location for different product categories","Only track dye recipes","Replace the need for factory audits"]','Compare and select suppliers/factories based on capability, cost, capacity, compliance, and location for different product categories','A sourcing matrix organizes supplier options against key decision criteria, helping merchandising teams strategically place orders with the most suitable factory for each product type.',3),
('ddedfe3c-fe07-4167-b56f-656c9fb89f5e','advanced','multiple_choice','Working capital management is especially critical for textile exporters mainly because:','["Fabric never requires upfront investment","Production requires significant upfront investment in raw materials/labor well before payment is received from the buyer, often months later","Buyers always pay in advance","There is no financing gap in export production"]','Production requires significant upfront investment in raw materials/labor well before payment is received from the buyer, often months later','The gap between paying for materials/labor and receiving buyer payment (often net 30-90+ days after shipment) creates a real working capital financing need that must be actively managed.',3),
('ddedfe3c-fe07-4167-b56f-656c9fb89f5e','advanced','multiple_choice','Free Trade Agreements (FTAs) between countries generally aim to:','["Increase tariffs between member countries","Reduce or eliminate tariffs and trade barriers between member countries to boost trade volume","Ban all textile trade between members","Only apply to agricultural goods"]','Reduce or eliminate tariffs and trade barriers between member countries to boost trade volume','FTAs negotiate reduced/zero tariffs and simplified trade rules between signatory countries, often reshaping where apparel brands choose to source production.',3),
('ddedfe3c-fe07-4167-b56f-656c9fb89f5e','advanced','multiple_choice','A factory''s "on-time delivery" (OTD) KPI is tracked by management mainly because poor OTD performance directly risks:','["Improved buyer relationships","Buyer chargebacks, order cancellations, and damage to the factory''s reputation/future order allocation","No consequence to the business","Increased fabric quality"]','Buyer chargebacks, order cancellations, and damage to the factory''s reputation/future order allocation','Missing shipment dates can trigger financial penalties, air-freight cost absorption, or lost future orders, making OTD one of the most closely watched performance metrics.',3);
INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points) VALUES
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411','advanced','multiple_choice','POY (Partially Oriented Yarn) is a synthetic filament yarn that:','["Is fully drawn and ready for final use as-is","Has only partial molecular orientation from spinning and requires further drawing/texturizing before final use","Is only produced from cotton fiber","Cannot be further processed"]','Has only partial molecular orientation from spinning and requires further drawing/texturizing before final use','POY is spun at high speed with only partial molecular chain orientation, serving as an intermediate feedstock for further draw-texturizing (e.g. into DTY).',3),
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411','advanced','multiple_choice','FDY (Fully Drawn Yarn) differs from POY in that FDY:','["Has no molecular orientation at all","Is spun and drawn in one continuous process to achieve full molecular orientation, ready for direct use without further drawing","Can only be used for spinning cotton yarn","Requires additional false-twist texturizing before any use"]','Is spun and drawn in one continuous process to achieve full molecular orientation, ready for direct use without further drawing','FDY combines spinning and drawing in a single continuous step, producing a fully oriented, dimensionally stable filament yarn ready for weaving/knitting directly.',3),
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411','advanced','multiple_choice','DTY (Draw Textured Yarn) is produced by:','["Simply drawing POY with no twist involved","Drawing and false-twist texturizing POY simultaneously to impart crimp/bulk and stretch to the filament yarn","Melting POY completely","Blending POY with cotton fiber"]','Drawing and false-twist texturizing POY simultaneously to impart crimp/bulk and stretch to the filament yarn','DTY machines draw POY to full orientation while simultaneously false-twist texturizing it, creating a bulkier, more fabric-like, stretchable filament yarn versus flat POY/FDY.',3),
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411','advanced','multiple_choice','Air-jet texturizing (as opposed to false-twist texturizing) creates yarn bulk by:','["Inserting real mechanical twist that is later removed","Using turbulent compressed air to loop and entangle filaments randomly, creating bulk without relying on twist/heat-setting","Using only chemical treatment","Using no air or twist at all"]','Using turbulent compressed air to loop and entangle filaments randomly, creating bulk without relying on twist/heat-setting','Air-jet texturizing blasts filaments with turbulent air to form random loops on the yarn surface, giving bulk and a more natural (often linen-like) appearance without a false-twist heat-set step.',3),
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411','advanced','multiple_choice','BCF (Bulked Continuous Filament) yarn is most commonly associated with which end use?','["Fine apparel shirting","Carpet manufacturing, where its bulk and durability are valued","Surgical thread","Delicate lace fabric"]','Carpet manufacturing, where its bulk and durability are valued','BCF yarns are texturized for high bulk and durability, making them the standard yarn type for tufted and woven carpet manufacturing.',3),
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411','advanced','multiple_choice','Microfiber, in textile terminology, refers to filament fiber with linear density typically:','["Above 10 denier per filament","Below approximately 1 denier per filament","Exactly equal to cotton fiber fineness","Only measurable in Ne count"]','Below approximately 1 denier per filament','Microfiber is defined by extremely fine individual filaments (commonly under 1 dpf), giving fabrics an exceptionally soft hand, high surface area, and fine drape.',3),
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411','advanced','multiple_choice','Bicomponent fiber is manufactured by:','["Blending two different staple fibers before spinning only","Extruding two different polymers (or the same polymer with different properties) together through a single spinneret to form one integrated filament","Twisting two finished yarns together","Dyeing a filament in two colors"]','Extruding two different polymers (or the same polymer with different properties) together through a single spinneret to form one integrated filament','Bicomponent fibers combine two polymer types in a single filament cross-section (e.g. side-by-side or sheath-core), engineering properties like self-crimp or dual functionality unavailable from a single polymer.',3),
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411','advanced','multiple_choice','A sheath-core bicomponent fiber structure is often used to:','["Combine a strong core polymer with a different sheath polymer offering desired surface properties (e.g. dyeability, softness, or low-melt bonding)","Eliminate the need for spinning entirely","Only produce fibers for medical use","Remove all crimp from the fiber"]','Combine a strong core polymer with a different sheath polymer offering desired surface properties (e.g. dyeability, softness, or low-melt bonding)','Sheath-core fibers let engineers optimize the outer surface (dye affinity, softness, or a low-melt bonding sheath for nonwovens) while a different core polymer provides bulk strength.',3),
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411','advanced','multiple_choice','Aramid fibers (e.g. Kevlar, Nomex) are high-performance fibers primarily valued for:','["High stretch and low cost","Exceptional strength-to-weight ratio and/or heat/flame resistance for protective and industrial applications","High moisture absorbency for comfort wear","Being the softest fiber available"]','Exceptional strength-to-weight ratio and/or heat/flame resistance for protective and industrial applications','Aramids offer very high tenacity and thermal stability, making them standard choices for ballistic protection, cut-resistant gear, and flame-resistant workwear.',3),
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411','advanced','multiple_choice','UHMWPE (Ultra-High Molecular Weight Polyethylene) fiber is notable in technical textiles for:','["Poor strength relative to weight","An exceptionally high strength-to-weight ratio, often exceeding steel on a weight basis, used in ropes and ballistic applications","Being biodegradable within days","High melting point above 500C"]','An exceptionally high strength-to-weight ratio, often exceeding steel on a weight basis, used in ballistic and rope applications','UHMWPE''s extremely long, highly aligned polymer chains give it outstanding tenacity per unit weight, exceeding steel and even aramid on a weight-for-weight basis in some applications.',3),
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411','advanced','multiple_choice','Conductive/smart yarns (e.g. incorporating metal or conductive polymer filaments) are engineered mainly to enable:','["Traditional dyeing only","Electronic functionality within textiles, such as e-textiles, sensors, or heating elements","Improved biodegradability","Lower yarn cost than standard fiber"]','Electronic functionality within textiles, such as e-textiles, sensors, or heating elements','Conductive yarns embed metal fiber or conductive coatings/polymers within the yarn structure, enabling wearable electronics, sensing, or heating applications directly in fabric.',3),
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411','advanced','multiple_choice','Phase-change material (PCM) yarns/fibers function by:','["Permanently changing fiber color with temperature","Absorbing and releasing thermal energy as the PCM changes physical state, helping regulate temperature against the skin","Increasing yarn strength at high temperature","Dissolving at body temperature"]','Absorbing and releasing thermal energy as the PCM changes physical state, helping regulate temperature against the skin','Microencapsulated PCM within or on the fiber absorbs excess heat (melting) or releases stored heat (solidifying) around a set temperature, buffering the wearer''s microclimate.',3),
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411','advanced','multiple_choice','The Uster Tester (evenness tester) is the standard instrument for measuring:','["Fabric color fastness","Yarn/sliver mass variation (unevenness, imperfections, hairiness) using a capacitive sensor as material passes through","Fiber staple length only","Loom efficiency"]','Yarn/sliver mass variation (unevenness, imperfections, hairiness) using a capacitive sensor as material passes through','A capacitive measuring head detects mass variation as yarn/sliver passes through at speed, generating CV%, imperfection counts, and (with an optical sensor) hairiness data.',3),
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411','advanced','multiple_choice','HVI (High Volume Instrument) testing of raw cotton fiber rapidly measures which combined set of properties?','["Only fiber color","Length, strength, micronaire, color, and trash content in one automated test sequence","Only trash content","Only fiber moisture"]','Length, strength, micronaire, color, and trash content in one automated test sequence','HVI systems rapidly test multiple key raw cotton quality parameters together, enabling efficient bale classification and mill fiber blending decisions.',3),
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411','advanced','multiple_choice','Space-dyed yarn is produced by:','["Dyeing the entire yarn package a single uniform color","Applying dye at intervals along the yarn length, creating a multi-color, variegated effect once knitted/woven","Bleaching the yarn completely white","Adding metallic thread only"]','Applying dye at intervals along the yarn length, creating a multi-color, variegated effect once knitted/woven','Intermittent dye application along the yarn''s length creates a repeating or random multi-color pattern effect directly in the fabric without printing.',3),
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411','advanced','multiple_choice','Melange yarn is produced by:','["Dyeing finished fabric after weaving","Blending dyed and undyed (or differently colored) fiber together before/during spinning, giving a heathered, mottled color effect","Printing a pattern onto plain yarn","Using only synthetic filament yarn"]','Blending dyed and undyed (or differently colored) fiber together before/during spinning, giving a heathered, mottled color effect','Melange yarn results from blending fiber of different colors (often dyed and raw white) at the blow room/carding stage, producing the classic heathered/mottled look throughout the yarn.',3),
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411','advanced','multiple_choice','Bare spandex (as opposed to covered spandex) yarn refers to spandex used:','["Wrapped completely by another fiber before any use","Directly, without any covering fiber, typically knitted/woven bare into fabric alongside other yarns for stretch","Only as a decorative surface yarn","Only in nonwoven applications"]','Directly, without any covering fiber, typically knitted/woven bare into fabric alongside other yarns for stretch','Bare spandex is fed directly into the knitting/weaving process without a fiber covering, commonly plated with a face yarn during knitting to add stretch cost-effectively.',3),
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411','advanced','multiple_choice','Covered spandex (single or double covered) yarn is produced by:','["Leaving spandex completely exposed","Wrapping staple or filament fiber around a spandex core using a covering/twisting machine, protecting the spandex and improving handling/dyeability","Blending spandex fiber directly into cotton before spinning","Melting spandex onto fabric surface"]','Wrapping staple or filament fiber around a spandex core using a covering/twisting machine, protecting the spandex and improving handling/dyeability','A covering machine wraps yarn around the spandex core, shielding it from heat/chemical damage during processing and giving better surface dyeability and hand versus bare spandex.',3),
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411','advanced','multiple_choice','Slub yarn is characterized by:','["Perfectly uniform thickness throughout","Intentionally engineered thick and thin sections along its length for a textured, irregular fabric appearance","Being usable only for sewing thread","Zero twist throughout"]','Intentionally engineered thick and thin sections along its length for a textured, irregular fabric appearance','Slub yarn deliberately varies thickness (via controlled drafting variation) to create a novelty textured appearance valued in casual/rustic fabric styling.',3),
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411','advanced','multiple_choice','Chenille yarn is produced using a specialized process that:','["Twists two plain yarns together only","Cuts a leno-woven fabric strip into narrow yarn with pile fibers protruding on all sides, creating a fuzzy, caterpillar-like yarn","Melts filament into a solid rod","Uses only metallic fiber"]','Cuts a leno-woven fabric strip into narrow yarn with pile fibers protruding on all sides, creating a fuzzy, caterpillar-like yarn','Chenille is made by weaving a leno fabric with cut pile threads, then slitting it into narrow strips -- each strip becomes a fuzzy yarn with pile radiating in all directions.',3),
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411','advanced','multiple_choice','Metallic yarn used for decorative fabric effects is most commonly constructed by:','["Spinning pure metal fiber alone","Laminating a thin metallic (often aluminized) film between polyester layers, then slitting it into fine yarn strips","Dyeing cotton yarn silver","Using only stainless steel filament"]','Laminating a thin metallic (often aluminized) film between polyester layers, then slitting it into fine yarn strips','Most decorative metallic yarn is a laminated polyester-metal-polyester film slit into narrow strips, giving a metallic look while remaining flexible and washable, rather than solid metal fiber.',3),
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411','advanced','multiple_choice','A two-for-one (TFO) twisting machine differs from a ring twister mainly because TFO machines:','["Insert only half a twist per spindle revolution","Insert two twists per spindle revolution, roughly doubling productivity for a given spindle speed versus single-twist methods","Cannot process filament yarn","Require no bobbin at all"]','Insert two twists per spindle revolution, roughly doubling productivity for a given spindle speed versus single-twist methods','TFO technology inserts two twists in the yarn for every one revolution of the spindle, significantly improving cabling/twisting productivity over older single-twist methods.',3),
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411','advanced','multiple_choice','Cabling (twisting multiple plied yarns together again) is done to produce yarns primarily intended for:','["Standard single-jersey T-shirts","Heavy-duty applications like ropes, upholstery, or reinforced sewing thread requiring extra strength/bulk","Sheer chiffon fabric","Fine lace only"]','Heavy-duty applications like ropes, upholstery, or reinforced sewing thread requiring extra strength/bulk','Cabled yarns (plies of plies) build up significant strength and bulk, suited to demanding end uses like industrial thread, upholstery, and rope rather than lightweight apparel.',3),
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411','advanced','multiple_choice','Ring-spun yarn generally outperforms rotor-spun and air-jet spun yarn in which key property?','["Bulk/loftiness","Tensile strength and overall yarn quality at fine counts","Production speed","Machine energy efficiency"]','Tensile strength and overall yarn quality at fine counts','Ring spinning''s highly parallel, well-consolidated fiber structure gives superior strength and evenness compared to rotor or air-jet structures, especially as counts get finer.',3),
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411','advanced','multiple_choice','Yarn hairiness reduction technologies (e.g. compact spinning, singeing/gassing) are commercially important mainly because lower hairiness improves:','["Fiber color only","Fabric surface clarity/pilling resistance and reduces downstream processing issues like fly/lint generation","Yarn cost with no quality change","Only twist multiplier"]','Fabric surface clarity/pilling resistance and reduces downstream processing issues like fly/lint generation','Fewer protruding fibers mean cleaner fabric surfaces, less pilling potential, and less airborne fiber fly/lint generated during weaving/knitting.',3),
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411','advanced','multiple_choice','Yarn "gassing" (singeing) is a process that:','["Adds twist to yarn","Passes yarn quickly through a flame to burn off surface hairiness, leaving a smoother, more lustrous yarn","Bleaches yarn white","Wraps yarn with a filament core"]','Passes yarn quickly through a flame to burn off surface hairiness, leaving a smoother, more lustrous yarn','Gassing singes protruding surface fibers off the yarn at high speed, producing a smoother, often more lustrous yarn favored for fine shirting and quality knitwear.',3),
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411','advanced','multiple_choice','Recycled (mechanically reclaimed) fiber yarn typically has which characteristic compared to virgin fiber yarn?','["Always superior strength","Generally shorter average fiber length and somewhat reduced strength/evenness, due to the mechanical breaking-down process","Identical properties in every respect","Higher achievable fineness than virgin fiber"]','Generally shorter average fiber length and somewhat reduced strength/evenness, due to the mechanical breaking-down process','Mechanically shredding fabric/garment waste back to fiber inevitably shortens and damages some fibers, typically limiting the fineness/strength achievable versus virgin fiber.',3),
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411','advanced','multiple_choice','Yarn twist liveliness (residual torque) is specifically addressed in processing by:','["Adding more twist in the same direction","Steam-setting the yarn (autoclave/package steaming) to relax internal torsional stress after spinning","Removing all twist entirely","Only applicable to filament yarn"]','Steam-setting the yarn (autoclave/package steaming) to relax internal torsional stress after spinning','Steam conditioning relaxes the internal torque locked into freshly twisted yarn, reducing snarling/kinking tendencies and spirality issues in downstream knitting.',3),
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411','advanced','multiple_choice','Fancy/effect yarns (slub, boucle, loop, chenille) are primarily produced to:','["Reduce yarn cost below standard yarns always","Create decorative surface texture and visual interest in the final fabric that a standard uniform yarn cannot achieve","Increase yarn tensile strength above standard levels","Eliminate the need for dyeing"]','Create decorative surface texture and visual interest in the final fabric that a standard uniform yarn cannot achieve','Fancy yarns intentionally introduce structural irregularity for aesthetic/textural effect, a design tool standard uniform yarns cannot provide.',3),
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411','advanced','multiple_choice','Boucle yarn structure is characterized by:','["A perfectly smooth, straight surface","Loops of one component yarn held in place along a core/binder yarn, giving a curly, looped surface texture","Zero twist throughout","Being producible only from synthetic filament"]','Loops of one component yarn held in place along a core/binder yarn, giving a curly, looped surface texture','Boucle is a fancy yarn where an effect yarn is fed at excess length relative to a core and binder, forming small loops fixed in place -- giving its signature curly, textured surface.',3),
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411','advanced','multiple_choice','Advanced electronic yarn clearers (e.g. foreign-fiber detection systems) on modern winding machines are able to detect:','["Only mass variation","Both mass-based faults and optically distinguishable foreign fiber contamination (e.g. polypropylene strands in cotton) that would otherwise cause dye-uptake defects","Only twist direction","Only fabric shrinkage"]','Both mass-based faults and optically distinguishable foreign fiber contamination (e.g. polypropylene strands in cotton) that would otherwise cause dye-uptake defects','Combined capacitive/optical clearer systems catch not just thick/thin mass faults but also contamination like foreign fiber, which dyes differently and creates visible fabric defects if not removed.',3),
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411','advanced','multiple_choice','Recombinant/bio-engineered protein fibers (an emerging technology, e.g. lab-grown spider-silk-like fiber) aim to achieve:','["Lower strength than natural silk always","High-performance fiber properties through biotechnology rather than conventional petrochemical or agricultural fiber production","Only decorative color effects","Elimination of all fiber testing requirements"]','High-performance fiber properties through biotechnology rather than conventional petrochemical or agricultural fiber production','These emerging fibers use engineered microorganisms to biologically produce protein-based fiber material, targeting high performance with a different (often more sustainable) production pathway than petrochemical synthetics.',3),
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411','advanced','multiple_choice','Yarn quality index (a composite score sometimes used in mills) typically combines which factors?','["Only yarn color","Strength, evenness (CV%), imperfections, and hairiness combined into a single relative quality benchmark","Only twist multiplier","Only production speed achieved"]','Strength, evenness (CV%), imperfections, and hairiness combined into a single relative quality benchmark','A composite quality index lets mills benchmark overall yarn quality across multiple key properties at once, rather than judging any single metric in isolation.',3),
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411','advanced','multiple_choice','A "murata vortex spinner" (MVS) is a specific commercial implementation of which spinning technology?','["Ring spinning","Air-jet (vortex) spinning","Rotor spinning","Friction spinning"]','Air-jet (vortex) spinning','MVS is a well-known commercial air-jet/vortex spinning system, prized for very high production speed on suitable fiber blends at medium-fine counts.',3),
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411','advanced','multiple_choice','Friction (DREF) spinning is particularly suited for producing:','["Very fine, high-strength yarns for fine shirting","Coarse, bulky yarns often used in industrial, technical, or home textile applications","Fine filament silk-like yarn","High-twist sewing thread"]','Coarse, bulky yarns often used in industrial, technical, or home textile applications','Friction spinning twists fiber against a rotating perforated drum, well suited to producing coarse, bulky, lower-strength yarns economically for non-apparel end uses.',3),
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411','advanced','multiple_choice','Antimicrobial-treated yarns achieve their functional property mainly by:','["Increasing yarn twist only","Incorporating or applying agents (e.g. silver ions) that inhibit microbial/bacterial growth on the fiber surface","Using only synthetic dye","Reducing fiber moisture regain to zero"]','Incorporating or applying agents (e.g. silver ions) that inhibit microbial/bacterial growth on the fiber surface','Antimicrobial finishing embeds or coats fiber with agents like silver ions that suppress bacterial growth, valued in activewear and medical/hygiene textiles for odor and hygiene control.',3),
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411','advanced','multiple_choice','UV-protective yarns/fabrics achieve improved UPF (Ultraviolet Protection Factor) mainly through:','["Only fiber color choice with no other factor","A combination of tight fabric construction, fiber type, and sometimes UV-absorbing additives/finishes","Only thread count, unrelated to fiber type","Reducing fabric weight"]','A combination of tight fabric construction, fiber type, and sometimes UV-absorbing additives/finishes','UPF depends on multiple factors together -- denser weave/knit blocking more UV, certain fibers inherently absorbing more UV, and specialized UV-absorbing chemical treatments boosting protection further.',3);
INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points) VALUES
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','A costing sheet prepared by a merchandiser typically breaks down the garment price into:','["Only the total FOB figure with no detail","Fabric, trims, CM (labor/overhead), and profit margin as separate line items summing to the quoted price","Only labor cost","Only fabric cost"]','Fabric, trims, CM (labor/overhead), and profit margin as separate line items summing to the quoted price','A detailed costing sheet itemizes every cost component so both factory and buyer can see exactly how the final quoted price is built up.',3),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','A "line sheet" used in apparel sales/merchandising presents:','["Only factory production capacity","A catalog-style overview of available/proposed styles with key details (image, price, colors, MOQ) for buyer review","Only shipping schedules","Only fabric test reports"]','A catalog-style overview of available/proposed styles with key details (image, price, colors, MOQ) for buyer review','Line sheets give buyers a quick visual and commercial summary of a season''s style offerings, streamlining the initial selection/negotiation process.',3),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','A tech pack in garment merchandising/development serves as:','["A marketing brochure only","The definitive technical specification document (measurements, construction, materials, artwork) guiding sampling and production","A financial invoice","A shipping manifest"]','The definitive technical specification document (measurements, construction, materials, artwork) guiding sampling and production','The tech pack is the technical blueprint a factory follows to construct the garment correctly, covering measurements, materials, trims, construction details, and artwork placement.',3),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','MOQ (Minimum Order Quantity) in a merchandising negotiation represents:','["The maximum quantity a factory can produce","The smallest order quantity a supplier is willing to accept for a given style/fabric, often tied to production economics","The buyer''s maximum budget","The total number of factories in the supply chain"]','The smallest order quantity a supplier is willing to accept for a given style/fabric, often tied to production economics','MOQs protect suppliers from uneconomical small runs (e.g. minimum fabric dye lot or minimum efficient production batch), and are a key negotiation point especially for new/small buyers.',3),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','A Purchase Order (PO) issued by a buyer to a factory typically confirms:','["Only the shipment date","Style, quantity, price, delivery date, and payment terms as the binding commercial agreement for that order","Only the factory''s address","Only the fabric supplier''s name"]','Style, quantity, price, delivery date, and payment terms as the binding commercial agreement for that order','The PO is the formal contractual document confirming exactly what is being ordered, at what price, quantity, and terms -- the basis for production planning and invoicing.',3),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','An LC (Letter of Credit) payment term provides the exporter with which key benefit?','["No payment security at all","A bank-guaranteed payment mechanism, reducing the risk of non-payment once compliant shipping documents are presented","Immediate cash before any goods are made","Elimination of the need for shipping documents"]','A bank-guaranteed payment mechanism, reducing the risk of non-payment once compliant shipping documents are presented','LC payment is secured by the issuing bank''s guarantee, paying the exporter once they present documents proving compliant shipment -- far more secure than open account terms.',3),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','TT (Telegraphic Transfer) payment, compared to LC, is generally:','["More complex and slower to arrange","A simpler, faster direct bank wire transfer, but with less built-in payment security for the exporter than an LC","Only usable for domestic transactions","Illegal in international trade"]','A simpler, faster direct bank wire transfer, but with less built-in payment security for the exporter than an LC','TT is a straightforward wire transfer (often partial advance plus balance on shipment) that''s simpler and cheaper than LC but relies more on trust between buyer and seller.',3),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','DP (Documents against Payment) terms mean the buyer receives shipping documents (needed to claim the goods) only after:','["Signing a simple promise to pay later with no immediate payment","Making immediate payment through their bank","The goods have already been sold to a retail customer","One year has passed"]','Making immediate payment through their bank','Under DP, the bank releases the title documents to the buyer only once payment is made, protecting the exporter from releasing goods without receiving funds.',3),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','DA (Documents against Acceptance) terms differ from DP in that DA:','["Requires immediate cash payment","Releases documents to the buyer once they formally accept a bill of exchange promising to pay at a future date, not immediately","Requires no documentation at all","Is only used for domestic sales"]','Releases documents to the buyer once they formally accept a bill of exchange promising to pay at a future date, not immediately','DA is riskier for the exporter than DP since the buyer takes possession of goods against a promise (accepted draft) to pay later, rather than paying immediately.',3),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','A buying house/agent in the apparel supply chain typically acts as:','["The final retail seller of garments","An intermediary that sources factories, manages quality/compliance, and coordinates orders on behalf of a buyer/brand","A fiber manufacturer","A dye chemical supplier"]','An intermediary that sources factories, manages quality/compliance, and coordinates orders on behalf of a buyer/brand','Buying houses/agents represent buyer interests locally, handling sourcing, QC coordination, and communication between overseas buyers and local factories.',3),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','Private label merchandise differs from branded merchandise in that private label products are:','["Manufactured and sold under the retailer''s own brand name rather than a separate designer/manufacturer brand","Always more expensive than branded goods","Only sold through the original manufacturer''s own stores","Never subject to quality inspection"]','Manufactured and sold under the retailer''s own brand name rather than a separate designer/manufacturer brand','Private label goods are made to a retailer''s specification and sold under the retailer''s own brand, as opposed to licensed or independently branded merchandise.',3),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','Retail markup (keystone or multiple-based pricing) is applied by retailers to determine:','["The factory''s production cost","The final consumer selling price based on a multiple of the wholesale/landed cost, covering retail overhead and profit","The fabric shrinkage allowance","The garment SMV"]','The final consumer selling price based on a multiple of the wholesale/landed cost, covering retail overhead and profit','Retailers apply a markup multiple to the landed/wholesale cost to set shelf price, covering their own operating costs, marketing, and profit margin.',3),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','Order allocation across multiple factories (order splitting) by a merchandiser/buyer is often done to:','["Increase risk of late delivery intentionally","Balance total capacity, mitigate single-factory risk, and match specific factory strengths to specific product types","Only reduce paperwork","Avoid working with more than one factory ever"]','Balance total capacity, mitigate single-factory risk, and match specific factory strengths to specific product types','Splitting large orders across multiple qualified factories manages capacity constraints, reduces dependency risk, and can leverage each factory''s particular strengths.',3),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','Capacity booking (reserving future production slots with a factory) is important for merchandisers mainly because:','["Factories always have unlimited free capacity","Popular factories can be fully booked well in advance, so securing a slot early is necessary to guarantee the desired delivery timeline","It has no effect on delivery timing","Booking is only relevant for sampling, not bulk production"]','Popular factories can be fully booked well in advance, so securing a slot early is necessary to guarantee the desired delivery timeline','Since factory production lines have finite capacity, merchandisers must reserve slots ahead of time or risk delayed production start dates on high-demand factories.',3),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','A commercial invoice in export documentation primarily serves to:','["Replace the need for a purchase order","Formally state the transaction value, goods description, and terms for customs clearance and buyer payment/accounting","Only list factory employee names","Only certify social compliance"]','Formally state the transaction value, goods description, and terms for customs clearance and buyer payment/accounting','The commercial invoice is the official record of sale value and goods description used by customs authorities and for the buyer''s accounting/payment processing.',3),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','A packing list accompanying a shipment specifies:','["Only the total invoice value","Detailed carton-by-carton breakdown of contents, quantities, weights, and dimensions","Only the fabric supplier''s details","Only the buyer''s marketing plan"]','Detailed carton-by-carton breakdown of contents, quantities, weights, and dimensions','The packing list details exactly what is in each carton (style, size, color, quantity, weight/dimensions), essential for customs, warehousing, and verification on arrival.',3),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','A Bill of Lading (B/L) in ocean freight shipping serves as:','["Only a marketing document","A receipt for goods shipped, a contract of carriage, and (depending on type) a document of title to the goods","A worker payslip","A fabric test report"]','A receipt for goods shipped, a contract of carriage, and (depending on type) a document of title to the goods','The B/L is a critical legal shipping document confirming the carrier has received the goods, outlining shipment terms, and (for a negotiable B/L) representing ownership title.',3),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','A freight forwarder in the export shipping process primarily:','["Manufactures the garments","Arranges and coordinates the logistics of shipping goods (booking, documentation, customs) between exporter and destination on behalf of the shipper","Designs the fabric print","Tests garment colorfastness"]','Arranges and coordinates the logistics of shipping goods (booking, documentation, customs) between exporter and destination on behalf of the shipper','Freight forwarders handle the complex logistics chain -- booking carriers, preparing documentation, coordinating customs clearance -- simplifying the shipping process for exporters.',3),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','LCL (Less than Container Load) shipping, as opposed to FCL (Full Container Load), means:','["A shipper''s goods fill an entire container alone","A shipper''s goods share container space with other shippers'' cargo, typically for smaller shipment volumes","No container is used at all","Only air freight is used"]','A shipper''s goods share container space with other shippers'' cargo, typically for smaller shipment volumes','LCL consolidates multiple smaller shipments from different shippers into one container, cost-effective for volumes too small to justify booking a full container (FCL).',3),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','Air freight shipment, compared to ocean freight, for apparel orders is generally chosen when:','["Cost is the only priority, with no time pressure","Speed is critical (e.g. to recover a delayed order) despite the significantly higher cost per unit","Order volume is extremely large and low-value","Never, since air freight cannot be used for apparel"]','Speed is critical (e.g. to recover a delayed order) despite the significantly higher cost per unit','Air freight''s much faster transit time makes it the choice when a delivery deadline is at risk, even though the cost per kg is substantially higher than ocean freight.',3),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','A "claim" filed by a buyer against a factory typically relates to:','["A request for a bigger future order","A formal complaint/compensation request for quality issues, shortages, or delivery failures found in a completed shipment","A congratulatory note for good performance","A new product design proposal"]','A formal complaint/compensation request for quality issues, shortages, or delivery failures found in a completed shipment','Claims are the formal mechanism buyers use to seek compensation or corrective action when received goods fail to meet the agreed quality, quantity, or delivery terms.',3),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','Seasonal buying cycles (e.g. Spring/Summer, Fall/Winter) in fashion merchandising drive:','["Random, unplanned order timing throughout the year","A structured annual calendar of design, sourcing, production, and delivery deadlines aligned to retail selling seasons","Elimination of all production planning","Only one order per year regardless of season"]','A structured annual calendar of design, sourcing, production, and delivery deadlines aligned to retail selling seasons','Fashion merchandising operates on a predictable seasonal rhythm, with development, sourcing, and production timed well ahead of each season''s in-store delivery window.',3),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','Fast fashion merchandising models differ from traditional seasonal models mainly by:','["Producing only two collections per year","Operating on much shorter design-to-shelf cycles with frequent small-batch style drops responding quickly to trends","Eliminating the need for any sourcing strategy","Only selling through physical stores"]','Operating on much shorter design-to-shelf cycles with frequent small-batch style drops responding quickly to trends','Fast fashion compresses the traditional seasonal cycle into weeks, enabling brands to react quickly to emerging trends with frequent, smaller replenishment orders.',3),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','PLM (Product Lifecycle Management) software used by merchandising/design teams primarily helps:','["Only calculate shipping cost","Centralize and track a style''s development from concept through tech pack, sourcing, and production in one digital system","Replace fabric mills entirely","Only manage social media marketing"]','Centralize and track a style''s development from concept through tech pack, sourcing, and production in one digital system','PLM platforms give teams a single source of truth for style data, tech packs, approvals, and status tracking across the entire product development and sourcing lifecycle.',3),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','Trims and accessories sourcing (buttons, zippers, labels) is managed by merchandising mainly to ensure:','["Only lowest possible cost regardless of compliance","Timely availability, quality, and compliance (e.g. no restricted substances, correct branding) of every non-fabric component needed for the garment","Trims are always sourced from the fabric mill directly","No approval process is needed"]','Timely availability, quality, and compliance (e.g. no restricted substances, correct branding) of every non-fabric component needed for the garment','Missing or non-compliant trims can delay a whole shipment, so merchandising closely tracks trim sourcing, approval, and delivery alongside fabric to keep the TNA on track.',3),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','A negotiation over garment FOB price between buyer and merchandiser typically weighs the factory''s cost breakdown against:','["Only the buyer''s desired retail price with no cost consideration","The buyer''s target price, competitive market rates, and the value/quality/service the factory offers","Random price guessing with no data","Only currency exchange rates"]','The buyer''s target price, competitive market rates, and the value/quality/service the factory offers','Effective price negotiation balances the factory''s real cost structure against buyer target pricing and competitive benchmarks, aiming for a sustainable win-win price.',3),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','A "critical path" delay flagged during TNA monitoring should trigger the merchandiser to:','["Ignore it since shipment date is far away","Immediately investigate the cause and take corrective action (expedite, reallocate resources, or negotiate) to protect the shipment date","Cancel the entire order automatically","Wait until the shipment date to react"]','Immediately investigate the cause and take corrective action (expedite, reallocate resources, or negotiate) to protect the shipment date','Since critical path delays directly threaten the final shipment date, proactive intervention as soon as a delay is flagged is essential to recovery planning.',3),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','Buyer compliance audits are typically coordinated by the merchandising/compliance team to:','["Avoid ever scheduling an audit","Prepare the factory (documentation, working conditions, safety) and facilitate the audit process to maintain the buyer relationship and vendor status","Only occur once at initial factory approval, never again","Replace the need for quality inspection"]','Prepare the factory (documentation, working conditions, safety) and facilitate the audit process to maintain the buyer relationship and vendor status','Compliance audits (social, security, environmental) recur periodically; merchandising/compliance teams prepare documentation and coordinate the visit to maintain approved vendor status.',3),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','Vendor scorecards used by buyers to evaluate factories typically track:','["Only fabric color history","On-time delivery, quality performance, compliance status, and responsiveness across multiple orders over time","Only the factory''s physical size","Only the CEO''s name"]','On-time delivery, quality performance, compliance status, and responsiveness across multiple orders over time','Scorecards give buyers an objective, trackable view of vendor performance across key KPIs, informing future order allocation decisions.',3),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','A "repeat order" in merchandising, as opposed to a new development order, typically has:','["A completely new tech pack and untested construction","An already-approved style/construction, generally allowing a faster, lower-risk path to production than a brand-new style","No sampling process at all, ever","A longer TNA than any new style"]','An already-approved style/construction, generally allowing a faster, lower-risk path to production than a brand-new style','Because the style, fit, and construction were previously approved, repeat orders can often skip or shorten several development stages, speeding up the timeline.',3),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','A merchandiser''s role in fabric booking (reserving fabric production capacity/quantity at the mill) is critical because:','["Fabric is always instantly available with no lead time","Fabric production itself has a lead time, and delays here directly push back the entire downstream cutting/sewing/shipping schedule","Fabric booking has no relation to the TNA","Only trims require booking, never fabric"]','Fabric production itself has a lead time, and delays here directly push back the entire downstream cutting/sewing/shipping schedule','Since fabric is usually an early, long-lead-time input, any delay in booking or receiving it cascades through the entire remaining production schedule.',3),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','Currency exchange rate fluctuation is a risk merchandising/export businesses manage mainly because:','["Exchange rates never change","Orders are often quoted and produced over weeks/months, so rate movements between quotation and payment can materially affect actual profit margin","It only affects the buyer, never the factory","It has no bearing on FOB pricing"]','Orders are often quoted and produced over weeks/months, so rate movements between quotation and payment can materially affect actual profit margin','Since costs are incurred locally but often invoiced in foreign currency, exchange rate shifts between order quotation and final payment can erode or boost realized margin.',3),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','A merchandiser coordinating a buyer factory visit typically ensures which preparation is complete beforehand?','["Nothing needs preparation","Sample availability, production status readiness, and compliance/documentation are all in order for buyer review","Only that lunch is arranged","Only that the factory is repainted"]','Sample availability, production status readiness, and compliance/documentation are all in order for buyer review','A well-prepared buyer visit has approved samples on hand, an accurate view of production status, and organized compliance documentation ready for inspection.',3),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','An "approved vendor list" (AVL) maintained by a buyer/brand includes:','["Every factory in the world with no vetting","Only factories that have passed the buyer''s required audits/qualification process and are authorized to receive orders","Only fabric mills, never garment factories","A list of banned suppliers only"]','Only factories that have passed the buyer''s required audits/qualification process and are authorized to receive orders','Buyers restrict sourcing to a vetted AVL to ensure every factory producing their goods meets minimum quality, compliance, and capability standards.',3),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','A "chase sheet" or order tracker used by merchandisers primarily helps:','["Design new fabric colors","Monitor the real-time status of every order against its TNA milestones across multiple styles/factories simultaneously","Only calculate shipping insurance","Replace the tech pack"]','Monitor the real-time status of every order against its TNA milestones across multiple styles/factories simultaneously','A chase sheet gives merchandisers a consolidated dashboard view of many orders'' progress at once, making it easier to spot and act on emerging delays across a full portfolio.',3),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','Consolidation (combining multiple smaller shipments from different suppliers into one larger shipment) is used to:','["Increase total shipping cost unnecessarily","Reduce per-unit freight cost and simplify customs clearance by shipping combined cargo together","Avoid ever using a freight forwarder","Eliminate the need for a packing list"]','Reduce per-unit freight cost and simplify customs clearance by shipping combined cargo together','Combining multiple smaller supplier shipments into one consolidated shipment can lower overall freight cost per unit and streamline destination handling/customs processing.',3),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','A "short shipment" situation occurs when:','["The factory delivers more units than ordered","The factory ships fewer units than the confirmed order quantity, often due to production shortfall or fabric wastage exceeding tolerance","The order is cancelled before shipping","Goods are shipped a day early"]','The factory ships fewer units than the confirmed order quantity, often due to production shortfall or fabric wastage exceeding tolerance','Short shipment happens when actual production output falls below the ordered quantity, which can trigger claims/penalties depending on the contract terms.',3),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','Overshipment tolerance clauses in a PO typically allow the factory to ship:','["Any quantity with no limit","A small percentage above the ordered quantity (commonly a few percent) to account for normal production yield variation, without it being treated as a contract breach","Exactly the ordered amount only, with zero tolerance ever","Double the ordered quantity as standard practice"]','A small percentage above the ordered quantity (commonly a few percent) to account for normal production yield variation, without it being treated as a contract breach','Since exact-to-the-unit output is often impractical given natural production yield variation, POs commonly define an acceptable over/under-shipment tolerance range.',3),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','A merchandiser negotiating with a fabric mill for better pricing on a large order is primarily leveraging:','["The mill''s inability to say no","Order volume/commitment, which can unlock better per-unit pricing due to the mill''s own economies of scale","Random luck with no strategic basis","Only personal friendship with the mill owner"]','Order volume/commitment, which can unlock better per-unit pricing due to the mill''s own economies of scale','Larger, more committed fabric orders let the mill run larger, more efficient production batches, which is the fundamental basis for volume-driven price negotiation.',3),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','A "cancellation clause" in a buyer-factory contract typically protects the factory by:','["Preventing the buyer from ever cancelling an order","Specifying compensation or cost recovery terms if the buyer cancels an order after production has already started","Guaranteeing unlimited free cancellations for the buyer","Removing all financial risk from the factory entirely"]','Specifying compensation or cost recovery terms if the buyer cancels an order after production has already started','Since factories commit real cost (fabric, labor, capacity) once production starts, cancellation clauses define what compensation is owed if a buyer cancels mid-process.',3),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','Digital/virtual sampling (3D garment simulation) is increasingly used in merchandising to:','["Completely eliminate the need for any physical sample ever","Reduce the number of costly, slow physical sample rounds needed for early-stage design/fit review, speeding up development","Only be used for marketing photography","Replace the tech pack entirely"]','Reduce the number of costly, slow physical sample rounds needed for early-stage design/fit review, speeding up development','3D simulation lets buyers and designers review fit/design digitally before committing to physical samples, cutting development time and sample costs, though physical fit confirmation is usually still needed eventually.',3),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','A merchandiser''s core responsibility in balancing "the merchandising triangle" of cost, quality, and delivery time involves:','["Always maximizing only one factor regardless of the other two","Managing trade-offs between the three, since pushing hard on one (e.g. lowest cost) often puts pressure on the others (e.g. quality or delivery)","Ignoring cost entirely in every decision","Ignoring delivery time entirely in every decision"]','Managing trade-offs between the three, since pushing hard on one (e.g. lowest cost) often puts pressure on the others (e.g. quality or delivery)','Cost, quality, and delivery time are interdependent; merchandisers must actively balance these competing priorities rather than optimizing any single one in isolation.',3),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5','advanced','multiple_choice','An "open costing" negotiation approach, where a factory shares its detailed cost breakdown with the buyer, is intended to:','["Hide the factory''s actual costs completely","Build trust and enable a fact-based price negotiation around real cost drivers rather than an opaque single quoted number","Guarantee the factory the highest possible margin with no scrutiny","Replace the need for a costing sheet"]','Build trust and enable a fact-based price negotiation around real cost drivers rather than an opaque single quoted number','Sharing itemized costs (fabric, trims, labor, overhead, margin) lets both sides negotiate transparently on specific cost elements instead of haggling over one undifferentiated total price.',3);
